// Detailed muscle reports for the Pain Tool's "Pick a muscle" mode: anatomy, symptoms, weighted self-check questions,
// self-tests, causes, look-alikes, red flags and a 3-phase protocol (video names match the exercise library).
// Drafted for Dr. Jared's review; educational, not a diagnosis.
window.MDFIT_MUSCLE_REPORTS = {"suboccipitals":{"origin":"Top two vertebrae in the neck (C1 and C2).","insertion":"Occipital bone at the base of the skull.","action":"Tilts the head back, turns the head side-to-side, and makes fine adjustments to head position. Key for head posture and balance.","nerve":"Suboccipital nerve (C1).","symptoms":["A headache that feels like a tight band starting at the base of your skull.","Pain that wraps forward to behind your eye or into your forehead.","The headache can feel deep, achy, and persistent, sometimes mistaken for a migraine.","Stiffness and reduced ability to nod or tuck your chin.","Pain is often worse when sitting at a desk or looking down at a phone.","May disrupt sleep if pillows don't support the neck properly."],"questions":[{"q":"Does your headache feel like a tight band wrapping from the back of your head to behind your eye?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain worse when you spend a long time looking down at a screen or book?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel stiffness mostly when trying to tuck your chin to your chest?","w":3},{"q":"Does pressing gently into the hollows at the very base of your skull reproduce the headache feeling?","w":3},{"q":"Does your pain feel different from a typical 'stiff neck' on the side, and more like a deep headache?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Chin Tuck Test","how":"Sit or stand tall. Gently tuck your chin towards your throat as if making a double chin, without looking down. Keep the back of your neck long.","positive":"Pain or a strong pulling sensation at the base of your skull, or an inability to perform the movement smoothly."}],"causes":["Forward head posture from prolonged screen time ('tech neck').","Sleeping with too many pillows or on your stomach.","Whiplash injury from a car accident or fall.","Clenching your jaw or grinding your teeth (bruxism).","Eye strain causing you to jut your chin forward.","Stress and emotional tension."],"mimics":["Migraine headache","Tension-type headache","Cervicogenic headache (from upper neck joints)","Occipital neuralgia"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe 'thunderclap' headache.","Headache accompanied by fever, stiff neck (can't touch chin to chest), and confusion.","Headache after a head injury, especially with loss of consciousness or vomiting.","New headache over age 50, or a significant change in your usual headache pattern."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Suboccipital Release","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, once or twice daily","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Place two lacrosse balls (or a peanut) under the base of your skull. Gently nod 'yes' and shake your head 'no' to massage the area."},{"name":"Chin Tucks","video":"Deep Cervical Flexor Nods Seated","dose":"2 x 10 slow reps, several times daily","how":"Gently nod your chin towards your throat as if making a double chin. This is a very small, subtle movement to activate the deep neck muscles."},{"name":"Diaphragmatic Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once or twice daily","how":"Lie on your back and focus on breathing into your belly, not your chest. This helps calm the nervous system and reduce overall muscle tension."}],"restore":[{"name":"Chin Glides","video":"Chin Glide Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps, twice daily","how":"Sit tall and gently glide your head backward over your shoulders, keeping your eyes level. This reverses the forward head posture that strains these muscles."},{"name":"Upper Cervical Mobility","video":null,"dose":"2 x 5 reps each way, once daily","how":"Tuck your chin slightly. Gently turn your head as far as you can to one side, then the other. The movement should feel like it's coming from the very top of your neck."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Isometric Neck Holds","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-second holds in each direction, daily","how":"Sit tall. Place your palm on your forehead and gently press your head forward into your hand without moving. Repeat pressing into your hand on the side and back of your head."},{"name":"Seated Posture Reset","video":"Seated Desk Posture Reset","dose":"Hold for 30 seconds, every hour","how":"Use this video to actively correct your posture throughout the day, strengthening the muscles that support a neutral head position."},{"name":"Scapular Retraction","video":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","dose":"3 x 10 reps, every other day","how":"Strengthening your mid-back muscles provides a stable base for your neck and head, reducing the strain on the suboccipitals."}]},"habits":["Adjust your monitor so the top third is at eye level to prevent looking down.","Take frequent micro-breaks from your desk to stand up and reset your posture.","Use a supportive pillow that keeps your neck in a neutral line with your spine.","Avoid cradling your phone between your ear and shoulder."],"expect":"With consistent exercises and habit changes, most people feel a noticeable reduction in headache frequency and intensity within 2-4 weeks.","seePro":"If your headaches are severe, worsening, or accompanied by dizziness, numbness, or weakness, please book an appointment for a full evaluation."},"occipitalis":{"origin":"Occipital bone and mastoid process.","insertion":"Galea aponeurotica (a tough layer of fibrous tissue covering the top of the skull).","action":"Pulls the scalp backward. Works with the frontalis muscle to raise the eyebrows and express surprise.","nerve":"Facial nerve (CN VII).","symptoms":["Aching pain at the very back of the head.","Pain that feels like it shoots through the skull to behind one eye.","Tenderness of the scalp, making it uncomfortable to brush hair or wear a hat.","A feeling of pressure or a 'skull cap' of pain.","Pain is often triggered or worsened by stress and clenching."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your scalp at the back of your head very tender to the touch?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain sometimes feel like it's shooting from the back of your head straight through to your eye?","w":3},{"q":"Do you notice the pain more when you are stressed or clenching your jaw?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain feel superficial, like it's in the scalp itself, rather than deep in the neck joints?","w":2},{"q":"Does wearing a tight hat, ponytail, or headband make the pain worse?","w":3}],"tests":[{"name":"Scalp Palpation","how":"Use your fingertips to gently but firmly press and move the skin over the bone at the back of your head. Explore the area for any exquisitely tender spots.","positive":"Finding a specific point of tenderness that, when pressed, reproduces your familiar head or eye pain."}],"causes":["Chronic forward head posture, which puts tension on the entire back line of the body.","Emotional stress leading to unconscious scalp muscle tension.","Clenching the jaw or grinding teeth (bruxism).","Prolonged desk work or screen time.","Whiplash or other head/neck trauma."],"mimics":["Occipital neuralgia","Tension-type headache","Suboccipital muscle pain","Giant cell arteritis (rare, but important)"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe 'thunderclap' headache.","Scalp tenderness with fever, weight loss, or vision changes (especially in older adults).","Headache that worsens with coughing or straining.","Pain that started after a significant head injury."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Occipitalis Release","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, once or twice daily","how":"Place a lacrosse ball or your fingertips on the tender spots at the back of your head. Apply gentle, sustained pressure or make small circles without sliding on the skin."},{"name":"Chin Glides","video":"Chin Glide Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps, several times daily","how":"Gently glide your head backward over your shoulders, keeping your eyes level. This reduces the tension on the back of the scalp."},{"name":"Box Breathing","video":"Box Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once or twice daily","how":"Inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 4, exhale for 4, hold for 4. This technique helps lower stress and reduce unconscious muscle clenching."}],"restore":[{"name":"Neck Neuroglide","video":"Neck Neuroglide Seated","dose":"1 x 10 slow reps each side, daily","how":"This gentle nerve mobilization helps reduce tension patterns that can affect the scalp and neck."},{"name":"Seated Posture Reset","video":"Seated Desk Posture Reset","dose":"Hold for 30 seconds, every hour","how":"Actively correcting your posture reduces the chronic strain on the muscles at the back of your head and neck."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Deep Neck Flexor Activation","video":"Deep Cervical Flexor Nods Seated","dose":"3 x 10 reps, every other day","how":"Strengthening the muscles at the front of your neck helps to balance the tension at the back, improving head posture."},{"name":"Dumbbell Row","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 10-12 reps, twice a week","how":"Building strength in your upper back creates a strong foundation for your neck and head, offloading the scalp muscles. Use a light weight."}]},"habits":["Be mindful of jaw clenching during the day; let your jaw hang loose with teeth slightly apart.","Avoid tight hairstyles, hats, or headbands that put pressure on the scalp.","Ensure your desk setup promotes a neutral head position.","Practice stress-reduction techniques like meditation or short walks."],"expect":"Scalp tenderness and referred pain often improve within 1-2 weeks with consistent release work and postural correction. Full resolution depends on managing underlying stress and habits.","seePro":"If your headache is severe, doesn't respond to these measures, or is accompanied by vision changes, please book an appointment for a thorough evaluation."},"upper-trap":{"origin":"Base of the skull (occiput), nuchal ligament, and spinous process of C7.","insertion":"Outer third of the clavicle (collarbone) and acromion process of the scapula (shoulder blade).","action":"Shrugs the shoulders up, tilts and turns the head, and helps stabilize the shoulder blade.","nerve":"Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and cervical nerves (C3, C4).","symptoms":["Pain that wraps up the side of the neck to the temple, like a question mark.","A very common source of 'tension headaches'.","A heavy, aching, or burning feeling on top of the shoulder.","Stiffness in the neck, especially when tilting your head to the side.","Pain can be constant and is often worse at the end of a stressful workday."],"questions":[{"q":"Does your pain start on top of your shoulder and travel up the side of your neck to your temple?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel a constant, heavy weight or ache on your shoulders, especially when stressed?","w":2},{"q":"Does carrying a heavy bag or purse on your shoulder make the pain significantly worse?","w":3},{"q":"Does tilting your head away from the painful side and gently pulling with your hand reproduce the pain?","w":2},{"q":"Do you often find yourself shrugging your shoulders up toward your ears when you're concentrating or cold?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Pincer Grip Palpation","how":"Use your opposite hand to grasp the thick muscle on top of your shoulder, between your neck and shoulder point. Squeeze gently and search for a tight, tender band of muscle.","positive":"Finding a very tender spot that, when squeezed, refers pain up the side of your neck or into your temple."},{"name":"Active Side Bend","how":"Sit tall and slowly tilt your ear toward your shoulder without shrugging. Compare the range of motion and sensation on both sides.","positive":"Significant tightness, pulling, or pain on the stretched side, or a noticeable difference in how far you can go compared to the other side."}],"causes":["Chronic stress, which leads to unconscious shoulder shrugging.","Poor posture, especially slouching with a forward head.","Carrying heavy bags, backpacks, or children on one side.","Repetitive arm movements with poor shoulder stability (e.g., typing with a high keyboard).","Chilling of the muscle, like sitting in a draft.","Trauma such as whiplash."],"mimics":["Cervical disc issues (radiculopathy)","Neck joint (facet) arthritis","Thoracic Outlet Syndrome","Levator scapulae pain (more at the angle of the neck)"],"redFlags":["Pain accompanied by numbness, tingling, or weakness that radiates down the entire arm.","Sudden, severe headache or neck pain after an injury.","Unexplained swelling or a lump in the neck/shoulder area.","Pain associated with fever, chills, or unexplained weight loss."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Upper Trap Release","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, once or twice daily","how":"Place a lacrosse ball between your upper trap muscle and a wall. Lean into the ball and gently roll up and down and side-to-side to find and release tender spots."},{"name":"Upper Trap Stretch","video":"Upper Trap Stretch Seated","dose":"2 x 30 seconds each side, twice daily","how":"Sit tall and gently tilt your ear toward your opposite shoulder. Use your hand for a light overpressure, but do not force it."},{"name":"Shoulder Shrugs & Drops","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15 reps, several times daily","how":"Inhale and shrug your shoulders up to your ears, then exhale with a sigh and let them drop completely. This helps reset muscle tension."}],"restore":[{"name":"Eccentric Neck Stretch","video":"Towel Neck Stretch Eccentric","dose":"2 x 5 slow reps each side, daily","how":"Use a towel to gently resist as you slowly return your head from a side-bent position to neutral. This builds control and resilience in the muscle."},{"name":"Scapular CARs","video":"Scapular CARs","dose":"1 x 5 circles each direction, daily","how":"Controlled Articular Rotations help improve the movement and awareness of your shoulder blades, which can reduce the workload on the upper traps."},{"name":"Seated Posture Reset","video":"Seated Desk Posture Reset","dose":"Hold for 30 seconds, every hour","how":"Regularly resetting your posture helps train your body to stop over-relying on the upper traps for support."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Farmer Carry","video":"Farmer Carry","dose":"3 sets of 30-second walks, twice a week","how":"Carry a moderate weight in each hand. Keep your chest up and shoulders pulled down and back. This teaches the traps to stabilize the shoulder girdle correctly."},{"name":"Face Pull","video":"Face Pull","dose":"3 x 12-15 reps, twice a week","how":"This exercise strengthens the mid-back and external rotators, which helps to counterbalance the pull of tight upper traps and improve posture."},{"name":"Wall Slides","video":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","dose":"3 x 10 reps, every other day","how":"This can also be done standing against a wall. It activates the lower trapezius and serratus anterior, muscles that help depress the shoulder blades and offload the upper traps."}]},"habits":["Adjust your chair and keyboard so your elbows are at a 90-degree angle and your shoulders are relaxed.","Use a backpack with two straps instead of a single-strap shoulder bag.","Be mindful of cradling your phone. Use a headset or speakerphone instead.","During stressful moments, take a deep breath and consciously drop your shoulders away from your ears."],"expect":"You can expect significant relief from pain and headaches within 1-2 weeks of consistent stretching and release work. Lasting relief comes from building strength and changing habits over 4-6 weeks.","seePro":"If you have persistent numbness or tingling in your arm, or if the pain doesn't improve with conservative care, book an appointment for a more detailed assessment."},"scm":{"origin":"Top of the sternum (breastbone) and inner part of the clavicle (collarbone).","insertion":"Mastoid process of the temporal bone (the bony bump behind the ear).","action":"When one side acts, it turns the head to the opposite side and bends it to the same side. Acting together, they bend the neck forward.","nerve":"Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and cervical nerves (C2, C3).","symptoms":["Pain in the forehead, often described as a 'headache over the eye'.","Pain around the eye, in the cheek, or in the jaw (TMJ) area.","Dizziness, vertigo, or a feeling of imbalance, especially with head turning.","Ear symptoms like ringing (tinnitus), fullness, or deep ear pain.","Pain at the top of the head or a sore throat feeling when swallowing."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you experience pain or pressure in your forehead, above or around your eye?","w":3},{"q":"Have you felt dizzy, off-balance, or had ringing in your ears that seems related to your neck pain?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain get worse when you turn your head to look over your shoulder, like when backing up a car?","w":2},{"q":"Do you have pain in your cheek or jaw that has been mistaken for a sinus or dental problem?","w":3},{"q":"Do you often sleep on your stomach or with your head turned sharply to one side?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"SCM Palpation","how":"Turn your head to one side and look slightly up; the SCM muscle will pop out. Gently pinch the muscle between your thumb and fingers and feel for tender, ropey spots from behind your ear down to your collarbone.","positive":"Finding a very tender spot that reproduces your specific headache, face pain, or dizziness."}],"causes":["Whiplash injuries, even minor ones.","Forward head posture, which over-stretches and strains the SCM.","Sleeping on your stomach or with thick pillows.","Chronic coughing, or activities involving deep, forceful breathing (e.g., playing a wind instrument).","Turning your head to one side for long periods (e.g., watching TV, dual monitor setup)."],"mimics":["Migraine with aura","Trigeminal neuralgia","Sinusitis or sinus headache","TMJ (jaw joint) dysfunction","Inner ear problems (e.g., BPPV, vestibular neuritis)"],"redFlags":["Sudden onset of severe dizziness or vertigo, especially with difficulty speaking, walking, or seeing.","Pain or swelling of the lymph nodes along the muscle that doesn't resolve.","A pulsating sensation in the neck (do not press on this).","Headache accompanied by fever and a stiff neck that prevents you from touching your chin to your chest."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"SCM Release","video":"SCM Release Seated","dose":"1-2 minutes per side, daily","how":"Gently pinch the muscle between your thumb and fingers as shown in the video. Hold tender spots with gentle pressure until they soften. Be careful not to press on the pulse in your neck (carotid artery)."},{"name":"Gentle SCM Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 20 seconds each side, daily","how":"Sit tall. Gently tilt your head back, then turn away from the side you want to stretch and tilt your ear to the opposite shoulder. You should feel a gentle pull along the front/side of your neck."},{"name":"Chin Glides","video":"Chin Glide Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps, several times daily","how":"This movement helps to reposition your head over your shoulders, taking the chronic strain off the SCM muscles."}],"restore":[{"name":"Neck Neuroglide","video":"Neck Neuroglide Seated","dose":"1 x 10 slow reps each side, daily","how":"This nerve mobilization can help calm down the nerve pathways that contribute to SCM tension and its unusual symptoms like dizziness."},{"name":"Deep Neck Flexor Activation","video":"Deep Cervical Flexor Nods Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Strengthening the small muscles at the front of your neck provides stability and helps the SCM to relax and do its job of moving, not just holding."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Isometric Neck Rotation","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-second holds each side, every other day","how":"Place your hand on the side of your head. Gently try to turn your head into your hand, but don't let it move. This strengthens the SCM in a controlled way."},{"name":"Core Brace","video":"Core Brace Intro Seated","dose":"5 x 10-second holds, several times daily","how":"A stable core provides a foundation for your head and neck. Learning to brace your core reduces strain on neck muscles during daily activities."},{"name":"Dumbbell Row","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 10-12 reps, twice a week","how":"A strong upper back helps maintain good posture, which is the key to unloading the SCM long-term. Use a light weight and focus on form."}]},"habits":["Avoid sleeping on your stomach. Try sleeping on your back or side with a supportive pillow.","Position screens directly in front of you to avoid prolonged head turning.","When reading a book or on your phone, prop it up to eye level.","Practice diaphragmatic (belly) breathing to avoid using your neck muscles for respiration."],"expect":"Symptoms like dizziness and ear fullness can improve quickly, within a week of starting release work. The headache and facial pain patterns may take 2-4 weeks to resolve as you improve your posture and strength.","seePro":"If you experience severe, persistent dizziness, hearing loss, or any of the red flag symptoms, please book an appointment immediately for a comprehensive neurological and vestibular screen."},"levator":{"origin":"Transverse processes of the top four cervical vertebrae (C1-C4).","insertion":"Superior angle and medial border of the scapula (top inner corner of the shoulder blade).","action":"Lifts the shoulder blade (scapula) and bends and turns the neck to the same side.","nerve":"Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and cervical nerves (C3, C4).","symptoms":["The classic 'stiff neck' pain, making it hard to turn your head to look over your shoulder.","A deep, nagging ache at the angle where your neck meets your shoulder.","Pain that refers down the inside edge of your shoulder blade.","A feeling that you constantly need to stretch your neck, but it provides little relief.","Waking up with a stiff neck after sleeping in an awkward position."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel a sharp or aching pain right at the corner where your neck and shoulder meet?","w":3},{"q":"Is your main problem an inability to turn your head to one side, as if your neck is 'locked'?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain also travel down along the inner border of your shoulder blade?","w":2},{"q":"Do you often carry a bag on that shoulder or cradle a phone there?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain feel worse in the morning after sleeping?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Head Turn Test","how":"Sit tall and try to turn your head to look over one shoulder, then the other. Keep your shoulders still.","positive":"Significant pain and/or a major restriction in movement on one side compared to the other. You may feel a sharp pull at the angle of your neck."},{"name":"Palpation at Insertion","how":"Reach over your opposite shoulder with your fingers and find the top, inner corner of your shoulder blade. Press firmly into the muscle at that corner.","positive":"Finding an exquisitely tender spot that reproduces your neck and shoulder blade pain."}],"causes":["Sleeping with your head in an awkward position, especially on your stomach or with a poor pillow.","Holding a phone between your ear and shoulder.","Carrying a heavy backpack or purse on one shoulder.","Sitting in a draft or under an A/C vent, which can chill the muscle.","Forward head posture and rounded shoulders from desk work.","Emotional stress, leading to chronic shoulder shrugging."],"mimics":["Cervical disc issues (radiculopathy)","Neck joint (facet) syndrome","Upper trapezius pain (often co-exists, but referral is different)","Rhomboid muscle strain"],"redFlags":["Neck stiffness accompanied by high fever, headache, and sensitivity to light.","Pain, numbness, or weakness that radiates all the way down your arm to your hand.","A recent fall or trauma to the head or neck.","Difficulty swallowing or a change in your voice associated with the neck pain."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Levator Scapulae Stretch","video":"Levator Scap Stretch Seated","dose":"2 x 30 seconds each side, twice daily","how":"Follow the video cues precisely: turn your head away from the painful side and look down towards your opposite armpit. Use gentle hand pressure."},{"name":"Levator Release","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, daily","how":"Place a lacrosse ball between the top/inner edge of your shoulder blade and a wall. Lean in and roll gently to find and hold pressure on tender spots."},{"name":"Shoulder Blade Squeezes","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15 reps, several times daily","how":"Sit or stand tall. Gently squeeze your shoulder blades together and down, as if tucking them into your back pockets. Hold for 2 seconds and release."}],"restore":[{"name":"Scapular CARs","video":"Scapular CARs","dose":"1 x 5 large, slow circles each direction, daily","how":"This exercise improves the mobility and control of your shoulder blade, which helps the levator scapulae to relax and function properly."},{"name":"Eccentric Neck Stretch","video":"Towel Neck Stretch Eccentric","dose":"2 x 5 slow reps in the levator stretch position, daily","how":"After stretching, use a towel to gently resist as you bring your head back to neutral. This builds strength and control in the muscle's lengthened range."},{"name":"Thoracic Rotation","video":"Quadruped T-Spine Rotation","dose":"2 x 10 reps each side, daily","how":"Improving mobility in your mid-back reduces the rotational stress on your neck, giving the levator a break."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Face Pull","video":"Face Pull","dose":"3 x 12 reps, twice a week","how":"Pull a light band toward your face with elbows high, squeezing the shoulder blades back and down. Keep the neck long and relaxed."},{"name":"Farmer Carry","video":"Farmer Carry","dose":"3 sets of 30-second walks, twice a week","how":"Walking with weights teaches you to keep your shoulder blades 'down and back', strengthening the muscles that oppose the levator's shrugging action."},{"name":"Push Up","video":"Push Up","dose":"3 sets to near failure, twice a week","how":"Proper push-ups (even on knees or a wall) strengthen the serratus anterior, a key muscle for scapular stability that helps offload the levator."}]},"habits":["Use a headset or speakerphone to avoid cradling the phone.","Evaluate your pillow: it should fill the space between your head and the mattress, keeping your neck neutral.","When sitting, ensure your armrests are at a height that allows your shoulders to relax down.","Alternate which shoulder you use to carry a bag, or switch to a backpack."],"expect":"Acute 'stiff neck' pain and range of motion limitations often improve significantly within 3-7 days with consistent stretching and release. Chronic ache may take 3-6 weeks to fully resolve as you build strength and change habits.","seePro":"If you can't turn your head at all, or if the pain is severe and not improving after a week, please book an appointment. Also, seek care for any radiating arm symptoms."},"scalenes":{"origin":"Sides of your neck vertebrae (C2-C7).","insertion":"The top surfaces of your first and second ribs.","action":"Lifts the top two ribs to help you take a deep breath (accessory breathing). Also bends your neck to the side and helps with turning.","nerve":"Cervical spinal nerves (C3-C8).","symptoms":["Aching pain in the chest, sometimes mistaken for a heart issue.","Pain, numbness, or tingling that travels from the neck down the arm to the thumb and index finger.","A feeling of a 'dead arm' or waking up with a numb hand.","Aching pain between the shoulder blades.","Pain is often worse when taking a deep breath, coughing, or carrying heavy items.","Difficulty finding a comfortable sleeping position for your head and arm."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel pain or tingling that runs from your neck down your arm to your thumb and index finger?","w":3},{"q":"Does your pain get worse when you carry something heavy, like a bag of groceries?","w":3},{"q":"Do you ever feel a strange, non-cardiac pain in your chest, especially after a lot of coughing?","w":2},{"q":"Do you often wake up with a 'dead arm' or tingling in your hand?","w":2},{"q":"Is your pain aggravated by looking at a phone or computer screen for long periods?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Scalene Relief Test","how":"While sitting, gently lift the arm on the painful side and rest your forearm and hand on top of your head. Hold for 30 seconds.","positive":"A significant reduction or complete relief of your arm and hand symptoms while your arm is raised."}],"causes":["Shallow, chest-breathing patterns, especially during stress or intense exercise.","Forward head posture from prolonged desk work or phone use ('tech neck').","Whiplash injury from a car accident or fall.","Chronic coughing from asthma, bronchitis, or allergies.","Carrying heavy backpacks or purses with a strap over one shoulder.","Sleeping with your arm overhead or under your head."],"mimics":["Cervical disc herniation (pinched nerve in the neck).","Thoracic Outlet Syndrome (TOS).","Rotator cuff injury.","Angina or cardiac pain (always rule this out first with a physician).","Carpal Tunnel Syndrome."],"redFlags":["Chest pain accompanied by shortness of breath, sweating, or nausea (call 911).","Sudden, severe weakness or loss of coordination in your arm or hand.","Pain that is constant, unrelenting, and not changed by position.","A cold, pale, or blue hand."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Diaphragmatic Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, twice daily","how":"Lie on your back with feet up on a chair. Focus on letting your belly rise as you inhale and fall as you exhale, keeping your chest and neck relaxed."},{"name":"Scalene Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"60-90 seconds per side, daily","how":"Using the fingers of your opposite hand, gently press into the soft, ropy muscles in the groove on the side of your neck, above your collarbone. Hold gentle pressure on tender spots."},{"name":"Gentle Scalene Stretch","video":"Standing Scalene Stretch","dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, daily","how":"Gently tilt your ear away from the shoulder you're stretching. To deepen, you can gently hold onto a chair or place your other hand over your head."}],"restore":[{"name":"Eccentric Scalene Control","video":"Eccentric Scalene Drop","dose":"2 x 8 reps per side, every other day","how":"Use your hand to help lift your head from a side-tilted position, then slowly lower it back down over 3-5 seconds, controlling the movement."},{"name":"Neck & Arm Nerve Glide","video":"Neck Neuroglide Seated","dose":"1 x 10 reps per side, daily","how":"Gently move through the prescribed range of motion to mobilize the nerves that pass through the neck and arm. Move slowly and without pain."},{"name":"Postural Reset","video":"Seated Desk Posture Reset","dose":"Every 30 minutes at your desk","how":"Follow the video cues to reset your head, neck, and shoulder position to a more neutral alignment."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Deep Neck Flexor Activation","video":"Deep Cervical Flexor Nods Seated","dose":"2 x 10 sec holds, 5 reps, daily","how":"Gently tuck your chin to create a 'double chin' and feel the deep muscles at the front of your neck engage. Don't use your large, outer neck muscles."},{"name":"Core Bracing","video":"Core Brace Intro Seated","dose":"3 x 30 sec holds, daily","how":"Engage your abdominal muscles as if you're about to be punched in the stomach. Breathe normally while holding this tension."},{"name":"Loaded Carries","video":"Farmer Carry","dose":"3 x 40 yards, twice weekly","how":"Hold a moderate weight in each hand, stand tall with good posture, and walk. This builds postural endurance for the neck and shoulders."}]},"habits":["Set a timer to take a 'Walk Break' every 30-60 minutes to break up prolonged sitting.","Adjust your monitor height so the top of the screen is at or slightly below eye level.","Practice belly breathing throughout the day, especially when you feel stressed.","Avoid sleeping on your stomach or with your arm tucked under your head."],"expect":"With consistent application of these steps, most people feel a noticeable improvement in 2-4 weeks. Full resolution may take 6-8 weeks.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if your symptoms include significant weakness, numbness that doesn't go away, or if the pain is not improving after 2 weeks of consistent self-care."},"splenius":{"origin":"The spine at the base of the neck and upper back (C7-T6).","insertion":"The base of the skull (capitis) and the sides of the upper neck vertebrae (cervicis).","action":"Working together, they tilt your head backward (extension). Working alone, one side turns and tilts your head to that same side.","nerve":"Dorsal rami of the middle and lower cervical nerves.","symptoms":["A headache that feels like it's on the very top of your head.","A strange, deep ache that feels like it's inside your skull, often behind one eye.","Neck stiffness, especially when trying to look down or turn your head.","Pain can sometimes feel like a 'cap' on your head.","Occasional blurry vision in the eye on the same side as the neck pain."],"questions":[{"q":"Does your headache feel like it's on the very top of your skull?","w":3},{"q":"Do you have a persistent, deep ache that feels like it's behind your eye?","w":3},{"q":"Is your neck stiff and painful when you try to tuck your chin to your chest?","w":2},{"q":"Does looking up for a long time (like painting a ceiling) trigger your neck pain or headache?","w":2},{"q":"Do you spend many hours a day with your head tilted forward looking at a screen?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Splenius Contraction Test","how":"Sit tall. Turn your head about 45 degrees to one side, then gently tilt your head backward to look up at the ceiling.","positive":"This movement reproduces the specific headache pain on the top of your head or behind your eye."}],"causes":["Forward head posture from computer or phone use.","Whiplash injury, which overstretches these muscles.","Sleeping with too many pillows, forcing the neck into flexion.","Activities that require looking up for long periods (e.g., rock climbing, painting).","Emotional stress, leading to chronic neck tension."],"mimics":["Cervicogenic headache (headache originating from the neck joints).","Migraine or tension-type headache.","Occipital neuralgia.","Sinus headache (pain behind the eye)."],"redFlags":["A 'thunderclap' headache that comes on suddenly and is the 'worst headache of your life' (call 911).","Headache accompanied by fever, stiff neck, confusion, or rash.","Headache that starts after a head injury.","New or worsening headache pattern, especially if over age 50."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Gentle Chin Tucks","video":"Chin Glide Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps, several times daily","how":"Gently glide your chin straight back as if making a 'double chin'. Avoid tilting your head down; the motion is a straight glide."},{"name":"Splenius Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"60-90 seconds per side, daily","how":"Place a lacrosse ball on the muscle at the back/side of your neck, just below the skull. Lean against a wall and apply gentle, sustained pressure to tender spots."},{"name":"Gentle Neck Flexion Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec, daily","how":"Sit tall and gently bring your chin toward your chest. Use your hand to apply very light overpressure on the back of your head. You should feel a stretch, not pain."}],"restore":[{"name":"Eccentric Neck Extension","video":"Towel Neck Stretch Eccentric","dose":"2 x 8 reps, every other day","how":"Use a towel to gently pull your head forward into flexion, then use your neck muscles to slowly resist the towel as you return to the start position over 3-5 seconds."},{"name":"Neck & Arm Nerve Glide","video":"Neck Neuroglide Seated","dose":"1 x 10 reps per side, daily","how":"This helps ensure the nerves in the area are moving freely. Follow the video cues and move gently without forcing into pain."},{"name":"Thoracic Spine Mobility","video":"Quadruped T-Spine Rotation","dose":"2 x 10 reps per side, daily","how":"Improving mobility in your upper back reduces the strain on your neck. Focus on rotating through your rib cage."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Deep Neck Flexor Endurance","video":"Deep Cervical Flexor Nods Seated","dose":"3 x 30 sec holds, daily","how":"Perform a gentle chin tuck and hold it, maintaining the contraction in the deep front-neck muscles. Breathe normally."},{"name":"Upper Back Strengthening","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 10-12 reps, twice weekly","how":"Hinge at your hips with a flat back. Pull the weights up towards your chest, squeezing your shoulder blades together at the top."},{"name":"Scapular Retraction","video":"Face Pull","dose":"3 x 15 reps, twice weekly","how":"Using a band or cable, pull the rope towards your face, aiming your hands towards your ears. Squeeze your shoulder blades together and back."}]},"habits":["Use a single, supportive pillow that keeps your neck in a neutral line with your spine.","Position your computer monitor so your eyes are level with the top third of the screen.","When reading on a phone, try to bring the phone up to your face rather than dropping your head down to the phone.","Take frequent micro-breaks from desk work to perform chin glides and look around."],"expect":"Headaches often decrease in frequency and intensity within the first 1-2 weeks. Neck stiffness may take 3-6 weeks to fully resolve with consistent exercises.","seePro":"See a professional if your headache is severe, doesn't respond to self-care, or is accompanied by any of the red flag symptoms."},"temporalis":{"origin":"The temporal fossa, a large fan-shaped area on the side of your skull.","insertion":"A bony process on your lower jaw (coronoid process of the mandible).","action":"Closes your jaw (elevation) and pulls it backward (retraction). It's essential for chewing and clenching.","nerve":"Trigeminal Nerve (Cranial Nerve V, Mandibular Division).","symptoms":["A headache felt in the temple and side of the head.","Pain that feels like it's above your eye or behind your eyebrow.","Sensitivity or aching in your upper teeth, which can be mistaken for a dental problem.","Pain when chewing hard or chewy foods.","Difficulty opening your mouth wide.","Pain is often worse during times of stress or concentration."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you get headaches primarily in your temples?","w":3},{"q":"Do your upper teeth ache, even though your dentist says they are healthy?","w":3},{"q":"Is your headache worse after chewing gum, eating a tough steak, or clenching your jaw?","w":2},{"q":"Do you wake up with a headache or a sore jaw?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain feel like a constant, dull ache above your eyebrow?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Temporalis Palpation Test","how":"Place your fingertips on your temples. Clench your teeth firmly and feel the muscle bulge. Now relax and press firmly into the muscle, searching for specific, tender spots.","positive":"Pressing on a tender spot reproduces your temple headache or tooth sensitivity."}],"causes":["Bruxism (clenching or grinding teeth), especially during sleep or concentration.","Excessive gum chewing or eating very hard/chewy foods.","Dental issues, such as a misaligned bite or recent dental work.","Emotional stress and anxiety.","Forward head posture, which can alter jaw mechanics."],"mimics":["Tension-type headache or migraine.","Dental problems (cavities, abscesses).","Trigeminal neuralgia.","Temporal arteritis (a serious condition in older adults)."],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe, one-sided headache with vision changes, fever, or jaw pain when chewing (could be temporal arteritis).","Headache following a head injury.","Inability to close your jaw after it has been opened wide (jaw dislocation).","Numbness or weakness in the face."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Temporalis Self-Massage","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, as needed","how":"With your jaw relaxed, use your fingertips to apply firm, slow, circular pressure to the tender areas in your temples. You can also try a 'pincer' grip on the muscle."},{"name":"Stress Reduction Breathing","video":"Box Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, 1-2 times daily or when stressed","how":"Inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 4, exhale for 4, hold for 4. This helps calm the nervous system and reduce clenching."},{"name":"Gentle Jaw Mobility","video":null,"dose":"10 reps, several times daily","how":"With your tongue on the roof of your mouth, slowly open and close your jaw only to the point of mild tension, not pain. This encourages relaxed movement."}],"restore":[{"name":"Postural Correction","video":"Chin Glide Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps, several times daily","how":"Correcting forward head posture reduces strain on the jaw muscles. Gently glide your chin straight back without tilting your head."},{"name":"Tongue Posture Training","video":null,"dose":"Throughout the day","how":"Practice resting your tongue gently on the roof of your mouth, just behind your front teeth, with your teeth slightly apart and lips together. This is the jaw's natural resting position."},{"name":"Controlled Jaw Opening","video":null,"dose":"2 x 8 reps, daily","how":"Place a finger on your chin. As you slowly open your mouth, provide gentle resistance with your finger. Then, as you close, gently resist the closing motion."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Deep Neck Flexor Activation","video":"Deep Cervical Flexor Nods Seated","dose":"2 x 10 sec holds, 5 reps, daily","how":"A stable neck provides a better foundation for the jaw. Gently tuck your chin and hold, feeling the deep muscles in the front of your neck work."},{"name":"Postural Endurance","video":"Seated Desk Posture Reset","dose":"Every 30 minutes at your desk","how":"Regularly resetting your posture prevents the slow creep into a forward-head position that strains the jaw."},{"name":"Core Stability","video":"Dead Bug","dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"A strong core helps stabilize the entire spine, reducing compensatory tension in the neck and jaw."}]},"habits":["Avoid chewing gum, ice, or very hard foods.","Cut food into smaller pieces to reduce the effort of chewing.","Place small sticky notes on your computer or mirror that say 'Relax Jaw' as a reminder.","If you clench at night, speak to your dentist about a night guard."],"expect":"You can often feel relief from acute pain within a few days of starting massage and relaxation. Lasting improvement, especially if related to clenching, may take 4-6 weeks of consistent habit changes and exercises.","seePro":"Book an appointment if your pain is severe, you have clicking or locking in your jaw, or if self-care doesn't provide relief after 2-3 weeks. A dentist or TMJ specialist may be needed."},"masseter":{"origin":"The zygomatic arch (your cheekbone).","insertion":"The outer surface of the angle and ramus of your mandible (your jawbone).","action":"The primary muscle for closing your jaw. It is the strongest muscle in the body by weight, used for chewing and clenching.","nerve":"Trigeminal Nerve (Cranial Nerve V, Mandibular Division).","symptoms":["Deep ache or pain directly over the jaw joint, in front of the ear.","Pain in the cheek that can radiate up to the temple or down into the jaw.","A feeling of fullness or pain inside the ear, like an earache.","Aching in the lower back teeth (molars), often leading to unnecessary dental visits.","Limited ability to open your mouth, or pain when yawning.","A 'clicking' or 'popping' sound in the jaw joint (TMJ)."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you have pain right in front of your ear, especially when you chew?","w":3},{"q":"Does it feel like you have an earache or toothache in your back molars, but the doctor/dentist can't find a cause?","w":3},{"q":"Do you wake up with a sore, tired jaw?","w":2},{"q":"Is your jaw pain accompanied by a clicking or popping sound when you open your mouth?","w":2},{"q":"Do you find yourself clenching your teeth during the day when you are stressed or concentrating?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Masseter Palpation Test","how":"Place your fingers on your cheek, just above the angle of your jaw. Clench your teeth to feel the muscle contract, then relax. Press deeply into the muscle belly to find tender, ropy bands.","positive":"Pressing on a tender spot reproduces your jaw, cheek, ear, or tooth pain."}],"causes":["Bruxism (clenching or grinding teeth), often related to stress.","Habitual gum chewing, nail biting, or chewing on pens.","Malocclusion (a bad bite) or recent extensive dental work.","Direct trauma to the jaw.","Forward head posture, which changes the resting position of the jaw."],"mimics":["Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ) Dysfunction.","Dental problems (cavities, abscess, cracked tooth).","Ear infection (otitis media).","Parotid gland inflammation (salivary gland).","Trigeminal neuralgia."],"redFlags":["Inability to open or close your jaw (a 'locked' jaw).","Sudden, severe swelling in the jaw or cheek area.","Pain accompanied by fever, which could indicate an infection.","Numbness in your jaw, chin, or lip."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Masseter Self-Massage","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, as needed","how":"With your jaw relaxed, use your knuckles or fingertips to apply deep, slow stripping strokes from your cheekbone down to your jawline. You can also do this from inside your mouth (with a clean finger or glove) to release the deeper part of the muscle."},{"name":"Stress Reduction Breathing","video":"Box Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, 1-2 times daily or when stressed","how":"Calming your nervous system is key to reducing clenching. Inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 4, exhale for 4, and hold for 4."},{"name":"Gentle Jaw Mobility","video":null,"dose":"10 reps, several times daily","how":"Place your tongue on the roof of your mouth. Slowly open and close your jaw without letting your tongue lose contact. This promotes a healthy hinge motion."}],"restore":[{"name":"Postural Correction","video":"Chin Glide Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps, several times daily","how":"A neutral head position is crucial for a relaxed jaw. Gently glide your chin straight back to stack your head over your shoulders."},{"name":"Tongue Posture Training","video":null,"dose":"Throughout the day","how":"Consciously rest your tongue on the roof of your mouth, with teeth slightly apart and lips closed. This is the 'freeway space' that allows the masseter to relax."},{"name":"Jaw Lateral Movement","video":null,"dose":"2 x 10 reps per side, daily","how":"Look in a mirror. Slowly glide your lower jaw from side to side without pain. Keep the movement smooth and controlled."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Deep Neck Flexor Activation","video":"Deep Cervical Flexor Nods Seated","dose":"2 x 10 sec holds, 5 reps, daily","how":"Strengthening the supporting neck muscles helps unload the jaw. Perform a gentle chin tuck and hold."},{"name":"Postural Endurance","video":"Seated Desk Posture Reset","dose":"Every 30 minutes at your desk","how":"Use this sequence to combat the forward head posture that contributes to jaw tension."},{"name":"Core Stability","video":"Dead Bug","dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"A stable trunk provides a solid base for your head and neck, reducing the need for compensatory tension in the jaw."}]},"habits":["Adopt the 'lips together, teeth apart' mantra throughout the day.","Avoid chewing gum and tough foods like bagels or steak while the jaw is painful.","Try not to rest your chin in your hand.","If you grind your teeth at night, consult your dentist about a custom night guard."],"expect":"Pain can decrease significantly within 1-2 weeks with massage and habit changes. Full recovery and preventing recurrence depends on managing stress and postural habits, which can take 4-8 weeks to solidify.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if you have significant clicking, locking, or deviation of your jaw, or if pain persists for more than 3 weeks despite your best efforts. A TMJ specialist or physical therapist can provide more specific care."},"supraspinatus":{"origin":"The top surface of your shoulder blade (supraspinous fossa).","insertion":"The top of your upper arm bone (greater tubercle of the humerus).","action":"Initiates lifting your arm out to the side (abduction) for the first 15-30 degrees. It also holds the 'ball' of your arm bone securely in the shoulder 'socket'.","nerve":"Suprascapular nerve (C5, C6).","symptoms":["A deep, nagging ache felt on the top or outer part of your shoulder.","Pain that refers down the side of your arm, often stopping around the mid-arm or elbow.","A sharp pinch or pain when you try to lift your arm overhead, like reaching into a high cabinet.","Weakness when trying to lift your arm out to the side.","Pain at night, especially when you lie on the affected shoulder.","Difficulty with tasks like brushing your hair or putting on a coat."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel a sharp pain in your shoulder when you start to lift your arm out to the side, especially between 60 and 120 degrees (a 'painful arc')?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain a deep ache on the outside of your shoulder that sometimes travels down your arm?","w":3},{"q":"Does it hurt to lie on that shoulder at night?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel weakness when trying to hold a heavy object out to your side?","w":2},{"q":"Is it difficult to reach behind your back, for example, to tuck in a shirt?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Empty Can Test","how":"Lift your affected arm forward and out to the side about 45 degrees, keeping your elbow straight. Turn your thumb down as if emptying a can. Gently try to resist as you use your other hand to push down on your arm.","positive":"Significant pain or weakness in the shoulder compared to the other side."},{"name":"Drop Arm Test","how":"Use your good arm to help lift your painful arm straight out to the side, parallel to the floor. Now, try to slowly and smoothly lower it back down to your side.","positive":"You are unable to lower the arm slowly, or it suddenly drops. This can indicate a more significant tear and warrants a professional evaluation."}],"causes":["Repetitive overhead activities (e.g., painting, swimming, throwing).","Lifting an object that is too heavy or with poor technique.","A fall onto an outstretched arm or directly onto the shoulder.","Poor posture (rounded shoulders) which narrows the space for the tendon.","Age-related degeneration or 'wear and tear' of the tendon."],"mimics":["Subacromial bursitis (often occurs alongside supraspinatus issues).","Biceps tendonitis.","Cervical radiculopathy (a pinched nerve in the neck referring pain to the shoulder).","Shoulder joint arthritis.","Acromioclavicular (AC) joint sprain."],"redFlags":["Inability to lift your arm at all after an injury.","A visible deformity or 'lump' on your shoulder or collarbone after a fall.","Pain accompanied by fever, redness, or swelling, suggesting an infection.","Pain that is constant and severe, even at rest."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Pendulum Swings","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec in each direction, several times daily","how":"Lean over, supporting your good arm on a table. Let the painful arm hang down and use your body to create small, gentle circles. This provides gentle traction and movement without using the muscle."},{"name":"Supraspinatus Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"60-90 seconds, daily","how":"Place a lacrosse ball between your shoulder blade and a wall. Find the muscle just above the 'spine' of your shoulder blade and apply sustained, gentle pressure to any tender spots."},{"name":"Scapular Wall Slides","video":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Perform this standing against a wall. Keep your forearms on the wall and slide them up and down, focusing on moving your shoulder blades without shrugging. Only go as high as you can without pain."}],"restore":[{"name":"Isometric External Rotation","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 sec holds, daily","how":"Stand in a doorway with your elbow bent to 90 degrees. Gently press the back of your hand into the door frame as if rotating outward. You should feel the muscle contract without any joint movement."},{"name":"Banded External Rotation","video":"Rotator Cuff External Rotation","dose":"2 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Keep your elbow pinned to your side at a 90-degree angle. Rotate your forearm outward against light band resistance. The movement should come from your shoulder, not your wrist."},{"name":"Scapular Mobility","video":"Scapular CARs","dose":"1 x 5 circles each way, daily","how":"Move your shoulder blade through its full range of motion—up, back, down, and forward—while keeping your arm relatively still. This improves shoulder blade control."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Scaption Raises","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-12 reps, twice weekly","how":"With a very light weight (or no weight), raise your arm with your thumb up at a 30-45 degree angle in front of your body. Only go to shoulder height. This is the natural plane of the shoulder blade."},{"name":"Rotator Cuff Endurance","video":"Rotator Cuff 90 Band Vibration","dose":"3 x 20 sec holds, twice weekly","how":"Hold your arm in the 'stop sign' position with a band providing tension. Perform small, quick vibrations to challenge the stability of the rotator cuff."},{"name":"Push-Up Plus","video":"Scap Push Ups","dose":"3 x 10 reps, twice weekly","how":"In a push-up or quadruped position, keep your elbows straight and just move your shoulder blades. Push the floor away to spread them apart, then let them sink together. This strengthens the serratus anterior, a key shoulder stabilizer."}]},"habits":["Avoid sleeping directly on the painful shoulder. Try sleeping on your back or other side with a pillow to hug.","When lifting, keep your elbows close to your body to reduce strain on the shoulder.","Break up overhead tasks with frequent rest periods.","Strengthen your upper back muscles (rows, face pulls) to improve posture and shoulder mechanics."],"expect":"Minor strains can feel better in 2-4 weeks. More significant tendinopathy or irritation can take 6-12 weeks of consistent, progressive rehabilitation to see significant improvement in strength and function.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if you have significant weakness (e.g., a positive Drop Arm Test), if the pain resulted from a specific trauma, or if there is no improvement after 3-4 weeks of dedicated rehab."},"infraspinatus":{"origin":"Infraspinous fossa of the scapula (the main flat part on the back of your shoulder blade).","insertion":"Greater tubercle of the humerus (the top, outer part of your upper arm bone).","action":"Primarily rotates your arm outward (external rotation), like when you're winding up to throw a ball. It also helps stabilize the shoulder joint.","nerve":"Suprascapular nerve (C5, C6).","symptoms":["A deep, aching pain felt in the front of the shoulder.","Pain that travels down the front and outside of the arm, sometimes to the hand.","Difficulty reaching behind your back, like to fasten a bra or grab a wallet.","Inability to sleep on the affected shoulder due to pain.","A feeling of weakness when trying to lift or rotate your arm outward."],"questions":[{"q":"Is it painful to sleep on your affected shoulder?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel a deep ache in the FRONT of your shoulder, even though the muscle is on the back?","w":3},{"q":"Is it difficult to reach behind your back, like tucking in a shirt or fastening a bra?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain travel down the outside or front of your arm?","w":2},{"q":"Do you have trouble combing your hair or reaching for something on a high shelf?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"External Rotation Resistance Test","how":"Bend your affected arm's elbow to 90 degrees, keeping it tucked into your side. Use your other hand to gently resist as you try to rotate your forearm outward.","positive":"Pain or noticeable weakness in the back of your shoulder compared to the other side."},{"name":"Hand-to-Back Reach","how":"Try to reach your affected arm behind your back and up toward your opposite shoulder blade. Compare how high you can reach with your good arm.","positive":"Significant pain or a major difference in how high you can reach."}],"causes":["Repetitive overhead activities (e.g., throwing, swimming, painting).","Sudden trauma, like a fall onto an outstretched arm.","Poor posture, with rounded shoulders and a forward head position.","Sleeping on the same side every night, compressing the shoulder.","Weakness in the surrounding back and core muscles, which overloads the shoulder."],"mimics":["Shoulder bursitis","Biceps tendonitis","Cervical radiculopathy (pinched nerve in the neck)","Shoulder joint arthritis","Supraspinatus tear"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe pain with an inability to lift your arm at all (possible full-thickness tear).","Pain accompanied by fever, chills, or unexplained weight loss.","Visible deformity of the shoulder joint after an injury.","Numbness or tingling that is constant and doesn't change with position."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Lacrosse Ball Release","video":null,"dose":"30-60 seconds, 3-5 times daily","how":"Lie on your back and place a lacrosse ball or tennis ball on the muscle belly on your shoulder blade. Gently roll to find a tender spot and hold pressure until the tenderness eases."},{"name":"Pendulum Swings","video":null,"dose":"2 x 10 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Lean over, supporting your good arm on a table. Let the affected arm hang down and gently swing it in small circles, using your body's momentum, not your shoulder muscles."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once or twice daily","how":"Lie on your back with your feet on a chair or wall, knees and hips at 90 degrees. Focus on deep belly breaths to relax the nervous system and reduce muscle guarding."}],"restore":[{"name":"Sleeper Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 seconds, once daily","how":"Lie on your affected side with your arm bent at 90 degrees in front of you. Use your other hand to gently press your forearm down toward the floor until you feel a stretch in the back of the shoulder."},{"name":"Lying Open Book","video":"Lying Open Book","dose":"2 x 8 reps per side, once daily","how":"Lie on your side with knees bent and arms straight out in front. Keeping your hips still, rotate your top arm and upper body open toward the ceiling to improve thoracic mobility."},{"name":"Isometric External Rotation","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-second holds, twice daily","how":"Stand in a doorway with your elbow bent to 90 degrees, tucked at your side. Press the back of your hand into the door frame as if trying to rotate outward, but don't let it move."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Rotator Cuff External Rotation","video":"Rotator Cuff External Rotation","dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Use a light resistance band. Keep your elbow tucked into your side at a 90-degree angle and rotate your forearm outward against the band's resistance. Control the movement back."},{"name":"Abducted External Rotation","video":"Abducted External Rotation","dose":"3 x 10 reps, every other day","how":"Raise your arm to the side, parallel to the floor, with your elbow bent at 90 degrees. Rotate your hand upward toward the ceiling, keeping your shoulder blade stable."},{"name":"Dumbbell Row","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 10-12 reps, every other day","how":"Support one knee and hand on a bench. Pull a dumbbell up toward your chest, squeezing your shoulder blade. This strengthens the back muscles that support the rotator cuff."}]},"habits":["Avoid sleeping directly on the painful shoulder. Try sleeping on your back or other side with a pillow to hug.","When sitting, ensure your shoulders are relaxed and back, not rounded forward. Use our 'Seated Desk Posture Reset' video.","Take frequent micro-breaks from computer work to roll your shoulders back and down.","When lifting, keep your elbows close to your body to reduce strain on the shoulder joint."],"expect":"With consistent adherence to the protocol, most people feel a significant reduction in pain within 2-4 weeks. Full strength and function may take 6-8 weeks to restore.","seePro":"If pain is severe, doesn't improve after 2 weeks of self-care, or if you suspect a tear from an injury, book an appointment for a full diagnostic evaluation."},"teres-minor":{"origin":"Upper two-thirds of the lateral border of the scapula (the outer edge of your shoulder blade).","insertion":"Greater tubercle of the humerus (just below the infraspinatus insertion on your upper arm bone).","action":"Rotates your arm outward (external rotation) and helps pull the head of the arm bone into the socket, stabilizing the shoulder.","nerve":"Axillary nerve (C5, C6).","symptoms":["A distinct, localized, deep ache at the back of the shoulder.","Pain is often described as a 'silver dollar' sized spot of tenderness.","Pain is sharp when reaching overhead or across the body.","Unlike infraspinatus, the pain rarely travels far down the arm.","Tenderness to direct pressure on the muscle, just below the bony ridge of the shoulder blade."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel a very specific, deep ache at the back of your shoulder, about the size of a coin?","w":3},{"q":"Does pressing on the fleshy part just below the bony point of your shoulder reproduce your exact pain?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain mostly localized to the back of the shoulder, without traveling down your arm?","w":2},{"q":"Does it hurt when you reach up and across your body, like putting on a seatbelt?","w":1},{"q":"Is it painful to lie on that shoulder at night?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Hornblower's Sign","how":"Lift your affected arm to the side to 90 degrees (parallel to the floor) and bend your elbow to 90 degrees. Try to hold this position; a positive test occurs if your arm drops into internal rotation.","positive":"Inability to hold the externally rotated position, indicating significant weakness."},{"name":"Palpation Test","how":"Reach across your body with your opposite hand to feel the back of your painful shoulder. Find the outer edge of your shoulder blade and press firmly into the muscle just below the bony spine.","positive":"Pressing on this spot reproduces your specific, deep ache."}],"causes":["Overuse in throwing sports or racquet sports.","Working with arms overhead for long periods (e.g., electrician, painter).","A sudden forceful outward rotation of the arm.","Chronic poor posture with rounded shoulders.","Imbalance between the strong internal rotators (chest/lats) and weaker external rotators."],"mimics":["Infraspinatus strain (often occur together)","Posterior shoulder impingement","Labral tear (SLAP lesion)","Thoracic outlet syndrome"],"redFlags":["Significant weakness or inability to rotate your arm outward after an injury.","A 'popping' or 'clicking' sensation deep within the joint during movement.","Pain associated with numbness or tingling in the hand, especially the pinky and ring finger.","Visible atrophy or wasting of the muscles on the back of the shoulder."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Teres Minor Ball Release","video":null,"dose":"30-60 seconds, 3-5 times daily","how":"Place a lacrosse ball between your shoulder blade and the wall, targeting the tender spot on the outer edge. Lean into the ball and hold steady pressure or make small movements."},{"name":"Pendulum Swings","video":null,"dose":"2 x 10 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Lean over, supporting your good arm on a table. Let the affected arm hang down and gently swing it in small circles to decompress the joint."},{"name":"Scapular CARs","video":"Scapular CARs","dose":"2 x 5 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Keeping your arms straight, gently move your shoulder blades through their full range of motion: up, forward, down, and back. This promotes gentle movement and awareness."}],"restore":[{"name":"Doorway Chest Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 seconds, twice daily","how":"Place your forearms on a door frame with elbows slightly below shoulder height. Step through gently to feel a stretch across your chest, which helps improve shoulder posture."},{"name":"Isometric External Rotation","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-second holds, twice daily","how":"Stand in a doorway with your elbow bent to 90 degrees, tucked at your side. Press the back of your hand into the door frame to activate the muscle without movement."},{"name":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","video":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","dose":"2 x 10 reps, once daily","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Press your forearms into the floor and slide them overhead, focusing on keeping your shoulder blades down and back."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Rotator Cuff External Rotation","video":"Rotator Cuff External Rotation","dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"With a light band and elbow at your side, rotate your forearm outward. Focus on the small muscles at the back of your shoulder doing the work."},{"name":"Face Pull","video":"Face Pull","dose":"3 x 12-15 reps, every other day","how":"Using a cable or band at chest height, pull the handles toward your face, driving your elbows back and externally rotating your shoulders at the end of the movement."},{"name":"Dumbbell Row","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 10-12 reps, every other day","how":"Strengthen the supporting back muscles by rowing a dumbbell, focusing on squeezing the shoulder blade toward the spine."}]},"habits":["Strengthen your mid-back muscles (rhomboids, mid-traps) to create a stable base for your shoulder.","Be mindful of your posture when sitting or standing; avoid letting your shoulders slump forward.","When reaching overhead, engage your core and avoid arching your lower back.","Warm up properly before any sport or activity involving overhead arm movements."],"expect":"Because this is a small muscle, targeted exercises can bring relief within 1-3 weeks. Building robust strength to prevent recurrence typically takes 4-6 weeks.","seePro":"If you have significant weakness (your arm drops in the Hornblower's test) or the localized pain doesn't improve with 2 weeks of care, schedule an evaluation to rule out a tear."},"subscapularis":{"origin":"Subscapular fossa (the entire front surface of your shoulder blade that faces your ribs).","insertion":"Lesser tubercle of the humerus (a small bump on the front of your upper arm bone).","action":"The primary internal rotator of the arm (rotates it inward). It's a powerful stabilizer that holds the head of the humerus in the socket.","nerve":"Upper and Lower Subscapular nerves (C5, C6).","symptoms":["Aching pain felt at the back of the shoulder and down the back of the upper arm.","A strange, band-like sensation or pain around the wrist, like a tight watchband.","Profound stiffness and inability to lift the arm, mimicking a 'frozen shoulder'.","Pain when reaching across the body or behind the back.","A 'catching' sensation in the shoulder with certain movements."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel a strange band of pain or tightness around your wrist, even though the main problem is your shoulder?","w":3},{"q":"Does your shoulder feel incredibly stiff, as if it's 'frozen', making it hard to lift your arm to the side?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain primarily at the back of your shoulder and down the back of your arm?","w":2},{"q":"Is it painful to reach across your chest to touch your opposite shoulder?","w":2},{"q":"Do you have difficulty reaching into your back pocket?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Belly Press Test","how":"Place the palm of your hand on your stomach. Try to press into your belly while keeping your wrist straight. Compare the strength to your other side.","positive":"Pain in the shoulder, or you have to bend your wrist or let your elbow drift backward to generate pressure, indicating weakness."},{"name":"Lift-Off Test","how":"Place the back of your hand on your lower back. Try to lift your hand away from your back. If you can't, this is a positive test.","positive":"Inability to lift the hand off the back, or severe pain when attempting to do so."}],"causes":["Repetitive forceful internal rotation (e.g., swimming, throwing).","Falling on an outstretched arm.","Immobilization of the shoulder, such as after a fracture or surgery.","Chronic poor posture, which shortens the muscle.","Over-training 'push' muscles (chest, front of shoulder) without balancing with 'pull' exercises."],"mimics":["Adhesive capsulitis (Frozen Shoulder)","Cervical radiculopathy (C7/C8 nerve root)","Long head of biceps tendon pathology","AC joint sprain","Carpal tunnel syndrome (due to the wrist referral)"],"redFlags":["Complete inability to lift your arm away from your side (abduction).","History of shoulder dislocation, as subscapularis tears are common with this injury.","Rapid onset of stiffness and pain without any known cause.","Pain accompanied by swelling, redness, or warmth over the shoulder joint."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Subscapularis Release","video":null,"dose":"30-60 seconds, 2-3 times daily","how":"Lie on your side with your arm overhead. Use your opposite thumb to press gently into your armpit, searching for the tender muscle on the front of your shoulder blade. Hold gentle pressure."},{"name":"Pendulum Swings","video":null,"dose":"2 x 10 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Lean over a table, letting the arm hang loose. Use your body to create gentle momentum and swing the arm in small circles to decompress the joint."},{"name":"Shoulder Drop Hold","video":"Shoulder Drop Hold","dose":"3 x 30-second holds, twice daily","how":"Lie on your back and let your arm fall open to the side, supported by the floor. This provides a gentle, passive stretch to the front of the shoulder."}],"restore":[{"name":"Doorway Stretch (Internal Rotators)","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 seconds, twice daily","how":"Stand in a doorway and place the back of your hand and wrist on the frame, with your elbow bent at 90 degrees. Gently turn your body away from the arm to feel a stretch."},{"name":"Isometric Internal Rotation","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-second holds, twice daily","how":"Stand facing the outside edge of a door frame. With your elbow at 90 degrees, press your palm into the frame as if trying to rotate your arm inward."},{"name":"Rotator Cuff Internal Rotation","video":"Rotator Cuff Internal Rotation","dose":"2 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Anchor a light band. With your elbow at your side, pull the band across your body, rotating your arm inward. Control the movement back to the start."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Abducted Internal Rotation","video":"Abducted Internal Rotation","dose":"3 x 10 reps, every other day","how":"Lie on your back with your arm abducted to 90 degrees, elbow bent. Slowly lower a very light weight toward the floor, then rotate it back up. This strengthens the muscle in a functional position."},{"name":"Push Up","video":"Push Up","dose":"3 x as many as possible with good form, every other day","how":"Perform push-ups (on knees or toes). This integrates the subscapularis with other pressing muscles like the chest and serratus anterior."},{"name":"Lat Pulldown","video":"Lat Pulldown","dose":"3 x 10-12 reps, every other day","how":"This exercise strengthens the lats, which are partners in internal rotation, helping to balance the shoulder complex."}]},"habits":["Balance your workout routine. For every 'push' exercise (like bench press), do a 'pull' exercise (like rows).","Stretch your chest and lats regularly to prevent them from pulling your shoulder forward and shortening the subscapularis.","Avoid carrying heavy bags with a straight arm; keep your elbow slightly bent.","Practice proper posture, ensuring your shoulder blades are gently pulled back and down."],"expect":"Due to its tendency to cause significant stiffness, subscapularis issues can be stubborn. Expect gradual improvement in mobility over 3-4 weeks, with strength returning over 6-12 weeks. Consistency is key.","seePro":"If you have symptoms of a 'frozen shoulder' (severe, progressive loss of motion) or if you fail the 'Lift-Off Test', it's crucial to get a professional diagnosis to rule out a full tear or adhesive capsulitis."},"deltoid":{"origin":"Clavicle (collarbone), acromion (top of the shoulder), and spine of the scapula (shoulder blade).","insertion":"Deltoid tuberosity of the humerus (a rough patch on the outside of your upper arm bone).","action":"The primary mover of the arm. The front part (anterior) lifts the arm forward, the middle (lateral) lifts it to the side, and the back (posterior) extends it backward.","nerve":"Axillary nerve (C5, C6).","symptoms":["Aching pain localized to the 'cap' of the shoulder.","Pain does not typically travel far down the arm.","Difficulty lifting the arm, especially to the side.","Pain is worse with activity and better with rest.","The muscle itself feels sore and tender to the touch."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located right on the 'cap' of your shoulder, where a military patch would sit?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain get worse when you try to lift your arm out to the side, like making a 'T'?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain more of a muscle ache rather than a deep, sharp joint pain?","w":2},{"q":"Does carrying a heavy bag or grocery bags make the shoulder ache?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain fairly localized and doesn't travel down your arm or into your neck?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Empty Can Test (Modified)","how":"Lift your affected arm to the side and slightly forward (about 30 degrees), with your thumb pointing down. Have a partner apply gentle downward pressure as you resist.","positive":"Pain localized to the top of the shoulder. (Note: This can also indicate a supraspinatus issue, but deltoid pain is more superficial)."},{"name":"Resisted Abduction","how":"Stand with your arm at your side. Try to lift it straight out to the side while using your other hand to provide gentle resistance at the wrist.","positive":"Pain or weakness felt directly in the deltoid muscle on the cap of the shoulder."}],"causes":["Direct trauma or blow to the shoulder.","Overdoing it with weightlifting, especially overhead presses or lateral raises.","Repetitive lifting or carrying heavy objects.","Poor injection technique (if receiving a shot in the arm).","Sleeping awkwardly on the arm."],"mimics":["Supraspinatus tendinopathy/tear (very common)","Shoulder impingement syndrome","AC joint arthritis or sprain","Subacromial bursitis"],"redFlags":["Severe pain and swelling after a fall or direct impact.","Inability to lift the arm at all.","Numbness or loss of sensation over the 'cap' of the shoulder (possible axillary nerve injury).","A visible 'divot' or deformity in the muscle."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Ice Pack Application","video":null,"dose":"15 minutes, 2-3 times daily","how":"Apply a cold pack wrapped in a thin towel directly to the sore part of the shoulder to help manage pain and inflammation, especially after activity."},{"name":"Pendulum Swings","video":null,"dose":"2 x 10 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Gently swing the arm in small circles while leaning over. This uses gravity to create space in the joint without actively using the deltoid muscle."},{"name":"Scapular CARs","video":"Scapular CARs","dose":"2 x 5 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Move your shoulder blades through their full range of motion without moving your arms. This improves the foundation upon which the deltoid works."}],"restore":[{"name":"Cross-Body Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 seconds, twice daily","how":"Gently pull your affected arm across your chest with your other arm. You should feel a stretch in the back and side of your shoulder."},{"name":"Isometric Abduction","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-second holds, twice daily","how":"Stand with your side against a wall, arm straight. Press your arm outward into the wall as if trying to lift it, but don't let it move. This activates the muscle gently."},{"name":"Shoulder Drop Hold","video":"Shoulder Drop Hold","dose":"3 x 30-second holds, twice daily","how":"Lie on your back and let your arm fall open to the side. This provides a gentle stretch to the front part of the deltoid and chest."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Lateral Raise","video":"Lateral Raise","dose":"3 x 10-15 reps with very light weight, every other day","how":"Stand with light weights. Lift your arms out to the side up to shoulder height, keeping a slight bend in your elbows. Control the movement on the way down."},{"name":"Strict Press","video":"Strict Press","dose":"3 x 8-10 reps with light weight, every other day","how":"Start with dumbbells at shoulder height. Press them straight overhead, keeping your core tight. This integrates all parts of the deltoid."},{"name":"Face Pull","video":"Face Pull","dose":"3 x 12-15 reps, every other day","how":"This exercise strengthens the rear deltoids and upper back, which helps to balance the shoulder and improve posture."}]},"habits":["Use proper form when lifting weights, avoiding momentum or using weights that are too heavy.","Carry heavy items close to your body to reduce the lever arm and strain on the deltoid.","Alternate which shoulder you use to carry a bag or purse.","Ensure your workstation is set up ergonomically to avoid reaching for your mouse or keyboard. Watch our 'Desk Setup Tutorial'."],"expect":"Deltoid strains are muscle injuries that typically respond well to rest and progressive exercise. Minor strains often feel much better within 1-2 weeks, with a return to full strength in 4-6 weeks.","seePro":"If you have significant weakness, numbness over the muscle, or the pain doesn't improve with a week of rest and gentle motion, book an appointment. A severe, sudden pain during a lift could indicate a tear."},"biceps":{"origin":"Long head: Supraglenoid tubercle of the scapula (a small bump just above the shoulder socket). Short head: Coracoid process of the scapula.","insertion":"Radial tuberosity (a bump on the radius bone in your forearm) and bicipital aponeurosis.","action":"Bends (flexes) the elbow, supinates the forearm (turns the palm up), and the long head helps lift the arm forward and stabilize the front of the shoulder.","nerve":"Musculocutaneous nerve (C5, C6, C7).","symptoms":["Aching pain located at the very front of the shoulder.","Pain that travels down the front of the arm, often stopping before the elbow.","A 'snapping' or 'clicking' sound in the front of the shoulder with certain movements.","Pain when lifting with your palm up, like carrying a tray.","Tenderness to pressure in the groove at the front of your shoulder."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located right at the front of your shoulder, in the groove between the deltoid and chest muscle?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel or hear a 'snap' or 'click' in the front of your shoulder when you rotate your arm or lift it?","w":3},{"q":"Does it hurt to lift things with your palm facing up, like a heavy pan?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain travel down the 'bicep' part of your arm?","w":2},{"q":"Is it painful to reach overhead, especially when also reaching slightly behind you?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Speed's Test","how":"Hold your affected arm straight out in front of you with your palm facing up. Use your other hand to apply gentle downward pressure at your wrist as you try to resist and lift your arm higher.","positive":"Pain located in the bicipital groove at the front of the shoulder."},{"name":"Direct Palpation","how":"Find the bony groove at the very front of your shoulder. Gently press into this groove with your fingertips.","positive":"Sharp tenderness that reproduces your specific pain, which may indicate tendon inflammation."}],"causes":["Repetitive overhead lifting or throwing.","Overuse from curling exercises with poor form or excessive weight.","Shoulder impingement, where the tendon gets pinched by surrounding structures.","Instability or weakness in the rotator cuff, which forces the biceps tendon to overwork as a stabilizer.","Age-related wear and tear on the tendon."],"mimics":["Infraspinatus trigger points (which refer to the front of the shoulder)","Subscapularis strain","Labral tear (SLAP lesion)","Shoulder impingement syndrome","AC joint pathology"],"redFlags":["A sudden, sharp pain in the front of the shoulder followed by a 'pop' and subsequent bruising down the arm.","A visible bulge in the lower part of the bicep muscle, often called a 'Popeye sign' (indicates a tendon rupture).","Inability to bend your elbow against resistance.","Pain that is constant, severe, and not relieved by rest."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Ice Massage","video":null,"dose":"5-7 minutes, twice daily","how":"Freeze water in a paper cup. Tear the rim off and massage the ice directly over the painful tendon at the front of the shoulder. Keep the ice moving."},{"name":"Pendulum Swings","video":null,"dose":"2 x 10 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Lean over and let the arm hang, creating space in the joint. This is a non-provocative way to maintain mobility."},{"name":"Wall Corner Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 seconds, twice daily","how":"Stand in a corner, place one forearm on each wall with elbows below shoulder height. Gently lean in to feel a stretch across the chest and front of the shoulders."}],"restore":[{"name":"Baseball Cable Stretch","video":"Baseball Cable Stretch","dose":"2 x 30 seconds, once daily","how":"This stretch targets the biceps and anterior shoulder capsule. Follow the video cues for proper positioning and gentle, controlled stretching."},{"name":"Isometric Bicep Curl","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-second holds, twice daily","how":"Bend your elbow to 90 degrees. Use your other hand to resist as you try to curl your arm up. This activates the muscle without stressing the tendon."},{"name":"Eccentric Wrist Pronator","video":"Eccentric Wrist Pronator","dose":"3 x 10 reps, every other day","how":"While this focuses on the forearm, the controlled rotation can help with neuromuscular control that affects the biceps tendon. Follow the video cues carefully."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Eccentric Bicep Curls","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 reps, every other day","how":"Use a very light dumbbell. Use your other hand to help lift the weight up, then slowly lower it over 3-5 seconds using only the affected arm. This strengthens the tendon."},{"name":"Dumbbell Row","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 10-12 reps, every other day","how":"Strengthening the back helps stabilize the shoulder, reducing the load on the biceps tendon. Focus on squeezing your shoulder blade."},{"name":"Rotator Cuff External Rotation","video":"Rotator Cuff External Rotation","dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"A strong rotator cuff is essential for shoulder stability, which unloads the biceps tendon. Keep your elbow tucked in."}]},"habits":["Avoid lifting heavy objects with your palms up; try a neutral (thumbs-up) grip instead.","When doing bicep curls, use a weight you can control without swinging your body or letting your shoulder roll forward.","Improve your posture to create more space in the front of the shoulder. Use the 'Standing Posture Reset' video.","During overhead activities, focus on using your back and shoulder blade muscles, not just your arm."],"expect":"Biceps tendonitis can be persistent. With consistent eccentric exercises and activity modification, you should notice a decrease in pain within 3-4 weeks. A full return to heavy lifting may take 8-12 weeks.","seePro":"If you experience a 'pop' or see a 'Popeye' bulge, seek medical attention promptly. If pain doesn't improve after 2-3 weeks of dedicated rehab, book an appointment for an evaluation, as an ultrasound may be needed."},"triceps":{"origin":"The back of the shoulder blade and the upper arm bone (humerus).","insertion":"The bony point of the elbow (olecranon process).","action":"Straightens the elbow. The long head also helps bring the arm down and back. It's your main 'pushing' muscle.","nerve":"Radial Nerve (C6, C7, C8, T1)","symptoms":["Aching pain in the back of the shoulder and upper arm.","Pain that refers to the outside of the elbow, often mistaken for 'tennis elbow'.","A feeling of weakness when pushing or straightening your arm.","Difficulty fully extending the elbow, especially when carrying something.","Pain may be a deep, nagging ache.","Sleep can be disturbed if you lie on the affected arm."],"questions":[{"q":"Does the back of your upper arm ache, especially after pushing activities like push-ups or yard work?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel pain on the *outside* of your elbow, even though the problem seems to be higher up your arm?","w":3},{"q":"Is it difficult or does it feel weak to fully straighten your arm against resistance, like pushing a heavy door?","w":2},{"q":"Does reaching overhead to a high shelf seem to aggravate the back of your arm?","w":2},{"q":"Do you find yourself propping your head on your hand with your elbow bent for long periods?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Resisted Elbow Extension Test","how":"Sit or stand. Bend your affected arm's elbow to 90 degrees. Use your other hand to gently resist as you try to straighten the affected arm.","positive":"Pain or noticeable weakness in the back of your upper arm as you push."}],"causes":["Repetitive pushing activities (push-ups, bench press, dips).","Using armrests that are too high, forcing the elbow into a bent position.","Bracing for a fall with your arms.","Prolonged gripping or typing with elbows sharply bent.","Direct trauma or impact to the back of the arm."],"mimics":["Tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis)","C7 or C8 nerve root irritation (radiculopathy)","Shoulder joint arthritis or rotator cuff issues","Olecranon bursitis (inflammation at the elbow tip)"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe pain with an inability to straighten the elbow after an injury (possible tendon rupture).","Significant swelling, redness, or heat around the elbow joint.","Numbness or tingling that extends into the hand, especially after an injury.","Pain that is constant, severe, and not relieved by rest."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Triceps Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, once daily","how":"Lie on your side and place a lacrosse ball or foam roller under the back of your upper arm. Gently roll to find tender spots and hold pressure for 30 seconds on each."},{"name":"Gentle Elbow Bends","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15, twice daily","how":"Sit or stand. Gently and slowly bend and straighten your elbow through a pain-free range of motion. Do not force it."}],"restore":[{"name":"Overhead Triceps Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec, twice daily","how":"Raise your arm overhead and bend the elbow so your hand is behind your neck. Use your other hand to gently pull the elbow back, feeling the stretch in the back of your arm."},{"name":"Thread The Needle","video":"Thread The Needle","dose":"2 x 10 per side, once daily","how":"This mobilizes the shoulder blade and gently stretches the back of the shoulder, which can help the triceps long head."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Tricep Kickback","video":"Tricep Kickback","dose":"3 x 12-15, every other day","how":"Use a light weight or resistance band. Hinge at your hips, keep your back flat, and extend your elbow straight back, squeezing the triceps at the end."},{"name":"Overhead Tricep Extension","video":"Overhead Tricep Extension","dose":"3 x 10-12, every other day","how":"Start with a light weight. Focus on controlling the movement as you lower the weight and fully extending at the top."}]},"habits":["Adjust your chair so your elbows are at a 90-95 degree angle when typing, not sharply bent.","Avoid leaning on your elbows for long periods.","When lifting, focus on using your whole body, not just pushing with your arms.","Take frequent breaks from repetitive pushing or gripping tasks."],"expect":"With consistent adherence to the protocol, most people feel a significant reduction in pain within 2-3 weeks. Full strength and function may take 4-6 weeks to restore.","seePro":"If pain is severe, doesn't improve after 2 weeks of self-care, or you have any of the 'Red Flags,' please book an appointment with Dr. Caban for a full evaluation."},"pec-major":{"origin":"The collarbone (clavicle), breastbone (sternum), and upper ribs.","insertion":"The upper arm bone (humerus).","action":"Brings the arm across the body, rotates it inward, and pulls it down from an overhead position. Key movements include pushing, hugging, and throwing.","nerve":"Medial and Lateral Pectoral Nerves (C5, C6, C7, C8, T1)","symptoms":["Aching pain in the front of the chest and shoulder.","Pain that travels down the inside of the arm, sometimes to the pinky and ring fingers.","A feeling that can mimic a 'heart attack,' which must always be ruled out by a doctor first.","Sharp pain with deep breaths, coughing, or reaching across your body.","A sense of tightness or restriction across the chest.","Difficulty sleeping on your side or stomach."],"questions":[{"q":"After ruling out heart issues, do you have a persistent ache in the front of your chest or shoulder?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain seem to travel down the *inside* of your arm, perhaps with tingling in your last two fingers?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain worse after prolonged desk work, driving, or looking down at your phone?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel a sharp 'catch' when you reach across your body or lift something heavy in front of you?","w":2},{"q":"Does stretching your chest by opening your arms wide feel tight or painful?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Doorway Stretch Test","how":"Stand in a doorway and place your forearms on the frame with elbows slightly below shoulder height. Step forward with one foot until you feel a stretch in your chest.","positive":"Sharp pain (not just a stretch) in the chest or front of the shoulder, or a reproduction of your familiar symptoms."}],"causes":["Prolonged slouching at a desk or while driving ('rounded shoulder' posture).","Heavy bench pressing or excessive push-ups, especially with poor form.","Carrying heavy bags with a strap over one shoulder.","Chronic coughing from a respiratory illness.","Sleeping curled up on your side."],"mimics":["Heart attack (angina) - MUST BE RULED OUT FIRST.","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Rib joint dysfunction (costochondritis)","Shoulder impingement or rotator cuff tear","C8/T1 nerve root irritation (radiculopathy)"],"redFlags":["Any chest pain with shortness of breath, sweating, nausea, dizziness, or pain radiating to the jaw or left arm requires an immediate 911 call.","Chest pain that feels crushing, squeezing, or like heavy pressure.","Pain that worsens with exertion (like walking) and improves with rest.","A sudden 'pop' or tearing sensation during activity, followed by bruising and weakness."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Pec Major Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, once daily","how":"Lie on your stomach and place a lacrosse or tennis ball just inside your armpit on the fleshy part of your chest. Gently roll to find tender spots and hold for 30 seconds."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once or twice daily","how":"This helps relax the chest wall and diaphragm, reducing tension in the pectoral muscles."},{"name":"Lying Open Book","video":"Lying Open Book","dose":"2 x 10 per side, once daily","how":"Perform this movement slowly and gently, focusing on breathing and allowing gravity to open the chest. Do not force the rotation."}],"restore":[{"name":"Doorway Pec Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec, twice daily","how":"Stand in a doorway, place forearms on the frame with elbows just below shoulder height. Step forward gently to feel a stretch across your chest. Keep your chin tucked."},{"name":"Full Neuroglide Flow Seated","video":"Full Neuroglide Flow Seated","dose":"1 x 5 repetitions, once daily","how":"This helps address any nerve tension down the arm that can be associated with pec tightness."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Wall Push Up","video":"Wall Push Up","dose":"3 x 10-15, every other day","how":"Focus on squeezing your shoulder blades together as you lower your chest to the wall. This activates the back muscles to balance the chest."},{"name":"Dumbbell Row","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 10-12 per side, every other day","how":"Strengthening the back muscles is crucial for improving posture and taking the strain off the pecs. Keep your back flat and pull the weight towards your hip."}]},"habits":["Use a lumbar roll to sit upright at your desk or in the car.","Set up your workstation ergonomically. Watch our 'Desk Setup Tutorial'.","Avoid sleeping on your stomach or curled tightly on your side. Try sleeping on your back with a pillow under your knees.","When carrying bags, use a backpack with both straps or switch sides frequently."],"expect":"You may feel relief from the acute pain within 1-2 weeks. Correcting the underlying postural habits and strengthening the back can take 4-8 weeks for lasting change.","seePro":"If you have ANY concern your chest pain could be heart-related, go to the ER. For musculoskeletal pain, see us if it doesn't improve in 2-3 weeks or if you have significant numbness/tingling in your hand."},"pec-minor":{"origin":"Ribs 3, 4, and 5.","insertion":"A small hook-like bone on the front of the shoulder blade (coracoid process).","action":"Pulls the shoulder blade forward and down. It's a key muscle in creating 'rounded shoulder' posture and can pinch nerves and blood vessels going to the arm.","nerve":"Medial Pectoral Nerve (C8, T1)","symptoms":["Sharp pain at the very front of the shoulder, under the collarbone.","Numbness, tingling, or weakness in the arm and hand, especially the last three fingers.","Pain that worsens when reaching overhead or backward.","A feeling of the arm 'going to sleep' easily, especially when sleeping or carrying a backpack.","A rounded or forward-slumped shoulder posture on one side.","Difficulty taking a full, deep breath into the upper chest."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you get numbness or tingling down your arm and into your hand when you carry a backpack or heavy purse?","w":3},{"q":"Is your pain located in a specific spot at the very front of your shoulder, just below the collarbone?","w":3},{"q":"Does reaching straight up, like to a high shelf, cause a sharp, pinching pain in the front of your shoulder?","w":2},{"q":"Do you often wake up with a 'dead arm' or pins and needles in your hand?","w":2},{"q":"When you look in the mirror, does one shoulder seem to sit lower and more forward than the other?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Pec Minor Length Test","how":"Lie on your back on the floor with your knees bent. Let your arms rest at your sides, palms up. Check if the back of your shoulder is touching the floor.","positive":"The back of your affected shoulder cannot rest comfortably on the floor without you arching your back. This suggests the pec minor is pulling it forward."}],"causes":["Chronic slouching posture with rounded shoulders (desk work, phone use).","Repetitive overhead reaching, especially with poor mechanics.","Using a computer mouse that is too far away, causing a constant forward reach.","Heavy breathing from conditions like asthma or after intense cardio.","Sleeping in a curled-up fetal position."],"mimics":["Thoracic outlet syndrome (Pec Minor is a common cause of this)","Shoulder impingement syndrome","Biceps tendonitis","C8/T1 nerve root irritation (radiculopathy)","AC joint sprain"],"redFlags":["Persistent numbness, tingling, or weakness in the hand that doesn't go away with position changes.","A cool or pale hand compared to the other side, suggesting poor circulation.","A visible lump or deformity at the front of the shoulder.","Any chest pain that could be heart-related (see Pec Major red flags)."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Pec Minor Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"2 minutes, once daily","how":"Place a lacrosse ball between your upper chest (just below the collarbone) and a wall or door frame. Gently apply pressure and make small movements to release this small, deep muscle."},{"name":"Thoracic Extension Over Chair","video":"Thoracic Extension Over Chair","dose":"5-10 breaths, 3-5 times per day","how":"This gently opens the chest and reverses the slumped posture that shortens the pec minor. Focus on breathing into your upper chest."}],"restore":[{"name":"Corner Pec Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec, twice daily","how":"Stand in a corner, place forearms on each wall with elbows below shoulder height. Gently lean in until you feel a stretch in the front of your shoulders and chest. Avoid arching your back."},{"name":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","video":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","dose":"2 x 10, once daily","how":"This activates the muscles that pull your shoulder blades back and down, directly opposing the pull of a tight pec minor."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Face Pull","video":"Face Pull","dose":"3 x 15, every other day","how":"This is a key exercise to strengthen the upper back and external rotators of the shoulder, promoting better posture."},{"name":"Scap Push Ups","video":"Scap Push Ups","dose":"3 x 10, every other day","how":"This strengthens the serratus anterior muscle, which helps hold the shoulder blade against the rib cage and counteracts the pec minor's pull."}]},"habits":["Position your mouse close to your keyboard to avoid reaching.","Use a headset for phone calls instead of cradling the phone.","When sitting, imagine a string pulling the top of your head towards the ceiling.","Perform a 'Seated Desk Posture Reset' every 30 minutes."],"expect":"Relief from nerve-like symptoms can occur within a week of consistent release and stretching. Lasting postural changes and strength balance will likely take 6-8 weeks of dedicated effort.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if you have persistent or worsening numbness, tingling, weakness, or color changes in your hand, or if your pain doesn't improve after 3 weeks."},"rhomboids":{"origin":"The spine (vertebrae C7 to T5).","insertion":"The inner border of the shoulder blade (scapula).","action":"Pull the shoulder blades together (retraction) and slightly upward. They are key postural muscles that oppose the pull of the chest muscles.","nerve":"Dorsal Scapular Nerve (C5)","symptoms":["A constant, nagging, superficial ache between the shoulder blade and the spine.","Pain may feel like it needs to be 'dug into' or massaged for relief.","A crunching or grinding sound (crepitus) when moving the shoulder blades.","The area often feels tender to the touch.","Pain is often the result of being over-stretched and weak, not tight.","Symptoms typically worsen after prolonged sitting or desk work."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you have a persistent, nagging ache right between your shoulder blade and your spine?","w":3},{"q":"Do you find yourself trying to stretch the area by rounding your back, even though it only gives temporary relief?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain worse after a long day of sitting at a computer or driving?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain feel better temporarily if you squeeze your shoulder blades together or get someone to press on the area?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain feel more like a surface-level ache rather than a deep, sharp pain?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Wall Posture Check","how":"Stand with your back against a wall, heels a few inches away. Your head, shoulder blades, and tailbone should be able to touch the wall without straining.","positive":"If your shoulder blades are pulled far away from the wall and you have to force them back, it suggests they are being pulled forward by tight chest muscles, straining the rhomboids."}],"causes":["Slumped, 'rounded shoulder' posture that constantly stretches the rhomboids.","Tight pectoral (chest) muscles pulling the shoulders forward.","Carrying a heavy backpack or purse on one shoulder.","Repetitive reaching forward (e.g., desk work, assembly line).","Weakness in the rhomboids and middle trapezius muscles."],"mimics":["Thoracic spine joint dysfunction","C5 or C6 nerve root irritation (radiculopathy)","Lower trapezius or serratus posterior superior trigger points","Gallbladder referral pain (right side only)"],"redFlags":["Pain that is severe, sharp, and associated with breathing (could be a rib or lung issue).","Pain accompanied by fever, cough, or general feeling of being unwell.","Numbness or weakness radiating into the arm.","Pain that is constant and doesn't change with position or movement."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Rhomboid Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, once daily","how":"Place a lacrosse or tennis ball between your shoulder blade and spine. Lean against a wall and gently roll to find tender spots. Hold pressure for 30 seconds."},{"name":"Cat Cow Desk Variation","video":"Cat Cow Desk Variation","dose":"2 x 10, several times per day","how":"This gently mobilizes the thoracic spine and shoulder blades, providing temporary relief and improving movement."}],"restore":[{"name":"Doorway Pec Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec, twice daily","how":"Stretching the tight chest muscles is the most important step to take the strain *off* the rhomboids. Stand in a doorway and gently lean forward."},{"name":"Thoracic Rotation Seated","video":"Thoracic Rotation Seated","dose":"2 x 10 per side, once daily","how":"Improving mid-back mobility helps the shoulder blades move better and reduces strain on the rhomboids."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","video":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","dose":"3 x 10, every other day","how":"This directly activates and strengthens the rhomboids and mid-traps. Focus on initiating the movement by squeezing your shoulder blades together."},{"name":"Dumbbell Row","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 10-12 per side, every other day","how":"This is a primary strengthening exercise for the rhomboids. Keep your back flat and focus on pulling your shoulder blade toward your spine."}]},"habits":["Focus on sitting and standing tall, gently drawing your shoulder blades back and down.","Use our 'Seated Desk Posture Reset' video frequently throughout the day.","Strengthen your back, don't just stretch it. The pain is often from being over-stretched.","Adjust your monitor height so the top of the screen is at eye level."],"expect":"Because this pain is often a symptom of poor posture, you may feel temporary relief quickly, but lasting results require 4-8 weeks of consistent postural correction and strengthening.","seePro":"If the pain is severe, doesn't respond to postural correction and exercise within 3-4 weeks, or is accompanied by any 'Red Flags,' please schedule an appointment."},"mid-trap":{"origin":"The spine (vertebrae C7 to T12).","insertion":"The spine of the shoulder blade (scapula) and collarbone (clavicle).","action":"The middle fibers pull the shoulder blades together. The lower fibers pull the shoulder blades down. They are crucial for stabilizing the scapula during arm movements.","nerve":"Spinal Accessory Nerve (Cranial Nerve XI) and cervical nerves (C3, C4).","symptoms":["A burning or aching pain along the inner border of the shoulder blade.","Pain can also refer to the top of the shoulder, near the AC joint.","A feeling of weakness or fatigue in the upper back, especially with overhead activities.","Difficulty holding your shoulders back and down; they may feel like they want to slump forward.","Sometimes described as a 'hot poker' sensation near the scapula.","Pain is often absent at rest but comes on with activity or sustained postures."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel a burning ache along the inside edge of your shoulder blade, especially when trying to hold good posture?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain get worse when you reach overhead for a prolonged period, like painting a ceiling?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel a specific ache on the very top of your shoulder point?","w":2},{"q":"Does it feel like your upper back muscles are weak and get tired easily?","w":2},{"q":"Is your pain better in the morning and gets progressively worse throughout the day, especially a day at the desk?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Wall Angel Test","how":"Stand with your back against a wall, feet slightly forward. Try to slide your arms up the wall in a 'snow angel' motion while keeping your wrists, elbows, and shoulder blades in contact with the wall.","positive":"Inability to keep your arms or wrists against the wall, or a reproduction of your burning pain, suggests weakness in these muscles and tightness in the chest."}],"causes":["Chronic forward-slumped posture, which overstretches and weakens these muscles.","Repetitive overhead arm movements without proper scapular stabilization.","Carrying heavy loads that pull the shoulders down and forward.","An over-emphasis on 'pushing' exercises (like bench press) without enough 'pulling' exercises (like rows).","Shallow, chest-dominant breathing patterns."],"mimics":["Rhomboid trigger points (often co-exist)","Thoracic spine joint dysfunction","C5 or C6 nerve root irritation (radiculopathy)","Supraspinatus (rotator cuff) referral pain"],"redFlags":["Visible 'winging' of the shoulder blade (sticking out from the back), which could indicate a nerve injury.","Sudden weakness or inability to lift the arm after an injury.","Pain that is present at night and wakes you from sleep.","Any signs of infection like fever, redness, or swelling."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Mid-Back Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, once daily","how":"Lie on your back and place a lacrosse ball on the muscular area between your spine and shoulder blade. Find tender spots and rest on them for 30 seconds, taking deep breaths."},{"name":"Thoracic Extension Over Chair","video":"Thoracic Extension Over Chair","dose":"5-10 breaths, 3-5 times per day","how":"This helps reverse the forward slump that weakens the mid/low traps by improving mid-back mobility."}],"restore":[{"name":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","video":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","dose":"2 x 15, once daily","how":"This is a fantastic exercise to begin activating these weak muscles without load. Focus on the feeling of the muscles between your shoulder blades doing the work."},{"name":"Doorway Pec Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec, twice daily","how":"Stretching the tight chest muscles is essential to allow the mid and lower traps to function properly in a better position."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Face Pull","video":"Face Pull","dose":"3 x 15, every other day","how":"Use a light resistance band. Focus on pulling the band towards your face while externally rotating your shoulders and squeezing your shoulder blades together."},{"name":"Prone 'Y' Raise","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-12, every other day","how":"Lie face down on the floor or a bench. With thumbs pointing up, lift your arms into a 'Y' shape, focusing on squeezing the lower part of your shoulder blades down and back. Start with no weight."}]},"habits":["Practice the 'Standing Posture Reset' throughout the day to engage these muscles.","When sitting, use a small pillow or lumbar roll to support your low back, which helps the upper back stay upright.","Think about keeping your shoulder blades 'in your back pockets.'","Balance your workout routine with at least as many pulling exercises (rows, face pulls) as pushing exercises (push-ups, bench press)."],"expect":"Activating and strengthening these often-neglected muscles takes time. You may notice less fatigue in 2-3 weeks, with significant improvements in strength and posture over 6-12 weeks.","seePro":"Schedule a visit if you have significant shoulder blade 'winging,' if your weakness is impacting your ability to lift your arm, or if pain persists despite 4 weeks of consistent exercise."},"serratus":{"name":"Serratus anterior","origin":"Outer surfaces of the upper 8 or 9 ribs.","insertion":"The front side of the inner border of the scapula (shoulder blade).","action":"Pulls the shoulder blade forward and around the rib cage (protraction), like when you punch or reach forward. It also holds the shoulder blade against the ribs and helps rotate it upward when you lift your arm overhead.","nerve":"Long thoracic nerve (from cervical roots C5, C6, and C7).","symptoms":["A sharp, nagging ache on the side of your chest or ribs, often described as a 'side stitch' that won't go away.","Pain that feels like it's under the bottom tip of your shoulder blade.","Difficulty taking a full, deep breath without a sharp pain or 'catch'.","A feeling of weakness or instability when pushing a door or performing a push-up.","Pain that is worse when lying on the affected side.","A sensation that your shoulder blade is 'winging' or not sitting flat on your back."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel a sharp 'stitch' in your side when you try to take a deep breath or run?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain feel like it's located under the bottom tip of your shoulder blade?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel a 'catch' or sharp pain when you reach forward or push something, like a heavy door?","w":3},{"q":"Is it uncomfortable to lie on the painful side at night?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain get worse with a persistent cough?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Wall Push Test","how":"Stand facing a wall, about arm's length away. Place your hands on the wall at shoulder height and lean forward slightly. Slowly perform a push-up against the wall.","positive":"You feel pain on the side of your ribs, or you see the inside edge of your shoulder blade 'wing' out away from your back as you push."}],"causes":["A sudden increase in strenuous exercise, especially running, swimming, or weightlifting.","Persistent, hard coughing from asthma, bronchitis, or allergies.","Repetitive pushing movements, like in push-ups or bench press with poor form.","Poor posture with shoulders rounded forward for long periods.","Sleeping in an awkward position on your side, compressing the muscle."],"mimics":["Intercostal muscle strain","Rib fracture or stress fracture","Pleurisy (inflammation of the lung lining)","Gallbladder problems (if on the right side)","Heart-related pain (if on the left side)"],"redFlags":["Pain accompanied by severe shortness of breath, dizziness, or sweating (could be a heart attack).","Pain that started after a significant fall or impact to your chest (could be a rib fracture).","Fever, chills, and a productive cough along with the chest pain (could be pneumonia).","Pain that doesn't change at all with movement or position (could be an organ issue)."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Side-Lying Rib Expansion","video":"Serratus Stretch Breathing","dose":"2 x 10 deep breaths, twice daily","how":"Lie on your non-painful side with a small pillow under your waist. Focus on breathing into your painful side, feeling the ribs expand with each slow inhale."},{"name":"Gentle Torso Rotation","video":"Thread The Needle","dose":"2 x 8 reps per side, once daily","how":"Start on your hands and knees. Gently reach one arm under your body, rotating your torso slightly. Move slowly and only within a pain-free range."},{"name":"Diaphragmatic Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once or twice daily","how":"Lie on your back with your feet up on a chair. Focus on breathing deep into your belly, allowing your rib cage to expand gently without forcing it."}],"restore":[{"name":"Scapular Push-Ups","video":"Scap Push Ups","dose":"2 x 10-15 reps, every other day","how":"In a plank or hands-and-knees position, keep your arms straight and pinch your shoulder blades together, then push them apart to round your upper back. The movement should only come from your shoulder blades."},{"name":"Wall Slides","video":"Floor Slide Scapular Retract","dose":"2 x 10 reps, once daily","how":"Stand with your back against a wall. Place your forearms on the wall in a 'goalpost' position. Slowly slide your arms up the wall, keeping your shoulder blades engaged, then return to the start."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Wall Push Up","video":"Wall Push Up","dose":"3 x 8-12 reps, every other day","how":"Stand facing a wall and perform a push-up, focusing on feeling the muscles on the side of your ribs engage as you push away from the wall. Keep your core tight."},{"name":"Single Arm Dumbbell Press","video":null,"dose":"3 x 8 reps per side, every other day","how":"Lie on your back holding a light dumbbell in one hand, arm extended toward the ceiling. Punch the dumbbell straight up, lifting your shoulder blade off the floor. Control the movement back down."}]},"habits":["When sitting, use a chair with good back support to prevent slouching and rounding your shoulders.","Avoid sleeping on the painful side; try sleeping on your back with pillows for support.","Practice deep belly breathing throughout the day to avoid shallow chest breathing patterns.","When lifting, engage your core and push through your legs, not just your arms and shoulders."],"expect":"With consistent gentle movement and strengthening, you should notice a significant decrease in pain within 2-3 weeks. Full recovery and return to strenuous activity may take 4-6 weeks.","seePro":"Book an appointment if the pain is severe, doesn't improve after 1-2 weeks of self-care, or if you have any of the 'Red Flag' symptoms."},"lats":{"name":"Latissimus dorsi","origin":"Spine (from mid-back to the sacrum), lower ribs, and top of the pelvis.","insertion":"The front of the upper arm bone (humerus).","action":"Pulls the arm down, toward the body, and rotates it inward. It's a powerful muscle for pulling movements like pull-ups, swimming, or rowing.","nerve":"Thoracodorsal nerve (from cervical roots C6, C7, and C8).","symptoms":["A persistent, deep ache at the bottom angle of the shoulder blade.","Pain that travels down the back of the arm and forearm, sometimes reaching the ring and little fingers.","A feeling of stiffness or restriction when reaching overhead.","A dull ache in the mid-back, especially after pulling activities.","Difficulty sleeping on either side due to pressure on the muscle.","A 'pinching' sensation in the front of the shoulder when lifting the arm."],"questions":[{"q":"Does the pain feel like a deep ache under your shoulder blade that refers down the back of your arm?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel a 'catch' or stiffness when you try to reach for something on a high shelf?","w":2},{"q":"Have you noticed numbness or tingling in your ring and little finger along with this shoulder and back pain?","w":3},{"q":"Does your mid-back ache after activities like swimming, rowing, or carrying a heavy backpack?","w":2},{"q":"Is it difficult to bring your arm fully overhead without arching your lower back?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Overhead Reach Test","how":"Stand with your back flat against a wall, feet slightly away from the wall. Try to raise both arms straight overhead to touch the wall with your thumbs, without arching your lower back or bending your elbows.","positive":"You feel a strong pull in your mid-back or the back of your armpit, or you are unable to touch the wall without your lower back arching away from it."}],"causes":["Overuse from pulling exercises like pull-ups, rowing, or swimming.","Carrying heavy bags or backpacks consistently on one shoulder.","Prolonged sitting with poor posture, especially reaching forward for a computer mouse.","Vigorous activities like chopping wood or playing tennis.","Sleeping with an arm stretched far overhead."],"mimics":["Rotator cuff tear or tendinopathy","Cervical radiculopathy (pinched nerve in the neck)","Thoracic outlet syndrome","Shoulder impingement syndrome","Teres major muscle strain"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe back pain that is not related to movement.","Pain accompanied by weakness in the legs or changes in bowel/bladder control (seek immediate emergency care).","Numbness or tingling that is constant, severe, and doesn't change with position.","Pain associated with fever, night sweats, or unexplained weight loss."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Child's Pose Lat Stretch","video":"Dynamic Childs Pose","dose":"2 x 30-60 seconds, once daily","how":"From a kneeling position, fold forward and walk your hands out in front, then to one side to feel a stretch along the opposite side of your back. Breathe into the stretch."},{"name":"Foam Roller Release","video":null,"dose":"1-2 minutes per side, once daily","how":"Lie on your side with a foam roller placed under the fleshy part of your back, just behind your armpit. Gently roll up and down, pausing on tender spots."},{"name":"Doorway Hang","video":null,"dose":"2 x 20-30 seconds, twice daily","how":"Grasp the top of a sturdy door frame. Let your knees bend and allow your body weight to gently stretch your lats and shoulders. Keep your core engaged."}],"restore":[{"name":"Quadruped Thoracic Rotation","video":"Thread The Needle","dose":"2 x 10 reps per side, once daily","how":"On hands and knees, reach one arm under your body, then open it up toward the ceiling. Follow your hand with your eyes to encourage rotation through your mid-back."},{"name":"Dynamic Childs Pose","video":"Dynamic Childs Pose","dose":"2 x 12-15 reps, every other day","how":"Sit back toward your heels with arms reaching forward, then walk the hands to one side to lengthen the opposite lat. Breathe into the side ribs."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Dumbbell Row","video":"Dumbbell Row","dose":"3 x 8-12 reps per side, every other day","how":"Hinge at your hips with a flat back. Pull the dumbbell up towards your hip, squeezing your shoulder blade towards your spine. Control the weight on the way down."},{"name":"Lat Pulldown","video":"Lat Pulldown","dose":"3 x 8-12 reps, every other day","how":"Using a machine or band, pull the bar down to your upper chest. Focus on driving your elbows down and back, feeling the contraction in your back muscles."}]},"habits":["Adjust your desk setup so your mouse is close, preventing a constant forward reach.","When carrying bags, switch sides frequently or use a backpack with both straps.","Avoid sleeping with your arm stretched far overhead. Try hugging a pillow instead.","Warm up properly with dynamic stretches before swimming, climbing, or lifting weights."],"expect":"You can expect relief from the sharpest pain within 1-2 weeks of stretching and release work. Building strength and resilience can take 4-8 weeks of consistent exercise.","seePro":"Schedule a visit if your overhead reach doesn't improve, if you have persistent numbness or tingling in your fingers, or if the pain significantly limits your daily activities after 2 weeks."},"intercostals":{"name":"Intercostals","origin":"The lower border of one rib.","insertion":"The upper border of the rib below.","action":"Elevate and depress the ribs to expand and contract the chest cavity, allowing you to breathe.","nerve":"Intercostal nerves (from thoracic spinal nerves T1-T11).","symptoms":["A sharp, stabbing, or aching pain located directly between or along a single rib.","Pain that gets significantly worse with a deep breath, cough, sneeze, or laugh.","Tenderness to the touch in the space between two ribs.","A feeling of tightness or restriction around your chest.","Pain that is very localized to one specific spot, rather than being widespread.","Difficulty twisting or bending your torso without sharp pain."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain sharp and located in a specific spot on your rib cage that you can point to with one finger?","w":3},{"q":"Does taking a deep breath, coughing, or sneezing make the pain dramatically worse?","w":3},{"q":"Can you press on a spot between your ribs and reproduce the exact pain?","w":2},{"q":"Did the pain start after a bout of heavy coughing, a sudden twisting motion, or a direct impact?","w":2},{"q":"Does twisting your upper body trigger the pain?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Palpation Test","how":"Gently press with one or two fingers in the spaces between your ribs in the painful area. Compare the sensation to the same area on the opposite, non-painful side.","positive":"Pressing on a specific spot between the ribs reproduces your sharp pain."}],"causes":["Forceful and repetitive coughing or sneezing.","A sudden, sharp twisting of the torso, such as in golf, tennis, or reaching into the back seat of a car.","A direct blow or impact to the chest or ribs.","Over-stretching the torso, such as reaching for something far to the side.","Lifting an object that is too heavy with poor technique."],"mimics":["Rib fracture","Serratus anterior muscle strain","Pleurisy or other lung conditions","Shingles (herpes zoster), which often follows a rib line","Costochondritis (inflammation of cartilage connecting ribs to the breastbone)"],"redFlags":["Severe difficulty breathing or shortness of breath (call 911).","Pain accompanied by fever, chills, or a cough that produces colored phlegm.","Pain that occurred after a major trauma or fall, with risk of fracture or internal injury.","A visible deformity or a 'crunching' sound in the rib cage."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Gentle Diaphragmatic Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, 2-3 times daily","how":"Lie comfortably on your back. Breathe in gently through your nose, letting your belly rise, but only to the point before pain begins. Exhale slowly and fully."},{"name":"Supported Side Stretch","video":"Serratus Stretch Breathing","dose":"2 x 10 gentle breaths, once daily","how":"Lie on your non-painful side with a pillow or rolled towel under your ribs. Let your body relax over the pillow to create a gentle opening stretch in the painful area as you breathe."}],"restore":[{"name":"Cat-Cow","video":"Cat Cow Desk Variation","dose":"2 x 10 cycles, once daily","how":"On your hands and knees (or seated), gently round your spine up towards the ceiling, then slowly arch it down. Move slowly and within a comfortable, pain-free range of motion."},{"name":"Lying Open Book","video":"Lying Open Book","dose":"2 x 8 reps per side, once daily","how":"Lie on your side with knees bent and arms extended in front of you. Keeping your hips still, rotate your top arm and torso open, as if opening a book. Only go as far as is comfortable."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Core Brace","video":"Core Brace Intro Seated","dose":"3 x 10 second holds, several times a day","how":"Gently tighten your abdominal muscles as if you are about to be poked in the stomach. Hold this gentle brace while breathing normally to help stabilize the rib cage."},{"name":"Dead Bug","video":"Dead Bug","dose":"3 x 8 reps per side, every other day","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent at 90 degrees. Slowly lower one opposite arm and leg towards the floor while keeping your core braced and low back flat. Start with small movements."}]},"habits":["When you need to cough or sneeze, hug a pillow against your chest to brace the area and reduce painful movement.","Avoid sudden twisting motions. When turning, move your feet and let your whole body turn together.","Use proper lifting technique: bend at the knees and hips, and keep objects close to your body.","Sleep on your back or the unaffected side to avoid putting pressure on the strained area."],"expect":"Intercostal strains can be very painful. Expect the sharpest pain to subside in 1-3 weeks, but a dull ache may linger for 4-6 weeks or more. It is important to avoid re-injury during this time.","seePro":"See a professional if the pain is severe and unmanageable, if you have any red flags, or if there is no improvement after 2 weeks of gentle care."},"wrist-extensors":{"name":"Wrist & finger extensors","origin":"Primarily the lateral epicondyle of the humerus (the bony bump on the outside of the elbow).","insertion":"Bones of the wrist and fingers on the back of the hand.","action":"Lift the wrist and fingers up (extension), as if signaling 'stop'. They are also crucial for stabilizing the wrist when you grip something.","nerve":"Radial nerve (from cervical roots C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1).","symptoms":["Aching or burning pain on the outside of the elbow, commonly known as 'tennis elbow'.","Pain that radiates down the top of the forearm towards the wrist.","Weakness in your grip; difficulty holding a coffee cup, turning a doorknob, or shaking hands.","Stiffness in the wrist and elbow, especially in the morning.","Pain that worsens with activities like typing, using a mouse, or lifting with your palm facing down.","A specific point of tenderness when you press on the bony bump on the outside of your elbow."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain centered on the bony bump on the outside of your elbow?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain get worse when you lift something with your palm facing down, like a gallon of milk?","w":3},{"q":"Do you have a weak grip or pain when you try to shake someone's hand or turn a doorknob?","w":2},{"q":"Does your job or hobby involve a lot of repetitive gripping, typing, or wrist movements?","w":1},{"q":"Does stretching your wrist by bending it down (palm first) reproduce the pain in your elbow or forearm?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Resisted Wrist Extension Test","how":"Rest your forearm on a table with your hand hanging off the edge, palm down. Make a fist and lift your wrist up. Use your other hand to gently push down on your fist, resisting the upward movement.","positive":"You feel a sharp pain on the outside of your elbow."}],"causes":["Repetitive motions from typing, using a mouse, or playing racquet sports.","Overuse in manual labor, such as using a screwdriver, painting, or carpentry.","A sudden increase in gripping activities, like a weekend of gardening or moving boxes.","Poor ergonomics at a computer workstation, with the wrist bent upwards.","Underlying weakness in the forearm muscles leading to overload."],"mimics":["Radial tunnel syndrome (compression of the radial nerve)","Cervical radiculopathy (a 'pinched nerve' in the neck referring pain down the arm)","Elbow joint arthritis","Posterior interosseous nerve entrapment"],"redFlags":["Visible swelling, redness, or warmth around the elbow joint (could be infection or inflammatory arthritis).","Inability to move your wrist or fingers at all.","Numbness or tingling that is constant and covers a large area of your hand.","Pain that started after a fall or direct blow to the elbow."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Gentle Wrist Extensor Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 20-30 seconds, twice daily","how":"Extend your arm straight out, palm down. With your other hand, gently bend your wrist downward until you feel a light stretch on top of your forearm. Do not push into sharp pain."},{"name":"Forearm Release with Ball","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, once daily","how":"Place a lacrosse or tennis ball on a table and rest the top of your forearm on it. Gently roll the ball over the muscular part of your forearm, applying gentle, sustained pressure on tender spots."}],"restore":[{"name":"Eccentric Wrist Extension","video":"Eccentric Wrist Drops","dose":"3 x 15 reps, once daily","how":"Sit with your forearm resting on your thigh, palm down. Use your good hand to lift a light weight (e.g., soup can) up into wrist extension. Then, very slowly lower the weight down on its own over 3-4 seconds."},{"name":"Radial Nerve Glide","video":"Full Neuroglide Flow Seated","dose":"2 x 10 slow reps, once daily","how":"Gently perform the arm movements shown in the video for the radial nerve. Move slowly and without pain to help the nerve slide and glide freely through the tissues."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Wrist Extension with Weight","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-15 reps, every other day","how":"Rest your forearm on a table or your thigh, palm down. Holding a light dumbbell, slowly lift your wrist up and then lower it back down with control."},{"name":"Weighted Wrist Circles","video":"Weighted Wrist Circles","dose":"2 x 10 circles each direction, every other day","how":"Holding a very light weight (1-2 lbs), perform slow and controlled circles with your wrist to build stability in all directions."}]},"habits":["Improve your desk ergonomics. Ensure your keyboard and mouse allow your wrist to be in a neutral, straight position.","Take frequent micro-breaks from typing or gripping to stretch your wrists and fingers.","When lifting objects, try to keep your palms facing up or neutral (thumbs up) to reduce strain on the extensors.","Use tools with larger, padded grips to reduce the amount of force your hand needs to generate."],"expect":"Tennis elbow can be stubborn. With consistent eccentric exercises and habit changes, you should feel noticeable improvement in 4-6 weeks. Full resolution can sometimes take several months.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if the pain is severe, doesn't improve with 3-4 weeks of consistent rehab, or if you experience significant loss of grip strength."},"wrist-flexors":{"name":"Wrist & finger flexors","origin":"Primarily the medial epicondyle of the humerus (the bony bump on the inside of the elbow).","insertion":"Bones of the wrist and fingers on the palm side of the hand.","action":"Bend the wrist down and close the fingers to make a fist (flexion). They are the primary muscles for gripping.","nerve":"Median and Ulnar nerves (from cervical roots C6, C7, C8, and T1).","symptoms":["Aching or burning pain on the inside of the elbow, commonly known as 'golfer's elbow'.","Pain that radiates down the palm-side of the forearm to the wrist.","Stiffness when trying to fully straighten the elbow or extend the wrist.","Pain when making a fist, gripping a tool, or carrying a heavy object like a suitcase.","A specific point of tenderness when you press on the bony bump on the inside of your elbow.","Occasionally, numbness or tingling in the ring and little fingers if the nearby ulnar nerve is irritated."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain centered on the bony bump on the inside of your elbow?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain get worse when you grip something tightly or carry a heavy bag with your arm straight?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel pain when you make a tight fist or when using tools like a hammer or racquet?","w":2},{"q":"Does stretching your wrist by bending it backwards (palm up) reproduce the pain in your inner elbow or forearm?","w":2},{"q":"Does your pain get worse after activities like rock climbing, throwing a ball, or extensive hammering?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Resisted Wrist Flexion Test","how":"Rest your forearm on a table with your hand hanging off the edge, palm facing up. Make a fist and bend your wrist upward. Use your other hand to gently push down on your fist, resisting the movement.","positive":"You feel pain on the inside of your elbow."}],"causes":["Repetitive or forceful gripping, common in golf, rock climbing, and throwing sports.","Manual labor involving hammering, carrying heavy objects (like a suitcase), or using a chainsaw.","A sudden increase in activity, like a long day of yard work or home repairs.","Poor technique in sports or at work that overloads the forearm muscles.","Typing for long hours with wrists bent or resting on a hard surface."],"mimics":["Ulnar nerve entrapment (Cubital Tunnel Syndrome)","Cervical radiculopathy (a 'pinched nerve' in the neck)","Elbow joint arthritis or ulnar collateral ligament sprain","Thoracic Outlet Syndrome"],"redFlags":["Visible swelling, redness, or warmth around the inner elbow.","Constant, severe numbness or a 'clawing' posture of the ring and little fingers.","Inability to bend your wrist or make a fist.","Pain that started after a sudden 'pop' or traumatic injury to the elbow."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Gentle Wrist Flexor Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 20-30 seconds, twice daily","how":"Extend your arm straight out, palm up. With your other hand, gently bend your wrist downward until you feel a light stretch on the inside of your forearm. Keep your elbow straight."},{"name":"Forearm Release with Ball","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, once daily","how":"Place a lacrosse or tennis ball on a table and rest the palm-side of your forearm on it. Gently roll the ball over the muscular part of your forearm, applying sustained pressure to tender spots."}],"restore":[{"name":"Eccentric Wrist Flexion","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15 reps, once daily","how":"Sit with your forearm resting on your thigh, palm up. Use your good hand to lift a light weight (e.g., soup can) up into wrist flexion. Then, very slowly lower the weight down on its own over 3-4 seconds."},{"name":"Eccentric Wrist Pronation","video":"Eccentric Wrist Pronator","dose":"3 x 10 reps, once daily","how":"Hold a light dumbbell or hammer handle vertically. Use your other hand to rotate it so the weight is on the outside. Slowly and with control, let the weight rotate your forearm inward."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Wrist Flexion with Weight","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-15 reps, every other day","how":"Rest your forearm on a table or your thigh, palm up. Holding a light dumbbell, slowly curl your wrist up and then lower it back down with control."},{"name":"Farmer Carry","video":"Farmer Carry","dose":"3 x 30 second walks, every other day","how":"Hold a moderate to heavy weight in each hand with your arms at your sides. Walk with tall posture, focusing on your grip strength to hold the weights without letting them slip."}]},"habits":["When carrying groceries or a briefcase, try to keep your wrist straight, not bent upwards.","Use ergonomic tools with thicker, padded handles to distribute grip force more evenly.","Take regular breaks from repetitive gripping tasks to stretch your hands and forearms.","Ensure your workstation allows for a neutral wrist posture while typing, avoiding resting your wrists on sharp edges."],"expect":"Similar to tennis elbow, golfer's elbow requires patience. You should notice a decrease in pain with daily activities in 3-4 weeks, but building full strength and returning to demanding activities can take 6-12 weeks.","seePro":"Schedule an appointment if you have persistent numbness in your fingers, if the pain is not improving after 3-4 weeks of consistent rehab, or if it significantly impacts your ability to work or perform daily tasks."},"brachioradialis":{"origin":"The lower, outer edge of the upper arm bone (humerus).","insertion":"The bony point on the thumb-side of the wrist (styloid process of the radius).","action":"Bends the elbow, especially with the forearm in a neutral, thumb-up position. Key movements include hammering, shaking hands, or lifting a grocery bag.","nerve":"Radial nerve (from neck roots C5, C6, C7).","symptoms":["Aching pain on the top and outside of the forearm, near the elbow.","Pain that can travel down the forearm to the web of the thumb.","A feeling of weakness when gripping or lifting objects.","Sharp pain when turning a doorknob, using a screwdriver, or typing.","The 'meaty' part of the top of your forearm feels tender to the touch.","Difficulty holding a coffee mug or other heavy items."],"questions":[{"q":"Does your pain feel worse when you lift something with your palm facing sideways, like carrying a grocery bag?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel an ache on the outside of your elbow that seems to travel down toward your thumb?","w":3},{"q":"Is it painful to shake hands firmly or turn a key in a lock?","w":2},{"q":"Does your grip feel weak, making it hard to hold a coffee mug or a hammer?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain located more on the muscular part of your forearm, rather than directly on the bony point of the elbow?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Resisted Elbow Flexion Test","how":"Rest your forearm on a table, thumb pointing to the ceiling. Use your other hand to gently press down on your wrist while you try to bend your elbow.","positive":"Pain or a feeling of weakness in the muscle on top of your forearm near the elbow."}],"causes":["Repetitive gripping activities like hammering, using tools, or extensive gardening.","Carrying heavy objects with straight arms, such as suitcases or shopping bags.","Sports involving forceful wrist and elbow movements like tennis, rock climbing, or rowing.","Typing for long hours with poor wrist posture.","A sudden, forceful pull on the arm, like catching a falling object."],"mimics":["Lateral epicondylitis ('Tennis Elbow')","De Quervain's tenosynovitis (thumb tendon pain)","Radial nerve entrapment","C6 or C7 cervical radiculopathy (pinched nerve in the neck)"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe swelling and bruising after an injury.","Inability to move your wrist or elbow through their full range of motion.","Numbness or tingling that is constant and does not change with position.","A visible deformity in the arm or wrist after a fall."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Brachioradialis Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"3 x 30-60 sec, daily","how":"Sit with your forearm resting on a table, palm down. Use the thumb of your other hand to apply firm, sustained pressure to tender spots in the muscle belly on top of your forearm."},{"name":"Gentle Wrist Circles","video":"Weighted Wrist Circles","dose":"2 x 10 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Without any weight, gently rotate your wrist in slow, controlled circles. Focus on a pain-free range of motion to improve joint mobility and blood flow."},{"name":"Diaphragmatic Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, 1-2 times daily","how":"Lie on your back with feet up on a chair to relax the nervous system. Breathe in through your nose, letting your belly rise, and exhale slowly through your mouth."}],"restore":[{"name":"Brachioradialis Stretch","video":null,"dose":"3 x 30 sec, daily","how":"Extend your arm straight out, palm down. Gently use your other hand to bend your wrist downward, then slightly turn your hand inward (like pouring a pitcher) to feel a stretch on top of the forearm."},{"name":"Eccentric Wrist Extension","video":"Eccentric Wrist Drops","dose":"3 x 15, every other day","how":"Rest your forearm on your thigh, palm down, holding a very light weight. Use your other hand to lift the weight up, then slowly lower it down over 3-5 seconds."},{"name":"Radial Nerve Glide","video":"Neck Neuroglide Seated","dose":"1 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Follow the video cues for the radial nerve portion: make a fist, bend your wrist, turn your arm in, and gently reach back while tilting your head away. Move slowly and without pain."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Hammer Curls","video":"Bicep Curl","dose":"3 x 10-12, every other day","how":"Hold a dumbbell in each hand with your palms facing each other (thumb up). Bend your elbows to lift the weights toward your shoulders, keeping your wrists straight. Lower slowly."},{"name":"Farmer Carry","video":"Farmer Carry","dose":"3 x 30 sec walks, every other day","how":"Hold a moderately heavy weight in each hand at your sides. Walk with tall posture, keeping your shoulders back and down, engaging your grip and core."}]},"habits":["Take frequent breaks from typing or manual work to stretch your wrists and forearms.","Use ergonomic tools with thicker handles to reduce grip force.","Lift heavy objects with palms facing up or towards each other, and keep them close to your body.","Adjust your desk and chair height to keep wrists in a neutral position. See our 'Desk Setup Tutorial'."],"expect":"With consistent adherence to the protocol, most people feel a significant reduction in pain within 2-4 weeks. Full recovery and strength may take 6-8 weeks.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if your pain is severe, doesn't improve after 2 weeks of self-care, or if you experience significant numbness or weakness."},"supinator":{"origin":"The outer elbow bone (lateral epicondyle of the humerus) and nearby forearm bone (ulna).","insertion":"The upper third of the other forearm bone (radius).","action":"Turns the forearm so the palm faces up (supination). Key movements include turning a doorknob, using a screwdriver, or accepting change in your hand.","nerve":"Radial nerve (from neck roots C6, C7).","symptoms":["A deep, aching pain at the outer elbow, often mistaken for 'tennis elbow'.","Pain that may radiate down the back of the forearm to the web of the thumb.","Weakness or sharp pain when trying to turn your palm up against resistance.","Aching at rest, especially after a day of heavy hand use.","Tenderness when you press deeply into the muscles on the top of your forearm.","Pain that persists even when you are not actively gripping anything."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel a deep ache on the outside of your elbow, even when you're not gripping anything?","w":3},{"q":"Is it painful to turn a doorknob or use a screwdriver, specifically the motion of turning your palm upwards?","w":3},{"q":"Have you been treated for 'tennis elbow' but the pain just won't go away?","w":2},{"q":"Does pressing deeply into the muscle on the top of your forearm, a few inches below the elbow, reproduce your specific pain?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain a constant, deep ache rather than a sharp pain that only happens when you lift?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Resisted Supination Test","how":"Rest your elbow on a table, bent to 90 degrees with your palm facing down. Use your other hand to resist as you try to turn your palm up towards the ceiling.","positive":"Pain deep in the outer elbow or forearm."}],"causes":["Repetitive turning motions, such as using tools or playing racquet sports with a lot of spin.","Carrying heavy items with bent elbows and palms up, like a waiter's tray.","A fall onto an outstretched hand.","Prolonged typing on a non-ergonomic keyboard that forces forearm rotation.","Forcefully tightening screws or jar lids."],"mimics":["Lateral epicondylitis ('Tennis Elbow')","Radial tunnel syndrome (nerve compression)","C6 or C7 cervical radiculopathy (pinched nerve in the neck)","Elbow joint arthritis"],"redFlags":["Inability to turn your palm up or down.","Constant, severe pins and needles or numbness in the hand.","Visible swelling or deformity around the elbow after an injury.","Pain that wakes you from sleep and is not relieved by changing position."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Supinator Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"3 x 30-60 sec, daily","how":"Rest your forearm on a table. Use the fingers or thumb of your other hand to find and press on tender spots deep in the muscle on the top of your forearm, just below the elbow."},{"name":"Gentle Palm Up/Palm Down","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15 reps, twice daily","how":"Sit with your elbow bent at 90 degrees and resting at your side. Slowly rotate your forearm to turn your palm up, then slowly turn it back down. Stay in a pain-free range."},{"name":"Box Breathing","video":"Box Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, 1-2 times daily","how":"Follow the video cues to inhale for 4 seconds, hold for 4, exhale for 4, and hold for 4. This helps calm the nervous system to reduce muscle tension."}],"restore":[{"name":"Pronator Stretch","video":null,"dose":"3 x 30 sec, daily","how":"Hold your arm out, palm up. Gently use your other hand to bend your wrist down, feeling a stretch on the top/inside of your forearm. Keep your elbow straight."},{"name":"Eccentric Wrist Pronation","video":"Eccentric Wrist Pronator","dose":"3 x 15, every other day","how":"Rest your forearm on your thigh, palm up, holding a light weight. Use your other hand to help turn your palm down, then slowly control the motion as the weight turns your palm back up."},{"name":"Radial Nerve Glide","video":"Neck Neuroglide Seated","dose":"1 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Perform the radial nerve glide as shown in the video. This helps the nerve that passes through the supinator muscle to move more freely."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Weighted Supination","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10-12, every other day","how":"Hold a light dumbbell or hammer handle vertically. Rest your forearm on a table. Slowly rotate the weight so your palm turns up, then control it back to the start."},{"name":"Bicep Curl","video":"Bicep Curl","dose":"3 x 10-12, every other day","how":"Perform standard bicep curls, focusing on the final part of the movement where you fully turn your palm up at the top. This strengthens the biceps, a primary supinator."}]},"habits":["Use power tools instead of manual screwdrivers for repetitive tasks.","Keep your wrists in a neutral (handshake) position when typing or using a mouse.","Open tight jars by using a gripper or tapping the lid to break the seal, rather than using maximum force.","Carry objects with palms facing in or down when possible."],"expect":"Pain relief often begins within 1-2 weeks of consistent release and stretching. Building strength to prevent recurrence can take 4-6 weeks.","seePro":"If you have persistent pain despite 2-3 weeks of this program, or if you have significant weakness or numbness, schedule an appointment for a full evaluation."},"ql":{"origin":"The top, back border of the hip bone (posterior iliac crest).","insertion":"The lowest rib (12th rib) and the sides of the upper four lumbar vertebrae.","action":"Hikes the hip up on one side, side-bends the trunk, and stabilizes the pelvis and low back when walking or lifting.","nerve":"Thoracic (T12) and lumbar (L1-L4) spinal nerves.","symptoms":["A deep, nagging ache in the low back, often worse on one side.","Sharp, stabbing pain when trying to stand up straight, roll over in bed, or cough.","Pain that refers over the top of the buttock (SI joint area) and sometimes to the front of the hip.","A feeling of being 'stuck' or unable to stand up straight after sitting for a while.","Pain that prevents you from finding a comfortable position in bed.","A feeling of intense vulnerability in the low back."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you get a sharp, 'catching' pain in your low back when you try to roll over in bed or get out of a car?","w":3},{"q":"Does coughing, sneezing, or taking a deep breath send a sharp pain into your low back or flank?","w":3},{"q":"Is it difficult to stand up completely straight after you've been sitting for a while?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain feel very deep, like it's right next to your spine, but also spreads out over your buttock or hip?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel pain when you stand or walk, but find some relief by leaning on a shopping cart or countertop?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Hip Hike Test","how":"Stand in front of a mirror. Try to lift one side of your pelvis straight up toward your ribs without bending your knee or leaning your torso.","positive":"Sharp pain in the low back on the side you are hiking, or an inability to perform the movement smoothly."}],"causes":["Sitting for long periods, especially with poor posture or in a slumped position.","Lifting a heavy object, especially with a twisting motion.","A fall or direct blow to the low back, hip, or buttock.","Standing or walking on uneven surfaces for extended periods.","A true or functional leg length discrepancy.","Weak gluteal and core muscles, forcing the QL to overwork."],"mimics":["Lumbar disc herniation","Sacroiliac (SI) joint dysfunction","Kidney pain or kidney stone (check for fever, nausea, or changes in urination)","Lumbar facet syndrome","Gluteus medius trigger points"],"redFlags":["Pain accompanied by fever, chills, or unexplained weight loss.","Loss of bowel or bladder control (this is a medical emergency).","Numbness or weakness in the legs that is severe or getting progressively worse.","Back pain that is constant, severe, and not relieved by any change in position."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"QL Pressure Release (Ball)","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, daily","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Place a lacrosse ball in the soft tissue of your low back, between your ribs and hip bone, just to the side of the spine. Gently rock your hips side to side."},{"name":"Pillow Squeeze Reset","video":"Pillow Belt Reset","dose":"1 x 5 reps of 5 sec holds, as needed","how":"Follow the video instructions to gently reset the pelvis. This can provide immediate, temporary relief by relaxing deep muscles around the hips and low back."},{"name":"Dynamic Child's Pose","video":"Dynamic Childs Pose","dose":"1 x 10 rocks, twice daily","how":"From hands and knees, sit your hips back towards your heels. Gently rock side to side to feel a stretch in your low back and lats. Breathe deeply into your back."}],"restore":[{"name":"QL Side Bend Stretch","video":"Sit Turn Lean","dose":"3 x 30 sec per side, daily","how":"Sit tall in a chair. Reach one arm overhead and gently lean to the opposite side, feeling a stretch in your flank and low back. Keep both sit bones on the chair."},{"name":"Core Brace Introduction","video":"Core Brace Intro Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps of 10 sec holds, daily","how":"Learning to gently engage your deep core muscles provides stability for the lumbar spine, allowing the overworked QL to relax."},{"name":"Quadruped T-Spine Rotation","video":"Quadruped T-Spine Rotation","dose":"2 x 10 per side, daily","how":"This mobilizes the mid-back, which can reduce the strain on the low back and QL. Follow the video cues for smooth, controlled rotation."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Side Plank","video":"Dynamic Plank","dose":"3 x 30-60 sec hold per side, every other day","how":"Lie on your side, propped on your elbow. Lift your hips off the floor, creating a straight line from head to feet. Start from your knees if a full plank is too difficult."},{"name":"One-Sided Farmer Carry","video":"Farmer Carry","dose":"3 x 20-30 steps per side, every other day","how":"Hold a single heavy weight in one hand. Walk with tall posture, fighting the urge to lean away from the weight. This directly challenges the opposite QL to stabilize the pelvis."},{"name":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 10-12 per side, every other day","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Squeeze your glutes to lift your hips. Progressing to the single-leg version improves pelvic stability, reducing strain on the QL."}]},"habits":["Avoid sitting for more than 30 minutes at a time. Use the 'Walk Break Protocol Seated'.","When standing up from a chair, scoot to the edge, place feet wide, and hinge at your hips, keeping your back straight.","Sleep on your side with a pillow between your knees to keep your pelvis aligned.","When lifting, face the object, brace your core, and lift with your legs, not your back."],"expect":"Acute QL pain can often be calmed within a few days. Consistent mobility and strengthening can lead to significant improvement in 2-4 weeks, with a focus on preventing recurrence over the next 2-3 months.","seePro":"If you have any red flag symptoms, or if the pain is severe and not improving with 1-2 weeks of care, book an appointment. Also see us if you suspect a leg length discrepancy."},"erectors":{"origin":"Broadly from the sacrum, hip bones (iliac crests), and lower vertebrae.","insertion":"All along the ribs and vertebrae, up to the base of the skull.","action":"Straightens the back from a bent-over position and side-bends the trunk. These are the primary muscles that hold you upright against gravity.","nerve":"Dorsal rami of the spinal nerves at each level.","symptoms":["Aching, 'ropey' or tight pain on one or both sides of the spine.","Pain that feels worse after prolonged sitting or standing in one position.","Stiffness and difficulty bending forward, especially in the morning.","Pain that may refer down into the buttock or wrap around the side toward the lower ribs.","A feeling that your back muscles are in constant spasm or are unable to relax.","Relief when lying down or bending forward."],"questions":[{"q":"Does your back feel achy and stiff after sitting at a desk or driving for a long time?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel significant relief when you lie down and put your feet up, or when you bend forward to touch your toes?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain located in the thick columns of muscle running up your back, rather than a single point right on the spine?","w":3},{"q":"Does your back feel 'locked up' or stiff when you try to stand up straight after bending over?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel a band of pain across your low back that sometimes wraps around your side like a belt?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Forward Fold Test","how":"Stand with feet hip-width apart. Slowly bend forward at the hips, letting your arms and head hang down. Do not force the movement.","positive":"A feeling of intense tightness or pulling pain along the sides of the spine, which may limit how far you can bend."}],"causes":["Prolonged sitting in a slouched posture ('C-shape' spine).","Lifting heavy objects improperly, using the back instead of the legs.","Repetitive bending and twisting motions, such as gardening or construction work.","Weak core and gluteal muscles, which forces the back muscles to overwork as stabilizers.","Sleeping on a soft, unsupportive mattress.","Anterior pelvic tilt posture."],"mimics":["Lumbar disc herniation","Lumbar facet joint arthritis","Quadratus lumborum (QL) pain","Kidney infection (pyelonephritis)","Ankylosing spondylitis (inflammatory arthritis)"],"redFlags":["Back pain following a significant trauma like a car accident or fall.","Loss of bowel or bladder control.","Unexplained fever or night sweats with back pain.","Progressive weakness or numbness in the legs."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Mid-Back Release (Foam Roller)","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, daily","how":"Lie on a foam roller placed horizontally under your mid-back. Support your head. Use your legs to gently roll from your mid-back to the top of your hips. Avoid rolling on the sensitive low back area."},{"name":"Cat-Cow Stretch","video":"Cat Cow Desk Variation","dose":"2 x 10 cycles, twice daily","how":"On hands and knees (or seated), gently round your spine up toward the ceiling (cat), then slowly arch it down (cow). This mobilizes the spine and gently stretches the erector muscles."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, daily","how":"Lying on your back with feet on a chair allows the erector spinae to fully relax. Focus on deep belly breaths to calm the nervous system and reduce muscle guarding."}],"restore":[{"name":"Pike Forward Fold","video":"Pike Forward Fold","dose":"3 x 30-60 sec, daily","how":"Sit on the floor with legs straight. Hinge forward from your hips, keeping your back as straight as possible. This provides a long, sustained stretch for the entire back line."},{"name":"Dead Bug","video":"Dead Bug","dose":"3 x 10 reps (5 per side), daily","how":"This exercise teaches you to activate your deep core stabilizers while keeping your back relaxed and stable against the floor. It's foundational for reducing erector overactivity."},{"name":"Dowel Hip Hinge","video":"Dowel Hip Hinge","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Learn the fundamental pattern of bending from the hips, not the waist. This retrains your movement to use your glutes and hamstrings, sparing your back."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Bird-Dog","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"From hands and knees, extend one arm forward and the opposite leg back, keeping your core tight and back flat. This builds endurance in the erectors in a safe, controlled way."},{"name":"Romanian Deadlift (RDL)","video":"Romanian Deadlift","dose":"3 x 8-10, every other day","how":"Using light weight, practice the hip hinge pattern under load. This strengthens the entire posterior chain (glutes, hamstrings, and erectors) together."},{"name":"Row","video":"Row","dose":"3 x 10-12, every other day","how":"Strengthening the upper back muscles (lats, rhomboids) helps improve posture, which reduces the chronic strain on the lumbar erectors."}]},"habits":["Use a lumbar roll in your car and office chair to maintain the natural curve of your low back.","When standing for long periods, place one foot on a small stool or rail to tilt your pelvis and relax your low back.","Set a timer to stand up and perform a 'Standing Posture Reset' every 30 minutes.","Sleep on a firm mattress, either on your back with a pillow under your knees or on your side with a pillow between them."],"expect":"Stiffness and pain often decrease within 1-2 weeks with gentle mobility and postural changes. Building robust strength to protect your back is a longer process, typically taking 6-12 weeks.","seePro":"Schedule a visit if pain is severe, accompanied by nerve symptoms (shooting pain, numbness), or does not improve after 2-3 weeks of consistent effort."},"multifidus":{"origin":"Back of the sacrum and the transverse processes of vertebrae from the low back to the neck.","insertion":"The spinous processes (the bony bumps in the middle of your back) of the vertebrae 2-4 levels above the origin.","action":"These small, deep muscles provide fine-tuned stability to each individual joint of the spine, preventing small, painful movements.","nerve":"Dorsal rami of the spinal nerves at each level.","symptoms":["A deep, sharp, or burning pain felt right next to the spine.","Pain is often pinpoint and you can put a finger directly on it.","Can refer pain down to the sacrum, tailbone, or occasionally into the abdomen.","A feeling of instability or 'giving way' in the low back.","Pain is often worse when bending forward, twisting, or returning to upright from a bent position.","Often persists as a chronic low-grade ache after an acute back injury has healed."],"questions":[{"q":"Can you point with one finger to a specific, deep spot of pain right next to your spine?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel a deep ache near your tailbone or sacrum that doesn't seem to be in the buttock muscles?","w":3},{"q":"Does your back feel weak or like it might 'give out' with certain movements?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain worse when you bend forward to put on your socks and shoes?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain worse when sitting on a soft couch compared to a firm chair?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Prone Leg Lift Test","how":"Lie face down. While keeping your pelvis flat on the floor, try to lift one leg straight up just an inch or two. Have a partner place their fingers on the muscles right next to your low back spine.","positive":"You feel a sharp, localized pain, or your partner feels the muscle on the lifting side contract late or not at all compared to the opposite side."}],"causes":["A sudden twist or awkward lift that injures the deep spinal structures.","Prolonged sitting, which allows the multifidus muscles to weaken and atrophy.","Chronic poor posture, especially slouching.","Following a lumbar disc injury, as pain can inhibit these muscles from firing correctly.","Repetitive flexion activities like sit-ups or gardening."],"mimics":["Lumbar facet joint syndrome (very closely related and often co-existing)","Sacroiliac (SI) joint dysfunction","Coccydynia (tailbone pain)","Lumbar disc herniation"],"redFlags":["Saddle anesthesia (numbness in the groin/inner thigh area).","Loss of bowel or bladder control.","Unexplained, progressive weakness in the legs.","Pain that is severe, constant, and worse at night."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Positional Release","video":null,"dose":"5-10 minutes, as needed","how":"Lie on your back with several pillows under your knees to put your low back in a comfortable, neutral position. The goal is to find a position of maximum ease to let the deep muscles relax."},{"name":"Pillow Squeeze Pelvic Reset","video":"Pillow Belt Reset","dose":"1 x 5 reps of 5 sec holds, 1-2 times daily","how":"This gentle isometric exercise can help reset the relationship between the pelvis and sacrum, calming the deep muscles that attach there, including the multifidus."},{"name":"Cat-Cow Gentle Mobilization","video":"Cat Cow Desk Variation","dose":"2 x 10 cycles, daily","how":"Perform this movement very slowly and within a small, pain-free range. The goal is gentle movement to reduce stiffness, not a deep stretch."}],"restore":[{"name":"Dead Bug","video":"Dead Bug","dose":"3 x 10 reps (5 per side), daily","how":"This is a key exercise for multifidus retraining. Focus on keeping your low back absolutely still as you move your limbs. Pressing your back gently into the floor helps activate the deep stabilizers."},{"name":"Quadruped Glute Kick","video":"Quadruped Glute Kick","dose":"3 x 15 per side, every other day","how":"From hands and knees, extend one leg back by squeezing your glute. The challenge is to keep your spine perfectly still, which requires the multifidus to work hard as a stabilizer."},{"name":"Core Brace Introduction","video":"Core Brace Intro Seated","dose":"2 x 10 reps of 10 sec holds, daily","how":"Practice co-contracting your deep abdominal and back muscles. Imagine gently tightening a corset around your midsection. This is the foundation of spinal stability."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Bird-Dog","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"From hands and knees, extend the opposite arm and leg while keeping your torso completely level. This directly challenges the multifidus to prevent rotation."},{"name":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 10-12 per side, every other day","how":"Lifting your hips requires the multifidus to stabilize the spine. The single-leg version increases the stability demand significantly."},{"name":"Rack Pull","video":"Rack Pull","dose":"3 x 8-10, every other day","how":"This is a limited range-of-motion deadlift. Set the bar on blocks so you only move it from just below the knees to standing. This strengthens the back in a safe, upright posture."}]},"habits":["Always sit with your feet flat on the floor and a small support in the curve of your low back.","When you bend, hinge at your hips, not your waist. Practice with the 'Dowel Hip Hinge'.","Engage your core brace ('Core Brace Intro Seated') before lifting anything, even if it's light.","Avoid sitting on couches or chairs that are too soft or deep, as this encourages slouching."],"expect":"Because these are deep stabilizing muscles, retraining them takes time and focus. You may feel less 'instability' in 2-4 weeks, but building true endurance and automatic activation can take 3-6 months of consistent practice.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if you have any red flag symptoms, if the pain is the result of a specific injury, or if you are not making steady progress after 4 weeks. This type of pain often benefits from hands-on assessment."},"psoas":{"origin":"Sides of the lower thoracic and lumbar vertebrae (T12-L5) and their discs.","insertion":"Lesser trochanter of the femur (a small knob on the inside of the upper thigh bone).","action":"A powerful hip flexor, it lifts your thigh toward your chest. It also helps stabilize your lower back.","nerve":"Anterior rami of lumbar nerves L1, L2, and L3.","symptoms":["A deep, vertical band of aching pain along one or both sides of your lower back.","Pain that refers to the front of your thigh and groin area.","Difficulty and pain when trying to stand up straight after sitting for a while.","A feeling of a 'catch' or 'click' deep in the front of the hip.","Pain that gets worse with prolonged sitting or walking.","Disrupted sleep, especially when trying to lie flat on your back."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you feel a deep ache running vertically up your low back, right next to the spine?","w":3},{"q":"Is it difficult or painful to stand up fully straight after sitting for more than 20-30 minutes?","w":3},{"q":"Does your pain also show up in the front of your thigh or groin?","w":2},{"q":"Does lying flat on your back feel uncomfortable or increase your back ache?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel better when you bring your knees toward your chest while lying down?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Thomas Test (Modified)","how":"Sit on the edge of a sturdy bed or table. Lie back, pulling both knees to your chest. Hold one knee tightly and let the other leg hang down. Your low back must stay flat on the surface.","positive":"If the hanging thigh doesn't touch the surface or if the knee straightens out on its own, your psoas is likely tight."}],"causes":["Prolonged sitting with hips flexed (e.g., desk work, driving).","Sleeping in a fetal position.","Repetitive hip flexion activities like running, cycling, or sit-ups.","A sudden fall or slip that forces the leg back.","Stress and shallow breathing patterns, as the psoas is connected to the diaphragm."],"mimics":["Lumbar disc herniation","Hip joint arthritis or labral tear","Appendicitis (on the right side)","Kidney stone pain","Inguinal hernia"],"redFlags":["Pain accompanied by fever, nausea, or unexplained weight loss.","Loss of bowel or bladder control.","Numbness or tingling in the groin or 'saddle' area.","Severe, unrelenting abdominal or back pain that doesn't change with position."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Psoas Release (Ball)","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, once daily","how":"Lie on your stomach. Place a soft ball (4-6 inches) just inside your hip bone. Gently sink your weight onto the ball, breathing deeply. Avoid pulsing or painful pressure."},{"name":"Constructive Rest Position","video":null,"dose":"5-10 minutes, once or twice daily","how":"Lie on your back with your knees bent and feet flat on the floor, hip-width apart. Rest your hands on your belly and focus on deep, diaphragmatic breathing."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once daily","how":"Lie on your back with your lower legs on a chair or box, so your hips and knees are at 90 degrees. Breathe deeply into your belly and low back."}],"restore":[{"name":"Couch Stretch","video":"Couch Stretch","dose":"2 x 30-60 sec per side, daily","how":"Kneel in a lunge position with your back shin against a wall or couch. Keep your torso upright and gently squeeze your glute to feel a stretch in the front of the hip."},{"name":"Hip Neuroglide","video":"Hip Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps per side, daily","how":"Follow the video to gently mobilize the nerves that run through the front of the hip, improving their ability to slide and glide."},{"name":"Quadruped Glute Kick","video":"Quadruped Glute Kick","dose":"2 x 15 reps per side, every other day","how":"On all fours, engage your core and lift one leg back, squeezing the glute at the top. This activates the opposing muscle to help the psoas relax."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Dead Bug","video":"Dead Bug","dose":"3 x 10 reps (alternating), every other day","how":"Lie on your back with arms and legs in the air. Lower the opposite arm and leg slowly while keeping your low back flat on the floor. This builds core stability."},{"name":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 12 reps per side, every other day","how":"Lie on your back with one foot flat on the floor and the other leg extended. Drive through your heel to lift your hips, squeezing the glute."},{"name":"Step Up","video":"Step Up","dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"Step onto a box or stair, driving through the heel of your front foot. Control the movement on the way down. This strengthens the glutes and quads."}]},"habits":["Use a standing desk or take frequent breaks from sitting every 30 minutes.","When sitting, ensure your hips are at or slightly higher than your knees.","Avoid sleeping curled up in a tight fetal position; try sleeping on your back with a pillow under your knees.","Practice deep belly breathing throughout the day to relax the diaphragm and psoas."],"expect":"With consistent adherence to the protocol, most people feel a significant reduction in pain and stiffness within 2-4 weeks. Full restoration of strength and function may take 6-8 weeks.","seePro":"Book an appointment if your pain is severe, doesn't improve after 2 weeks of self-care, or is accompanied by any of the 'Red Flags'."},"iliacus":{"origin":"Iliac fossa (the inner surface of the pelvic bone).","insertion":"Lesser trochanter of the femur (shares a tendon with the psoas).","action":"Works with the psoas as a primary hip flexor, lifting the thigh toward the chest. Also helps stabilize the pelvis.","nerve":"Femoral nerve (L2, L3).","symptoms":["A persistent ache deep in the groin or front pocket area.","Pain that refers to the front of the thigh.","A tender spot on the front of the hip, just inside the bony point (ASIS).","Pain that can also refer to the low back or upper buttock area.","Stiffness and pain when getting up from a chair, especially after a long car ride."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your primary pain located deep in your groin, like in your front pocket?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel a very tender spot if you press your fingers just inside the bony point at the front of your hip?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain get worse when you are sitting for a long time, especially while driving?","w":2},{"q":"Does your pain sometimes feel like it's in your low back or high in your glute, but the main spot is in the front?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel a 'catching' sensation in the front of your hip when you stand up?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Iliacus Palpation","how":"Lie on your back with your knees bent. Find the bony point at the front of your hip (ASIS). Place your fingertips just inside this bone and gently curl them into your abdomen as you exhale. Slowly straighten your leg.","positive":"A sharp, localized tenderness or reproduction of your familiar groin pain indicates iliacus involvement."}],"causes":["Prolonged sitting, which keeps the muscle in a shortened position.","Activities involving repetitive hip flexion, like running, cycling, or kicking.","Driving for long periods, especially with the gas pedal foot.","Wearing a heavy tool belt that presses on the front of the hips.","A history of falls or direct impact to the front of the pelvis."],"mimics":["Hip joint impingement (FAI) or labral tear","Inguinal hernia","Sports hernia (athletic pubalgia)","Femoral nerve entrapment","Hip flexor strain (tendonitis)"],"redFlags":["A palpable lump or bulge in the groin area.","Numbness, tingling, or weakness in the front of the thigh.","Pain accompanied by fever, night sweats, or unexplained weight loss.","Inability to bear weight on the leg."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Iliacus Release (Ball)","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, once daily","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Place a small, soft ball just inside your hip bone. Let the ball sink in as you breathe deeply. Avoid any pulsing or sharp pain."},{"name":"Dynamic Childs Pose","video":"Dynamic Childs Pose","dose":"2 x 10 rocks, twice daily","how":"From a child's pose position, gently rock your hips side to side to create a mild stretch and movement in the deep hip flexors and low back."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once daily","how":"Lie on your back with your lower legs on a chair or box, so your hips and knees are at 90 degrees. This position helps the iliacus and psoas to relax."}],"restore":[{"name":"Couch Stretch","video":"Couch Stretch","dose":"2 x 30-60 sec per side, daily","how":"This stretch targets the entire iliopsoas complex. Focus on keeping your torso upright and squeezing the glute of the back leg to deepen the stretch."},{"name":"Hip Neuroglide","video":"Hip Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps per side, daily","how":"Gently mobilize the femoral nerve, which passes near the iliacus, to ensure it can move freely and reduce irritation."},{"name":"Glute Bridge","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Squeeze your glutes and lift your hips toward the ceiling. This activates the glutes, which helps the tight hip flexors to relax (reciprocal inhibition)."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Dead Bug","video":"Dead Bug","dose":"3 x 10 reps (alternating), every other day","how":"This exercise strengthens the core muscles that stabilize the pelvis, reducing the strain on the iliacus."},{"name":"Bodyweight Squat","video":"Bodyweight Squat","dose":"3 x 12 reps, every other day","how":"Practice squatting with good form, focusing on using your glutes to power the movement up. This retrains proper hip mechanics."},{"name":"Lunges","video":"Lunges","dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"Lunges build single-leg stability and strength in the glutes and quads, which supports healthy hip function."}]},"habits":["Take frequent breaks from sitting. Use our 'Walk Break Protocol Seated' video.","Adjust your car seat to be more upright, reducing the sharp angle at your hips.","When standing, avoid locking your knees and letting your pelvis tilt forward.","Use a lumbar support roll when sitting to maintain a neutral spinal curve."],"expect":"Most people experience noticeable relief within 1-2 weeks of consistent release work and stretching. Building strength to prevent recurrence typically takes 4-6 weeks.","seePro":"Schedule a consultation if the pain is sharp, doesn't improve with 2 weeks of this protocol, or if you suspect a hernia."},"glute-med":{"origin":"Outer surface of the ilium (pelvic bone), between the posterior and anterior gluteal lines.","insertion":"Greater trochanter of the femur (the bony point on the outside of your hip).","action":"Abducts the hip (moves leg out to the side) and stabilizes the pelvis. It's crucial for walking, running, and single-leg balance.","nerve":"Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1).","symptoms":["A persistent, nagging ache along the belt line or top of the buttock.","Pain that can wrap around to the bony point on the side of the hip.","Pain that refers over the sacroiliac (SI) joint, often mistaken for an SI joint problem.","Difficulty and pain when lying on the affected side at night.","A feeling of weakness or instability in the hip, especially when climbing stairs or running."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located high on your buttock, right along your belt line?","w":3},{"q":"Does it hurt to lie on that side at night, forcing you to switch positions?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel pain or a sense of your hip 'giving way' when you stand on one leg to put on pants?","w":2},{"q":"Does walking, standing for a long time, or climbing stairs make the ache worse?","w":2},{"q":"Have you been told you have 'SI joint pain' but treatments for it haven't helped?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Single Leg Stance Test","how":"Stand in front of a mirror and lift one foot off the ground, balancing on the painful leg for 30 seconds. Watch your hips in the mirror.","positive":"If the hip of your lifted leg drops down, or if your familiar pain is reproduced in the standing leg's buttock within 30 seconds, it suggests gluteus medius weakness."},{"name":"Resisted Hip Abduction","how":"Lie on your good side with your legs straight. Have a partner gently press down on your top ankle as you try to lift the leg toward the ceiling.","positive":"Significant weakness or reproduction of your pain compared to the other side points to gluteus medius involvement."}],"causes":["Sitting for long periods with legs crossed.","Standing with all your weight shifted onto one hip ('hip-sitting').","Running on a banked surface (like the edge of a road) or always running the same direction on a track.","Weakness from inactivity, leading to overuse when activity is resumed.","Sleeping on one side without a pillow between the knees."],"mimics":["Sacroiliac (SI) joint dysfunction","Lumbar facet joint syndrome","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome (hip bursitis)","Lumbar radiculopathy (sciatica)"],"redFlags":["Numbness or tingling that travels down the leg.","Loss of bowel or bladder control.","Pain that is constant and severe, even at rest.","History of a recent fall or trauma to the hip."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Glute Medius Release (Ball)","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, daily","how":"Lie on your side with a lacrosse or tennis ball under the fleshy part of your upper buttock, just below the pelvic bone. Gently roll to find tender spots and hold with steady pressure."},{"name":"Clamshells (Isometric)","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 sec holds, twice daily","how":"Lie on your side with knees bent and hips stacked. Without moving your pelvis, gently lift your top knee. Hold at the top without pain, focusing on the muscle contraction."},{"name":"90/90 Hip Switch","video":"90/90 Hip Switch","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Perform this movement slowly and gently to improve hip mobility without stressing the irritated muscle."}],"restore":[{"name":"Standing Clamshell","video":"Standing Clamshell","dose":"2 x 15 reps per side, every other day","how":"This exercise activates the gluteus medius in a functional, weight-bearing position. Focus on keeping your pelvis level."},{"name":"Glute Walk","video":"Glute Walk","dose":"2 x 10 steps each direction, every other day","how":"Place a resistance band around your ankles or knees. Take slow, controlled side steps, keeping your toes pointed forward and your torso upright."},{"name":"Figure-4 Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, daily","how":"Lie on your back and cross one ankle over the opposite knee. Gently pull the bottom thigh toward your chest until you feel a stretch in the buttock of the crossed leg."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Standing Hip Abduction/Adduction","video":"Standing Hip Abduction/Adduction","dose":"3 x 12 reps per side, every other day","how":"Use a cable or band to add resistance as you move your leg out to the side. Maintain a stable torso and pelvis throughout the movement."},{"name":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"This variation challenges pelvic stability, forcing the gluteus medius to work harder to prevent the hip from dropping."},{"name":"Step Up","video":"Step Up","dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"Focus on keeping your knee aligned over your foot and your pelvis level as you step up. This is a key functional strengthener for the glute medius."}]},"habits":["Avoid 'hip-sitting'; stand with your weight evenly distributed on both feet.","When sitting, avoid crossing your legs for long periods.","Sleep with a pillow between your knees if you are a side-sleeper.","When carrying a heavy bag or child, switch sides frequently."],"expect":"You should notice less pain at night and with standing within 2-3 weeks. Consistent strengthening for 6-8 weeks is often needed to build the endurance to prevent the pain from returning.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if you have a 'snapping' sensation in your hip, if pain persists beyond 3-4 weeks, or if you have significant weakness that makes you feel unstable."},"glute-min":{"origin":"Outer surface of the ilium (pelvic bone), below the gluteus medius.","insertion":"Anterior aspect of the greater trochanter of the femur.","action":"Abducts the hip (moves leg out) and internally rotates the thigh. A key stabilizer of the hip joint.","nerve":"Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1).","symptoms":["Deep, aching pain in the buttock that is hard to pinpoint.","Pain that refers down the side of the thigh and leg, sometimes all the way to the ankle.","Pain that refers down the back of the thigh and calf, mimicking sciatica.","Intense pain when lying on the affected side, often worse than with gluteus medius.","Pain and a limp when walking, especially after getting up from a chair."],"questions":[{"q":"Does your pain travel down the outside of your leg, possibly reaching your ankle?","w":3},{"q":"Does your pain sometimes travel down the back of your leg, making you think you have sciatica?","w":3},{"q":"Is it nearly impossible to lie on the painful side at night?","w":2},{"q":"Do you have a noticeable limp, especially for the first few steps after you stand up?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain a deep, constant ache in your buttock that you can't seem to touch directly?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Gluteus Minimus Palpation","how":"Lie on your good side. Find the bony point of your hip (greater trochanter). The gluteus minimus trigger points are often found just in front of and above this bone. Press deeply to explore for exquisite tenderness.","positive":"Finding a spot of extreme, sharp tenderness that reproduces your familiar buttock and leg pain."}],"causes":["Sitting on a wallet in your back pocket.","A direct fall onto the side of the hip.","Prolonged sitting, especially in a chair that is too high or too hard.","Walking or running with poor pelvic stability (a 'hip drop' gait).","Wearing worn-out shoes that alter your walking mechanics."],"mimics":["Lumbar radiculopathy (true sciatica from a disc herniation)","Greater trochanteric pain syndrome (hip bursitis)","Hip joint osteoarthritis","Piriformis syndrome","Iliotibial (IT) band syndrome"],"redFlags":["True weakness (foot drop) or loss of sensation in the leg or foot.","Loss of bowel or bladder control.","Pain that is not relieved by any position and is worsening.","History of cancer, fever, or unexplained weight loss."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Glute Minimus Release (Ball)","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes per side, daily","how":"Lie on your side with a lacrosse or tennis ball placed just in front of the bony point of your hip. Lean into it gently, breathing deeply. Avoid rolling directly on the bone."},{"name":"90/90 Hip Switch","video":"90/90 Hip Switch","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"This gentle mobility drill helps to improve internal and external rotation of the hip, which can help calm an irritated gluteus minimus."},{"name":"Side-Lying Pillow Squeeze","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 sec holds, twice daily","how":"Lie on your side with a pillow between your knees. Gently squeeze the pillow and hold, engaging the inner thigh muscles, which can help the outer hip muscles relax."}],"restore":[{"name":"Standing Clamshell","video":"Standing Clamshell","dose":"2 x 15 reps per side, every other day","how":"This exercise helps activate and strengthen the gluteus medius and minimus in a functional standing position."},{"name":"Standing Hip IR Liftoff","video":"Standing Hip IR Liftoff","dose":"2 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"This specifically targets the internal rotation function of the gluteus minimus. Focus on slow, controlled movement without leaning your torso."},{"name":"Piriformis Stretch (Seated)","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, daily","how":"While seated, cross one ankle over the opposite knee. Gently lean forward with a flat back until you feel a stretch in the buttock. This can help stretch the deep hip rotators."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Glute Walk","video":"Glute Walk","dose":"2 x 10 steps each direction, every other day","how":"Use a resistance band around your ankles or knees. The side-stepping motion is excellent for building endurance in both the gluteus medius and minimus."},{"name":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 8 reps per side, every other day","how":"Drive through one heel to lift the hips level, without letting the pelvis drop on the free side. Pause 2 seconds at the top."},{"name":"Side Crab Walk","video":"Side Crab Walk","dose":"3 x 6 reps per side, every other day","how":"Stay low with a band around the knees or ankles and step sideways, keeping toes forward and the pelvis level."}]},"habits":["Never sit on your wallet. Keep it in your front pocket or jacket.","Get up and walk around at least once every hour.","Ensure your office chair provides good support and is at the correct height.","Invest in supportive footwear and replace running shoes regularly."],"expect":"Due to its common mimicry of sciatica, this muscle can be stubborn. Consistent release and activation can provide relief from the referred leg pain within 2-4 weeks. Building robust strength to correct underlying walking patterns may take 2-3 months.","seePro":"Book an appointment if you have numbness, tingling, or true weakness in your leg, or if the pain does not begin to improve after 3 weeks of dedicated effort."},"piriformis":{"origin":"Anterior surface of the sacrum.","insertion":"Superior aspect of the greater trochanter of the femur.","action":"Externally rotates the hip (turns the leg outward) when the hip is extended, and abducts the hip when it is flexed.","nerve":"Nerve to piriformis (S1, S2).","symptoms":["A deep, aching pain in the center of the buttock.","Pain that refers down the back or back-outside of the thigh, often stopping at the knee.","Numbness or tingling down the back of the leg if the muscle is compressing the sciatic nerve.","Pain that is significantly worse with sitting, especially on hard surfaces or while driving.","Difficulty crossing the affected leg over the other one."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain focused in the dead center of one buttock?","w":3},{"q":"Does sitting for more than 15-20 minutes dramatically increase your buttock pain?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain travel down the back of your thigh, but usually not past your knee?","w":2},{"q":"Do you get relief by standing up and walking around after sitting?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel pain when you try to pull your knee towards your opposite shoulder?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Piriformis Stretch Test (FAIR Test)","how":"Lie on your back, cross the ankle of the painful side over the opposite knee. Gently pull the bottom knee toward your chest and slightly toward the opposite shoulder.","positive":"Reproduction of your specific buttock and/or posterior thigh pain indicates piriformis involvement."}],"causes":["Prolonged sitting, which compresses and shortens the muscle.","Sitting on a wallet in a back pocket ('wallet neuritis').","Overuse from activities like running or cycling without proper glute strength.","Anatomical variation where the sciatic nerve passes through the piriformis muscle.","Direct trauma or fall onto the buttock."],"mimics":["Lumbar radiculopathy (true sciatica from a disc herniation)","Sacroiliac (SI) joint dysfunction","Hamstring tendinopathy","Ischial bursitis ('weaver's bottom')","Gluteus minimus trigger points"],"redFlags":["Loss of bowel or bladder control (cauda equina syndrome).","Progressive leg weakness, such as a 'foot drop'.","Numbness in the 'saddle' area (groin and inner thighs).","Pain that is severe, constant, and not relieved by any change in position."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Piriformis Release (Ball)","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, daily","how":"Sit on the floor with your knees bent. Place a tennis or lacrosse ball under the center of the painful buttock. Support yourself with your hands and gently roll to find the tender spot, then hold with steady pressure."},{"name":"90/90 Hip Switch","video":"90/90 Hip Switch","dose":"2 x 10 reps, twice daily","how":"This is an excellent gentle mobilizer for the deep hip rotators. Move slowly and within a pain-free range of motion."},{"name":"Figure-4 Stretch (Gentle)","video":null,"dose":"3 x 20 sec holds, twice daily","how":"Lie on your back, cross the ankle of the painful side over the opposite knee. Do NOT pull yet. Just hold this position and breathe, allowing the muscle to relax."}],"restore":[{"name":"Figure-4 Stretch (Active)","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, daily","how":"From the gentle Figure-4 position, now reach through and gently pull the bottom thigh toward your chest until you feel a comfortable stretch in the buttock."},{"name":"Hip Neuroglide","video":"Hip Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps per side, daily","how":"This is crucial for piriformis issues. It helps the sciatic nerve slide more freely relative to the piriformis muscle, reducing irritation and referred symptoms."},{"name":"Clamshells","video":"Standing Clamshell","dose":"2 x 15 reps per side, every other day","how":"Lie on your side with knees bent and hips stacked. Lift your top knee without rocking your pelvis back. This strengthens other hip rotators to take the load off the piriformis."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Standing Hip ER Liftoff","video":"Standing Hip ER Liftoff","dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"This directly strengthens the piriformis and other external rotators in a functional, standing position. Focus on control, not height."},{"name":"Glute Bridge","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Strengthening the main gluteus maximus muscle provides stability to the pelvis and can reduce the over-activity and strain on the smaller piriformis."},{"name":"Hip Thrust","video":"Hip Thrust","dose":"3 x 8 reps, every other day","how":"Shoulders on a bench, drive the hips up by squeezing the glutes, ribs down. Lower slowly; add load only when pain-free."}]},"habits":["Take your wallet out of your back pocket permanently.","Set a timer to get up and walk every 20-30 minutes when sitting.","When driving, use a seat cushion with a cutout for the tailbone/piriformis area.","Try to sit with your feet flat on the floor rather than with your legs crossed."],"expect":"Relief from sitting-related pain can often be felt within the first 1-2 weeks. Reducing nerve irritation and building enough glute strength to prevent recurrence is a longer process, typically taking 6-12 weeks of consistent work.","seePro":"Schedule an in-person evaluation if you experience true numbness, tingling, or weakness that doesn't improve with nerve glides, or if your pain is not significantly better after 4 weeks."},"glute-max":{"origin":"Ilium (back of the hip bone), sacrum, and coccyx (tailbone).","insertion":"Gluteal tuberosity of the femur (thigh bone) and the iliotibial (IT) band.","action":"The main muscle for extending your hip. It powers movements like standing up from a chair, climbing stairs, running, and lifting.","nerve":"Inferior gluteal nerve (L5, S1, S2).","symptoms":["A deep, nagging ache in the buttock.","Pain concentrated around the tailbone (coccyx) and the bony part you sit on (ischial tuberosity).","Stiffness and pain after sitting for a long time, especially on hard surfaces.","A feeling of weakness when climbing stairs or getting up from a squat.","Pain that makes it hard to lie on your back or the affected side.","A 'bruised' feeling deep in the buttock."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain mainly in the fleshy part of your buttock, possibly near your tailbone?","w":3},{"q":"Does the ache get worse after sitting for more than 30 minutes, especially on a hard chair?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel pain or weakness when pushing up stairs, walking up a hill, or rising from a deep chair?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain a deep ache rather than a sharp, shooting, or tingling pain down your leg?","w":2},{"q":"Does pressing deeply into the main part of your buttock reproduce your specific ache?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Active Hip Extension Test","how":"Lie on your stomach with your legs straight. Squeeze the buttock muscles on the painful side and lift that entire leg a few inches off the floor, keeping the knee straight.","positive":"You feel your specific buttock pain, or the leg feels significantly weaker or heavier than the other side."},{"name":"Glute Bridge Test","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent and feet flat on the floor, hip-width apart. Squeeze your buttocks and lift your hips toward the ceiling.","positive":"This movement reproduces your buttock or tailbone pain, or you feel your hamstrings cramping because the glutes aren't working well."}],"causes":["Prolonged sitting, which compresses and deactivates the muscle.","Sudden increase in activities like running, hiking, or weightlifting (squats, deadlifts).","Direct trauma, such as a fall onto the buttocks.","Sleeping on a hard mattress or on one side without support.","An inactive lifestyle leading to weak, inhibited gluteal muscles.","Poor form during exercise, leading to overuse or strain."],"mimics":["Sciatica or lumbar radiculopathy (nerve root irritation).","Sacroiliac (SI) joint dysfunction.","Piriformis syndrome.","Ischial bursitis ('sits bone' bursitis).","Coccygodynia (tailbone pain)."],"redFlags":["Pain accompanied by numbness or tingling in the 'saddle' area (groin, inner thighs).","Sudden, unexplained loss of bowel or bladder control.","Progressive weakness in the leg, causing you to trip or your foot to 'drop'.","Pain that is constant, severe, and doesn't change with position or movement."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Glute Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"2 minutes, twice daily","how":"Sit on a lacrosse ball or tennis ball, placing it on the most tender spot in your buttock. Gently roll to apply pressure, but avoid sharp, shooting pain."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"2 x 5 minutes, daily","how":"Lie on your back with feet on a wall or chair, knees and hips at 90 degrees. Focus on deep belly breaths to relax the pelvic floor and hips."},{"name":"Dynamic Childs Pose","video":"Dynamic Childs Pose","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Gently rock your hips side to side in a child's pose to create space and mobility in the low back and hips."}],"restore":[{"name":"Quadruped Glute Kick","video":"Quadruped Glute Kick","dose":"3 x 15 per side, every other day","how":"On all fours, keep your core braced and extend one leg back, squeezing the glute at the top. Avoid arching your lower back."},{"name":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 10 per side, every other day","how":"Lie on your back with one knee bent and the other leg straight. Drive through the heel to lift your hips, focusing the work in your glute."},{"name":"Hip Neuroglide","video":"Hip Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Perform this gentle nerve mobilization to ensure the nerves around the hip are moving freely."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Hip Thrust","video":"Hip Thrust","dose":"3 x 10-12, twice weekly","how":"With your upper back on a bench and a weight across your hips, drive your hips up by squeezing your glutes. Keep your chin tucked."},{"name":"Romanian Deadlift","video":"Romanian Deadlift","dose":"3 x 8-10, twice weekly","how":"Holding weights, hinge at your hips with a flat back, feeling a stretch in your hamstrings. Squeeze your glutes to return to standing."},{"name":"Constant Glute Engagement","video":"Constant Glute Engagement","dose":"Incorporate into daily walking","how":"Practice this cue to gently engage your glutes with each step, improving your mind-muscle connection throughout the day."}]},"habits":["Take frequent breaks from sitting. Use our 'Walk Break Protocol Seated' every 30 minutes.","Sit on a cushion or donut pillow to reduce direct pressure on the muscle.","When standing, maintain a neutral pelvis instead of letting your hips sway forward.","Focus on pushing through your heels when climbing stairs or standing up to engage the glutes."],"expect":"With consistent application of this protocol, most people feel a significant reduction in pain within 2-3 weeks. Building full strength and resilience can take 6-8 weeks.","seePro":"Book an appointment if your pain is severe, doesn't improve after 2 weeks of self-care, or if you experience any of the 'Red Flags'."},"tfl":{"origin":"Anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) - the bony point at the front of your hip.","insertion":"Iliotibial (IT) band.","action":"Flexes, abducts, and internally rotates the hip. It helps stabilize the pelvis when you're standing on one leg.","nerve":"Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1).","symptoms":["A sharp or aching pain at the front and/or outer part of the hip.","Pain that refers down the outside of the thigh, often mistaken for IT band syndrome.","Discomfort that worsens when lying on the affected side at night.","Pain when standing up after prolonged sitting.","A feeling of tightness or restriction at the front of the hip pocket area.","Pain that is aggravated by running, especially on a banked surface."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located at the very front and side of your hip, right where a front pocket would be?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain travel down the outside of your thigh, but usually stops before reaching your knee?","w":3},{"q":"Is it painful to lie directly on the affected hip at night?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel a sharp 'catch' when you lift your knee towards your chest or swing your leg out to the side?","w":2},{"q":"Does your pain get worse with prolonged walking or running, but feel better with rest?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Ober's Test (Modified)","how":"Lie on your pain-free side with the bottom leg bent for stability. Let the top (painful) leg drop down behind you towards the floor. A partner can help stabilize your pelvis.","positive":"You feel a sharp pain or strong stretch at the front/side of your hip, or the leg is unable to drop below parallel to the floor."},{"name":"Resisted Hip Flexion/Abduction","how":"While seated, try to lift your knee up and out to the side against resistance from your own hand.","positive":"This action reproduces the specific pain at the front-side of your hip."}],"causes":["Prolonged sitting with hips flexed, which shortens the muscle.","Running or cycling with poor mechanics, causing the TFL to overwork.","Weak gluteus medius muscles, forcing the TFL to compensate for pelvic stability.","Sleeping in the fetal position, which keeps the muscle in a shortened state.","Wearing worn-out shoes or running on uneven surfaces.","Leg length discrepancy."],"mimics":["Trochanteric bursitis (hip bursitis).","IT band syndrome (though TFL is often the root cause).","Hip labral tear or femoroacetabular impingement (FAI).","Gluteus medius tendonitis or tear.","Lumbar spine referral (L4-L5 nerve root)."],"redFlags":["A 'clicking' or 'locking' sensation deep inside the hip joint.","Inability to bear weight on the leg.","Pain accompanied by fever, chills, or unexplained weight loss.","Pain that is severe and constant, even at rest."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"TFL Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"60-90 seconds, twice daily","how":"Lie on your side with a lacrosse ball or foam roller just below and in front of the bony point of your hip. Gently roll to find the tender spot and hold pressure."},{"name":"90/90 Hip Switch","video":"90/90 Hip Switch","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Sit on the floor and gently rotate your hips from side to side. This mobilizes the hip joint without stressing the TFL."},{"name":"Pillow Belt Reset","video":"Pillow Belt Reset","dose":"1 x 5 minutes, daily","how":"Use this technique to gently reset the pelvis and reduce compensatory tension in the hip flexors."}],"restore":[{"name":"Couch Stretch","video":"Couch Stretch","dose":"2 x 30-45 sec per side, daily","how":"Perform this stretch gently, focusing on squeezing the glute of the back leg to open the front of the hip. Do not force it."},{"name":"Standing Clamshell","video":"Standing Clamshell","dose":"3 x 15 per side, every other day","how":"This exercise activates the gluteus medius, which helps reduce the workload on the TFL. Focus on rotating the hip, not just lifting the leg."},{"name":"Standing Hip CARs","video":"Standing Hip CARs","dose":"2 x 5 circles each way, daily","how":"Perform slow, controlled circles with your hip to improve joint mobility and control, reducing strain on the TFL."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Side Crab Walk","video":"Side Crab Walk","dose":"3 x 10 steps each way, twice weekly","how":"Place a band around your knees or ankles. Stay low and take slow, controlled steps sideways, keeping tension on the band to strengthen your gluteus medius."},{"name":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 12 per side, twice weekly","how":"This strengthens the gluteus maximus, a key stabilizer that prevents the TFL from overworking."},{"name":"Step Up","video":"Step Up","dose":"3 x 10 per side, twice weekly","how":"Focus on driving through the heel of the front foot and keeping your pelvis level to build single-leg stability."}]},"habits":["Avoid sitting with your legs crossed.","When sleeping on your side, place a pillow between your knees to keep your hips in a neutral alignment.","Stand up and stretch your hips into extension (like in the Couch Stretch) every 30-60 minutes.","Check your running form; ensure your feet don't cross the midline ('crossover gait')."],"expect":"You should notice less pain with daily activities within 1-2 weeks. Full resolution, especially for athletes, may take 4-6 weeks as you retrain movement patterns and build glute strength.","seePro":"Schedule a visit if you have a painful clicking in the hip, if the pain doesn't improve with 2 weeks of care, or if it significantly impacts your ability to walk or sleep."},"adductors":{"origin":"Pubic bone and ischium (lower parts of the pelvis).","insertion":"Femur (thigh bone) and tibia (shin bone).","action":"A group of muscles that pull the legs together (adduction). They also help with hip flexion and rotation, and are crucial for stabilizing the pelvis during walking and running.","nerve":"Obturator nerve and parts of the sciatic nerve (L2, L3, L4).","symptoms":["Pain high in the groin, where the thigh meets the pelvis.","Aching or sharp pain along the inner thigh.","Pain that can refer down to the inside of the knee or even the top of the shin.","Stiffness or pain when spreading the legs apart.","A feeling of weakness or instability in the hip or groin.","Pain that is sharp with quick side-to-side movements, like in soccer or tennis."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located in your groin or along your inner thigh?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain get worse when you move sideways, get out of a car, or kick a ball?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel pain when you squeeze your knees together against resistance?","w":2},{"q":"Does the pain sometimes travel down to the inside of your knee?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel a deep ache in the groin after running or playing sports?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Adductor Squeeze Test","how":"Lie on your back with your knees bent. Place your fist or a small ball between your knees and squeeze for 5 seconds.","positive":"This action reproduces your specific groin or inner thigh pain."},{"name":"Passive Adductor Stretch","how":"Sit on the floor and bring the soles of your feet together, letting your knees fall out to the sides (butterfly stretch). Gently press on your knees.","positive":"You feel a sharp, painful pull in the groin, not just a normal stretch."}],"causes":["Sudden changes in direction or explosive movements in sports (e.g., soccer, hockey, tennis).","Over-striding during running or doing the splits without proper warm-up.","Weakness in the adductor muscles or core, leading to strain.","Prolonged sitting with legs crossed.","A sudden increase in training intensity or volume.","Poor flexibility in the hips."],"mimics":["Sports hernia (athletic pubalgia).","Inguinal hernia.","Hip joint arthritis or labral tear.","Stress fracture of the pubic ramus or femur.","Nerve entrapment (obturator or ilioinguinal nerve)."],"redFlags":["A visible bulge in the groin, especially when coughing or straining (suggests a hernia).","Numbness or tingling in the groin or inner thigh.","Severe pain that prevents you from walking.","Pain accompanied by testicular pain or swelling."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Adductor Pressure Release","video":null,"dose":"60-90 seconds per spot, daily","how":"Sit on the floor with one leg bent out to the side. Place a foam roller or soft ball under your inner thigh and gently roll from knee to groin, pausing on tender spots."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"2 x 5 minutes, daily","how":"This helps relax the pelvic floor and surrounding muscles, which can be tight and contribute to adductor tension."},{"name":"Gentle Isometric Squeeze","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 sec holds, twice daily","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Place a soft pillow between your knees and gently squeeze at 20-30% of your max effort. This is for pain relief, not strengthening."}],"restore":[{"name":"Standing Hip Abduction/Adduction","video":"Standing Hip Abduction/Adduction","dose":"2 x 15 reps per side, daily","how":"Perform slow, controlled leg swings side-to-side to gently mobilize the hip and activate the adductors and their opposing muscles (abductors)."},{"name":"Standing Hip Abduction/Adduction","video":"Standing Hip Abduction/Adduction","dose":"3 x 8 per side, every other day","how":"Hold a support and sweep the leg out and back across the body slowly, in a pain-free range."},{"name":"Single Leg Balance","video":"Single Leg Balance","dose":"3 x 30 sec holds per side, daily","how":"Balancing challenges the stabilizing role of the adductors, rebuilding their endurance and control."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Bodyweight Squat","video":"Bodyweight Squat","dose":"3 x 10-12, twice weekly","how":"Use a slightly wider stance with toes turned out, knees tracking over the toes; squeeze the inner thighs as you stand."},{"name":"Side Plank with Leg Lift","video":null,"dose":"3 x 8-10 lifts per side, twice weekly","how":"In a side plank, lift the top leg to strengthen the abductors (glute medius), then lower it and lift the bottom leg slightly off the floor to strengthen the adductors."},{"name":"Copenhagen Adduction Exercise","video":null,"dose":"3 x 8-10 reps per side, twice weekly","how":"Lie on your side and place your top leg on a bench or chair. Lift your hips and body off the floor using your inner thigh muscles. This is an advanced, highly effective adductor exercise."}]},"habits":["Warm up thoroughly before sports, including dynamic stretches like leg swings.","Avoid sitting with your legs crossed for long periods.","When getting out of a car, keep your knees together and pivot your whole body.","Gradually increase training intensity; avoid sudden sprints or changes of direction when you're not prepared."],"expect":"Minor strains often feel better within 1-3 weeks. More significant strains, especially those involving the tendon, can take 6-12 weeks to fully heal and return to sport.","seePro":"See a professional if you suspect a hernia (bulge in the groin), if pain is severe and you can't walk, or if there's no improvement after 2-3 weeks of consistent rehab."},"rectus-femoris":{"origin":"Anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS) - a bony point on the front of the pelvis, just below the TFL origin.","insertion":"Tibial tuberosity via the patellar tendon (the bony bump below your kneecap).","action":"A key part of the quadriceps group. It extends the knee (straightens the leg) and also flexes the hip (lifts the knee towards the chest).","nerve":"Femoral nerve (L2, L3, L4).","symptoms":["A deep, nagging ache felt around or behind the kneecap (patella).","Pain at the front of the hip, near the hip bone, especially when lifting the leg.","Stiffness and pain in the front of the knee after sitting for a long time ('movie-goer's sign').","Pain that is worse when going down stairs or down a hill.","A feeling of weakness or instability in the knee.","Difficulty fully straightening or bending the knee due to pain."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your main pain a deep ache around or under your kneecap?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain get worse after you've been sitting for a while with your knees bent?","w":3},{"q":"Do you also sometimes feel a separate pain point at the very front of your hip bone?","w":2},{"q":"Is going DOWN stairs more painful than going up?","w":2},{"q":"Does your knee feel stiff when you first stand up?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Ely's Test","how":"Lie on your stomach. Have a partner gently bend your knee, bringing your heel towards your buttock.","positive":"You feel a sharp pain in the front of your thigh or hip, and the hip on the same side may lift off the table as the muscle pulls on it."},{"name":"Resisted Knee Extension","how":"Sit on a chair with your knee bent. Try to straighten your leg against resistance from your own hand placed on your shin.","positive":"This action reproduces the ache around your kneecap or pain in the front of your thigh."}],"causes":["Prolonged sitting, which keeps the muscle in a shortened state at the hip and lengthened at the knee.","Overuse from activities like kicking, sprinting, or cycling.","Weak gluteal muscles, causing the rectus femoris to overwork as a hip flexor.","Direct trauma, like a blow to the front of the thigh.","Poor squatting or lunging mechanics.","An imbalance between the different quadriceps muscles."],"mimics":["Patellofemoral pain syndrome (runner's knee).","Patellar tendonitis (jumper's knee).","Chondromalacia patellae (cartilage wear).","Hip flexor strain (iliopsoas).","Femoral nerve entrapment."],"redFlags":["A 'pop' sound at the time of injury, followed by immediate swelling and inability to straighten the leg (possible quad tendon rupture).","A visible deformity or a gap in the muscle on the front of the thigh.","Knee locking, catching, or giving way completely.","Significant, rapid swelling of the knee joint."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Quad Foam Rolling","video":null,"dose":"2 minutes, daily","how":"Lie face down with a foam roller under your front thigh. Gently roll from the hip to just above the knee, avoiding the kneecap itself. Pause on tender spots."},{"name":"Knee Circles","video":"Knee Circles","dose":"2 x 10 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Stand and perform slow, controlled circles with your knees to gently mobilize the joint and improve fluid dynamics without load."},{"name":"Heel Slides","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15 reps, twice daily","how":"Lie on your back and gently slide your heel towards your buttock to bend the knee, then slide it back out. Move only within a pain-free range."}],"restore":[{"name":"Couch Stretch","video":"Couch Stretch","dose":"2 x 30-45 sec per side, daily","how":"This is the key stretch for the rectus femoris as it extends the hip and flexes the knee. Squeeze the glute of the back leg to intensify the stretch."},{"name":"Knee Neuroglide","video":"Knee Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"This helps ensure the femoral nerve, which runs through the quads, is moving freely. Perform gently."},{"name":"Quad Sets","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 sec holds, 10 reps, daily","how":"Sit or lie with your leg straight. Tighten the muscle on the front of your thigh, trying to push the back of your knee down. This re-establishes muscle activation."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Step Up","video":"Step Up","dose":"3 x 10-12 per side, twice weekly","how":"Focus on a slow, controlled lowering (eccentric) phase, as this builds strength and resilience in the quad tendon and muscle."},{"name":"Bodyweight Squat","video":"Bodyweight Squat","dose":"3 x 15, twice weekly","how":"Practice squatting with perfect form, ensuring your knees track over your feet and you're using your glutes to power the movement up."},{"name":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 12 per side, twice weekly","how":"Strengthening your glutes helps to offload the rectus femoris from its hip flexion duties, reducing overuse."}]},"habits":["When sitting for long periods, try to periodically straighten your leg out under your desk.","Use our 'Walk Break Protocol Seated' to get up and move every 30-60 minutes.","Adjust your bike seat height. A seat that is too low can increase strain on the rectus femoris.","Avoid sitting on your feet or in a deep 'W' sit position."],"expect":"Pain from sitting should decrease within a week or two. Building the strength to handle activities like running and stairs without pain typically takes 4-8 weeks of consistent effort.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if you experience knee locking or complete giving-way, if you heard a 'pop' during an injury, or if pain is not improving after 3 weeks of following the protocol."},"vmo":{"origin":"Medial lip of the linea aspera and intertrochanteric line of the femur (inner back and top of the thigh bone).","insertion":"Medial border of the patella (inner edge of the kneecap) and tibial tuberosity.","action":"Part of the quadriceps group, its primary role is to extend the knee. The lower fibers (often called the VMO) are crucial for the final degrees of extension and for keeping the kneecap tracking correctly in its groove.","nerve":"Femoral nerve (L2, L3, L4).","symptoms":["Aching or sharp pain on the inner side of the knee and kneecap.","A feeling that the knee might 'give way' or buckle, especially on stairs.","Swelling or puffiness on the inside of the knee, just above the kneecap.","A 'crunching' or 'grinding' sound (crepitus) under the kneecap with movement.","Pain that is worse when going up or down stairs, squatting, or running.","Difficulty fully straightening the leg with a 'locking' sensation."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located specifically on the inner side of your knee or kneecap?","w":3},{"q":"Does your knee ever feel like it's going to buckle or give out, especially on stairs?","w":3},{"q":"Do you see or feel a 'teardrop' shaped muscle on the inside of your thigh just above the knee, and is it tender to press on?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain worse after activities that involve repeated knee bending, like running or squatting?","w":2},{"q":"Does it hurt to sit for a long time with your knee bent?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"VMO Contraction Test","how":"Sit on the floor with your leg straight out in front of you. Place your fingers on the 'teardrop' muscle on the inside of your thigh, just above the kneecap. Try to tighten only this muscle.","positive":"You feel pain under your fingers, or you are unable to contract this muscle without contracting the entire thigh."},{"name":"Terminal Knee Extension Test","how":"Stand with a resistance band looped around the back of your painful knee and anchored to something in front of you. Let the band pull your knee into a slight bend, then straighten your leg by squeezing your quad.","positive":"This movement reproduces your specific inner knee pain."}],"causes":["An imbalance between the vastus medialis and the vastus lateralis (outer quad), causing the kneecap to pull sideways.","Weakness or poor activation of the VMO, often following a knee injury or surgery.","Flat feet (overpronation), which can cause the lower leg to rotate inward and affect kneecap tracking.","Weak hip and gluteal muscles, leading to poor leg alignment.","A sudden increase in squatting, lunging, or running volume."],"mimics":["Medial meniscus tear.","Medial collateral ligament (MCL) sprain.","Patellofemoral pain syndrome.","Medial plica syndrome.","Knee joint arthritis."],"redFlags":["The knee is locked in a bent position and you cannot straighten it.","A 'pop' sound at the time of injury followed by significant, rapid swelling.","Inability to put any weight on the leg.","Redness, heat, and extreme tenderness in the knee joint (could be infection or gout)."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"VMO Soft Tissue Release","video":null,"dose":"60-90 seconds, daily","how":"Sit with your leg slightly bent. Use your thumbs or a massage ball to apply gentle but firm pressure to the tender muscle fibers on the inside of your thigh, just above the knee."},{"name":"Quad Sets with Medial Focus","video":null,"dose":"3 x 10 sec holds, 10 reps, daily","how":"Sit with your leg straight. Place a small rolled towel under your knee. Squeeze your quad to straighten the leg and lift your heel, focusing on the inner quad muscle."},{"name":"Knee Neuroglide","video":"Knee Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps, daily","how":"Gently perform this nerve glide to ensure the nerves supplying the quad are not restricted, which can inhibit muscle function."}],"restore":[{"name":"Terminal Knee Extensions (TKEs)","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15 per side, every other day","how":"Loop a resistance band around the back of your knee, anchored in front of you. Step back to create tension. Bend and straighten the last 20-30 degrees of knee extension, focusing on the VMO."},{"name":"Wall Sit with Ball Squeeze","video":null,"dose":"3 x 30-45 sec holds, every other day","how":"Perform a wall sit at a comfortable height. Place a small ball between your knees and maintain a gentle squeeze to increase VMO activation."},{"name":"Single Leg Balance","video":"Single Leg Balance","dose":"3 x 30 sec holds per side, daily","how":"This improves proprioception and stability around the knee joint, which is crucial for VMO function."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Step Up","video":"Step Up","dose":"3 x 10-12 per side, twice weekly","how":"Use a low step. Focus on keeping your knee aligned over your second toe and avoid letting it collapse inward. Control the movement on the way down."},{"name":"Bodyweight Squat (Heels Elevated)","video":"Bodyweight Squat","dose":"3 x 12-15, twice weekly","how":"Place your heels on a small plate or book. This allows for a more upright torso and can help you focus on quad activation, including the VMO."},{"name":"Step Up","video":"Step Up","dose":"3 x 8-10 per side, twice weekly","how":"Step up onto a low box, keeping the knee lined up over the second toe; lower slowly over 3 seconds."}]},"habits":["Avoid letting your knee collapse inward during daily activities like climbing stairs or standing up.","Consider footwear or orthotics if you have significantly flat feet.","When sitting, try to avoid positions that twist the knee, like sitting with one foot tucked under you.","Focus on squeezing your quad to fully 'lock out' your knee at the top of each step when walking."],"expect":"A reduction in buckling sensations and daily pain can often be felt in 2-4 weeks. Rebuilding the VMO's strength and coordination to prevent recurrence during sports can take 8-12 weeks.","seePro":"Book an appointment if your knee is locking, if it swells up significantly after activity, or if the feeling of instability persists despite 3-4 weeks of consistent exercise."},"vastus-lateralis":{"origin":"Greater trochanter and linea aspera of the femur (thigh bone).","insertion":"Patella (kneecap) and tibial tuberosity (upper shin bone) via the patellar ligament.","action":"Extends (straightens) the knee. It is a primary power muscle for standing up, climbing stairs, squatting, and running.","nerve":"Femoral nerve (L2, L3, L4).","symptoms":["Aching pain along the outside of the thigh.","Pain that refers down to the outer side of the knee.","Sharp pain when lying on the affected side, often disturbing sleep.","A feeling of stiffness or a 'catching' sensation in the knee.","Weakness or a feeling of instability when squatting or going down stairs."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain mainly on the *outer* side of your thigh, sometimes traveling to the outside of your knee?","w":3},{"q":"Does lying directly on that side in bed make the pain noticeably worse?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel a sharp pain on the outside of your knee when you squat or go down stairs?","w":2},{"q":"Does your knee sometimes feel stiff, as if it's hard to bend or straighten fully?","w":1},{"q":"Have you recently increased your running, cycling, or squatting activities?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Single Leg Squat Test","how":"Stand on one leg and slowly bend your knee as if sitting in a chair, going only as far as is comfortable. Observe your knee's movement in a mirror.","positive":"Pain on the outside of the thigh or knee, or if your knee wobbles or dives inward, can indicate weakness or tightness in this muscle."}],"causes":["Overuse from activities like running, cycling, or leg-heavy gym workouts.","Sitting for long periods with knees bent, especially in a cramped space.","Sleeping on your side without a pillow between your knees, compressing the muscle.","Direct trauma, like a fall or impact to the outer thigh.","Muscle imbalances, such as weak gluteal muscles, causing the quads to overwork."],"mimics":["Iliotibial (IT) band syndrome","Lateral knee meniscus tear","Lateral collateral ligament (LCL) sprain","Lumbar radiculopathy (nerve pain from the low back)"],"redFlags":["Inability to bear weight on the leg.","Visible, significant swelling or deformity around the knee or thigh.","A 'popping' sound at the time of injury followed by immediate, severe pain.","Numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation spreading down the leg."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Foam Roll Outer Thigh","video":null,"dose":"1-2 minutes, once daily","how":"Lie on your side with a foam roller under your outer thigh. Roll slowly from your hip to just above your knee, pausing on tender spots for 20-30 seconds."},{"name":"Gentle Knee Bends","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15, twice daily","how":"Holding onto a counter for support, slowly bend and straighten your knees through a pain-free range of motion. Do not push into sharp pain."}],"restore":[{"name":"Standing Quad Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, daily","how":"Stand on one leg, holding onto something for balance. Grab your other ankle and gently pull your heel towards your buttock, feeling a stretch in the front of your thigh."},{"name":"Couch Stretch","video":"Couch Stretch","dose":"2 x 30-60 sec per side, daily","how":"Follow the video to perform this deep stretch for the quads and hip flexors. Keep your core engaged to protect your lower back."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Bodyweight Squat","video":"Bodyweight Squat","dose":"3 x 10-15, every other day","how":"Focus on keeping your knees aligned over your second toe; don't let them collapse inward. Control the movement on the way down and up."},{"name":"Lunges","video":"Lunges","dose":"3 x 8-12 per leg, every other day","how":"Take a controlled step forward, lowering your hips until both knees are bent at about a 90-degree angle. Push back to the starting position."}]},"habits":["When sleeping on your side, place a pillow between your knees to keep your hips aligned.","Avoid sitting with your legs crossed for long periods.","Take regular breaks from sitting to stand up and stretch your legs.","Ensure your bike seat height is properly adjusted to avoid over-stressing the quads."],"expect":"With consistent adherence to the protocol, most people feel a significant reduction in pain within 2-4 weeks. Full recovery and return to sport may take 6-8 weeks.","seePro":"Book an appointment if pain is severe, doesn't improve after 2 weeks of self-care, or if you suspect a more serious injury like a ligament or meniscus tear."},"hamstrings":{"origin":"Ischial tuberosity (the 'sit bone').","insertion":"Back of the tibia and fibula (the two lower leg bones).","action":"Bend the knee and extend the hip (move the thigh backward). Crucial for walking, running, and jumping.","nerve":"Sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2).","symptoms":["A deep ache in the back of the thigh, often worse with prolonged sitting.","Pain concentrated in the crease of the buttock where the hamstring attaches.","A feeling of tightness or pulling behind the knee.","Pain that gets sharp when trying to straighten the leg fully or bend forward.","Difficulty sleeping due to a persistent, deep ache in the back of the thigh."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain a deep ache in the back of your thigh that gets worse when you sit for a long time, like in a car or at a desk?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel pain right at your 'sit bone' or in the crease where your buttock meets your thigh?","w":3},{"q":"Does bending forward to touch your toes cause a sharp, pulling pain in the back of your thigh (not just a normal stretch)?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain worse after running or doing activities that involve sprinting or kicking?","w":2},{"q":"Does the back of your knee feel tight and achy, especially after activity?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Seated Hamstring Test","how":"Sit on the edge of a chair with one leg straight out in front of you, heel on the floor. Gently lean forward from your hips with a straight back until you feel a stretch.","positive":"Sharp pain (not just a stretch) in the back of the thigh or behind the knee indicates hamstring tension or injury."}],"causes":["Prolonged sitting, which keeps the hamstrings in a shortened position.","Sudden sprinting, jumping, or kicking, especially without a proper warm-up.","Over-striding during running.","An anterior pelvic tilt, which puts the hamstrings on constant tension.","Weak gluteal muscles, forcing the hamstrings to overwork during hip extension."],"mimics":["Sciatica (nerve pain from the low back)","Proximal hamstring tendinopathy","Ischial bursitis (inflammation of the bursa at the sit bone)","Adductor magnus trigger points"],"redFlags":["A 'pop' or tearing sensation during an activity, followed by immediate, severe pain and bruising.","Numbness, tingling, or weakness that travels down into the foot.","Pain accompanied by loss of bowel or bladder control (seek immediate emergency care).","Inability to walk without a significant limp."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Lacrosse Ball on 'Sit Bone'","video":null,"dose":"1-2 minutes, once daily","how":"Sit on a firm chair and place a lacrosse ball under your 'sit bone' area. Gently roll to find tender spots, and hold pressure for 20-30 seconds. Avoid direct pressure on the nerve."},{"name":"Gentle Pelvic Tilts","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15, twice daily","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Gently flatten your low back into the floor, then arch it away. This mobilizes the pelvis and gently moves the hamstring attachments."}],"restore":[{"name":"Pike Forward Fold","video":"Pike Forward Fold","dose":"2 x 30-60 sec, daily","how":"Sit on the floor with legs straight. Hinge forward from your hips, keeping your back straight, until you feel a gentle stretch. Do not force it."},{"name":"Hip Neuroglide","video":"Hip Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10-15 reps per side, daily","how":"Follow the video to gently mobilize the sciatic nerve, which runs alongside the hamstrings. This is a nerve movement, not a stretch, so be gentle."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Glute Bridge","video":"Single Leg Glute Bridge","dose":"3 x 10-15 (start with two legs), every other day","how":"Lie on your back with knees bent. Squeeze your glutes to lift your hips toward the ceiling. This activates the glutes to help the hamstrings."},{"name":"Romanian Deadlift","video":"Romanian Deadlift","dose":"3 x 8-12 (start with no weight), every other day","how":"Follow the video, focusing on hinging at the hips with a flat back. This strengthens the hamstrings and glutes through their full range of motion."}]},"habits":["Use a standing desk or take frequent breaks from sitting every 30 minutes.","When sitting for long periods, use a cushion or rolled towel to slightly elevate your hips.","Warm up thoroughly before sports with dynamic stretches like leg swings.","Focus on a shorter, quicker stride when running to avoid over-reaching."],"expect":"Relief from sitting-related pain can often be felt within 1-2 weeks. Strengthening and restoring full function for sports can take 4-8 weeks, depending on the initial severity.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if you suspect a tear (a 'pop' with bruising), if pain is severe and not improving, or if you have any nerve symptoms like tingling or numbness."},"popliteus":{"origin":"Lateral condyle of the femur (outer thigh bone at the knee).","insertion":"Posterior surface of the tibia (back of the shin bone).","action":"Internally rotates the tibia on the femur to 'unlock' the knee from a straight position, allowing it to bend. It also helps stabilize the back of the knee.","nerve":"Tibial nerve (L4, L5, S1).","symptoms":["Sharp pain located directly in the back of the knee.","Pain that is worse when walking or running, especially downhill.","A feeling of instability or that the knee might 'give way' when twisting.","Difficulty fully straightening or 'locking' the knee.","Pain when crouching or squatting down low."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located precisely in the hollow at the back of your knee?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain significantly worse when you walk or run downhill?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel a sharp pain when you try to squat down low?","w":2},{"q":"Does your knee feel unstable, especially when twisting or turning?","w":1},{"q":"Do you have pain when you first 'unlock' your knee from a fully straight position to begin bending it?","w":2}],"tests":[{"name":"Resisted Knee Flexion Test","how":"Sit on a chair and cross the ankle of your good leg over the front of the painful leg's ankle. Try to bend the painful knee against the resistance of your other leg.","positive":"Pain in the back of the knee during this resisted movement points to the popliteus or hamstring tendons."}],"causes":["Overuse in sports that involve a lot of downhill running (e.g., trail running).","A sudden twist of the knee while the foot is planted.","Chronic over-pronation of the foot (foot rolling inward).","Muscle imbalances, particularly weak hamstrings or glutes.","Returning to activity too quickly after a previous knee injury."],"mimics":["Posterior horn of the meniscus tear","Baker's cyst (a fluid-filled sac behind the knee)","Hamstring tendinopathy","Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) injury"],"redFlags":["A 'pop' at the time of injury with immediate, significant swelling.","Inability to put any weight on the leg.","A feeling of the knee being 'locked' where you cannot bend or straighten it at all.","Visible swelling and redness in the back of the knee."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Gentle Knee Rotations","video":"Knee Circles","dose":"2 x 10 circles each way, twice daily","how":"Sit on a high chair so your feet don't touch the floor. Gently swing your lower leg to make small circles, moving from the knee joint."},{"name":"Pressure Release Behind Knee","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30-60 sec, daily","how":"Sit and place a lacrosse or tennis ball in the crease behind your knee. Gently bend and straighten your leg to apply pressure to the muscle. Avoid pressing on the very center of the knee pit."}],"restore":[{"name":"Knee Neuroglide","video":"Knee Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10-15 reps, daily","how":"Follow the video to gently mobilize the nerves around the back of the knee. This is a gentle flossing motion, not an aggressive stretch."},{"name":"Heel Slides","video":null,"dose":"2 x 15 reps, daily","how":"Lie on your back and slowly slide the heel of your affected leg towards your buttock, bending the knee. Slide back to the start. Move only within a pain-free range."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Terminal Knee Extensions (Band)","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15, every other day","how":"Anchor a resistance band to a post. Loop the other end around the back of your knee. Step back to create tension, then straighten your knee against the band's pull."},{"name":"Wall Sits","video":null,"dose":"3 x 30-60 sec holds, every other day","how":"Lean your back against a wall and slide down until your knees are at a comfortable angle. Hold this position, ensuring your knees track over your ankles."}]},"habits":["Avoid locking your knees when standing for long periods.","When walking downhill, take shorter steps and try to land with a slightly bent knee.","Ensure your footwear provides adequate support to prevent over-pronation.","Strengthen your glute muscles (e.g., with bridges, clamshells) to improve overall leg stability."],"expect":"Because it's a small stabilizer, popliteus pain can be stubborn. Consistent, gentle exercises should bring noticeable relief in 2-3 weeks, with a return to more demanding activities in 4-6 weeks.","seePro":"Book an appointment if you suspect a meniscus or ligament injury, if the knee is locking or giving way, or if pain does not improve with 2-3 weeks of conservative care."},"gastroc":{"origin":"Two heads from the medial and lateral condyles of the femur (back of the thigh bone just above the knee).","insertion":"Calcaneus (heel bone) via the Achilles tendon.","action":"Plantarflexes the foot (points the toes down) and assists in flexing (bending) the knee. It's the primary 'push-off' muscle for walking, running, and jumping.","nerve":"Tibial nerve (S1, S2).","symptoms":["A deep, cramp-like ache in the upper or middle part of the calf.","Pain that can refer up into the back of the knee.","Aching pain in the arch of the foot, especially in the morning or after rest.","Frequent calf cramps, especially at night.","A feeling of tightness or pulling in the calf when walking up stairs or hills."],"questions":[{"q":"Do you get intense, cramp-like pain in your upper calf, sometimes even at night?","w":3},{"q":"Does your pain also seem to travel into the arch of your foot?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain worse when you push off your toes, like when running, jumping, or climbing stairs?","w":2},{"q":"Does the back of your knee ache, in addition to your calf pain?","w":2},{"q":"Do you often wear high heels or shoes with a significant heel-to-toe drop?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Calf Raise Test","how":"Stand on one leg, holding onto something for balance. Perform a single-leg calf raise, pushing up onto the ball of your foot.","positive":"Sharp pain in the calf, weakness, or an inability to lift the heel off the ground can indicate a gastrocnemius issue."}],"causes":["Sudden increase in running mileage or intensity.","Activities with a lot of jumping or hill climbing.","Wearing high heels, which keeps the muscle in a shortened position.","Dehydration or electrolyte imbalances (e.g., low magnesium or potassium).","Switching to minimalist or 'zero-drop' shoes too quickly."],"mimics":["Soleus muscle strain (pain is typically lower in the calf)","Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - a blood clot (this is a medical emergency)","Achilles tendinopathy","Popliteal artery entrapment syndrome","Lumbar radiculopathy (S1 nerve root)"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe calf pain with swelling, redness, and warmth (could be a DVT - go to ER).","A 'pop' or feeling of being 'shot' in the back of the leg during activity, with inability to walk properly (possible Achilles tendon rupture or 'tennis leg').","Pain that doesn't change with movement or rest.","Numbness or a 'pins and needles' feeling in the foot."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Foam Roll Calf","video":null,"dose":"1-2 minutes per calf, daily","how":"Sit on the floor with a foam roller under your calf. Use your hands to support your weight and roll from below the knee to above the ankle. Cross one leg over the other for more pressure."},{"name":"Straight-Knee Calf Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, twice daily","how":"Stand facing a wall, step one foot back, keeping the back leg *straight* and the heel on the floor. You should feel this higher up in the calf."}],"restore":[{"name":"Ankle Neuroglide","video":"Ankle Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 15 reps per side, daily","how":"Follow the video to gently mobilize the nerves that run through the calf. This helps differentiate between muscle tightness and nerve tension."},{"name":"Wall Calf Stretch (Straight Knee)","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, daily","how":"Stand facing a wall with hands on the wall for support. Step one foot back, keeping the back leg straight and heel firmly on the floor until you feel a stretch in the upper calf."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Eccentric Calf Drops","video":"Eccentric Calf Drops","dose":"3 x 15 per side, every other day","how":"Stand on a step. Use both feet to rise up onto your toes, then lift the uninjured foot and *slowly* lower the heel of the affected foot below the step level. Use both feet to rise up again."},{"name":"Toe Walk","video":"Toe Walk","dose":"3 x 30 seconds, every other day","how":"Walk around on the balls of your feet, keeping your heels off the ground. This builds endurance in the calf muscles."}]},"habits":["Stay well-hydrated and ensure adequate electrolyte intake, especially if you are active.","Gradually transition to new footwear over several weeks.","Avoid sitting with your feet tucked under you for long periods.","Incorporate regular calf stretching into your post-workout routine."],"expect":"For mild strains or trigger points, you should feel better within 1-2 weeks. A more significant strain may take 4-6 weeks of consistent rehab to return to full activity.","seePro":"Seek immediate medical attention for any red flag symptoms. Book an appointment if you suspect an Achilles tendon rupture or if pain is severe and not improving after 2 weeks."},"soleus":{"origin":"Posterior (back) surface of the tibia and fibula, underneath the gastrocnemius.","insertion":"Calcaneus (heel bone) via the Achilles tendon.","action":"Plantarflexes the foot (points the toes down). It is a powerful postural muscle, active whenever you are standing to prevent you from falling forward.","nerve":"Tibial nerve (L5, S1, S2).","symptoms":["A deep, aching pain in the lower half of the calf.","Pain that refers directly into the heel and Achilles tendon area.","Pain that feels like 'plantar fasciitis' but is located more in the heel pad.","Stiffness and pain that is worse after long periods of standing or walking.","Pain that is often a constant, low-grade ache rather than a sharp, acute pain."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain a deep ache in your *lower* calf, heel, or Achilles tendon?","w":3},{"q":"Does your pain get significantly worse after prolonged standing or walking, like a day of shopping or sightseeing?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain feel similar to plantar fasciitis, but more in the back of the heel than the bottom of the foot?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain more of a constant, nagging ache than a sharp, stabbing pain?","w":2},{"q":"Do you feel pain when you press deeply into the sides of your Achilles tendon, into the fleshy part of your lower calf?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Bent-Knee Calf Raise Test","how":"Stand on one leg with the knee significantly bent (about 45 degrees), holding a wall for balance. Try to lift your heel off the ground. This isolates the soleus.","positive":"Pain in the lower calf or heel, or significant weakness/inability to lift the heel, points to a soleus issue."}],"causes":["Jobs or activities that require prolonged standing on hard surfaces.","Running long distances, as the soleus is a primary endurance muscle.","Wearing unsupportive footwear with poor shock absorption.","Driving for long hours, keeping the foot on the pedals.","A gait pattern with excessive forward lean or a heavy heel strike."],"mimics":["Achilles tendinopathy","Plantar fasciitis","Heel spur or stress fracture of the calcaneus","Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - a medical emergency","Lumbar radiculopathy (S1/S2 nerve root)"],"redFlags":["Sudden, severe calf pain with swelling, redness, and warmth (could be a DVT - go to ER).","A 'pop' or tearing sensation in the Achilles area (possible Achilles rupture).","Inability to bear weight or walk.","Pain that is present and severe even at rest, especially at night."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Bent-Knee Calf Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, twice daily","how":"Face a wall and step one foot back. Keeping both heels on the floor, bend both knees until you feel a deep stretch in the lower calf of the back leg."},{"name":"Self-Massage Lower Calf","video":null,"dose":"1-2 minutes per side, daily","how":"Sit on the floor and use your thumbs or a massage ball to apply firm, sustained pressure to tender spots in the lower, fleshy part of your calf, to the sides of the Achilles tendon."}],"restore":[{"name":"Ankle Neuroglide","video":"Ankle Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 15 reps per side, daily","how":"Follow the video instructions for this gentle nerve mobilization exercise. This helps ensure the nerves in the lower leg are moving freely."},{"name":"Seated Calf Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 sec per side, daily","how":"Sit on the floor with one leg extended. Loop a towel or band around the ball of your foot and gently pull back, keeping your knee slightly bent to target the soleus."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Seated Calf Raises","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15-20, every other day","how":"Sit on a chair with your feet flat on the floor. Place a weight (e.g., a heavy book or dumbbell) on your knees. Slowly lift your heels as high as possible, then lower them back down."},{"name":"Eccentric Calf Drops (Bent Knee)","video":"Eccentric Calf Drops","dose":"3 x 15 per side, every other day","how":"Perform as shown in the video, but maintain a bent knee throughout the movement to place more emphasis on the soleus muscle during the lowering phase."}]},"habits":["Wear supportive shoes, especially if you stand or walk for long periods.","Use an anti-fatigue mat if you have a standing desk or work on a hard floor.","Avoid standing with your knees locked back.","On long drives, use cruise control when safe to do so and move your ankle around periodically."],"expect":"Soleus issues can be persistent due to their constant use in standing and walking. Expect gradual improvement over 3-6 weeks with consistent stretching and strengthening. Full resolution may take longer.","seePro":"Seek immediate medical attention for any red flag symptoms. Book an appointment if pain is preventing you from walking normally or if there is no improvement after 3-4 weeks of dedicated self-care."},"tib-post":{"origin":"Posterior surfaces of the tibia and fibula, and the membrane between them.","insertion":"Multiple bones on the underside of the foot, primarily the navicular bone.","action":"Points the foot down and turns it inward. It is the primary muscle that holds up the main arch of your foot.","nerve":"Tibial Nerve (L4, L5)","symptoms":["Aching pain deep in the calf or along the inner shin bone.","Sharp pain on the inside of the ankle or in the arch, especially with activity.","A feeling that your arch is collapsing or your foot is becoming flatter.","Weakness or instability when trying to stand on your tiptoes.","Pain that gets worse after long periods of standing, walking, or running.","Morning stiffness in the inner ankle and arch."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located along the inside of your ankle bone, running down into your arch?","w":3},{"q":"Do you feel like the arch on the inside of your foot is getting lower or 'falling'?","w":3},{"q":"Is it very difficult or painful to do a single-leg heel raise (go up on your tiptoes) on the affected side?","w":2},{"q":"Does your pain feel deep inside your calf, rather than on the surface?","w":2},{"q":"Does wearing shoes with good arch support seem to help?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Single Leg Heel Raise","how":"Stand on the affected foot only and try to raise your heel off the ground, going up onto your tiptoes. You can lightly touch a wall for balance.","positive":"A positive test is significant pain on the inside of the ankle, inability to lift the heel, or if your heel rolls outward as you try to lift it."}],"causes":["Over-pronation or having 'flat feet'.","A sudden increase in impact activities like running or jumping.","Wearing unsupportive footwear for long periods.","Chronic ankle instability from previous injuries.","Being overweight, which increases the load on the arch.","Age-related changes to the tendon."],"mimics":["Plantar fasciitis","Tarsal tunnel syndrome (tibial nerve entrapment)","Shin splints (medial tibial stress syndrome)","Navicular stress fracture","Deltoid ligament sprain"],"redFlags":["Inability to bear any weight on the foot after an injury.","Numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation in the foot.","Sudden, severe swelling and bruising around the ankle without a clear injury.","Signs of a blood clot in the calf: localized swelling, redness, and warmth."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Lacrosse Ball Arch Release","video":null,"dose":"2 minutes, twice daily","how":"While seated, roll a lacrosse or tennis ball under the arch of your foot with firm pressure. Focus on any tender spots to release tension in the muscles supporting the tendon."},{"name":"Ankle Pumps","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15 reps, several times per day","how":"While sitting or lying down, gently point your toes away from you, then pull them back towards your shin. This gentle movement helps reduce swelling and stiffness."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once daily","how":"Follow the video to lie in a relaxed position. This helps calm the nervous system and reduce overall muscle guarding and pain perception."}],"restore":[{"name":"Short Foot Exercise","video":null,"dose":"5 reps of 10-second holds, twice daily","how":"With your foot flat on the floor, try to shorten your foot by pulling the ball of your foot towards your heel, creating an arch. Do this without curling your toes."},{"name":"Ankle Neuroglide","video":"Ankle Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps, once daily","how":"Follow the video to gently mobilize the nerves around the ankle. This can help reduce irritation and improve tissue health in the area."},{"name":"Towel Curls","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15 reps, once daily","how":"Sit with your foot flat on a towel. Use your toes to scrunch the towel towards you, which helps strengthen the small muscles in your foot that support the arch."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Eccentric Calf Drops","video":"Eccentric Calf Drops","dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Perform on a step as shown, focusing on a very slow (3-4 second) and controlled lowering of your heel. This builds strength and resilience in the tendon."},{"name":"Single Leg Balance","video":"Single Leg Balance","dose":"3 x 30-60 seconds, daily","how":"Stand on one leg, focusing on keeping your arch lifted and your ankle stable. This retrains the muscle's crucial role in stabilizing your foot."},{"name":"Toe Walk","video":"Toe Walk","dose":"3 x 20 steps, every other day","how":"Walk on your tiptoes as shown in the video. This directly strengthens the tibialis posterior and other calf muscles that support the arch."}]},"habits":["Wear supportive shoes with good arch support daily. Avoid going barefoot on hard surfaces.","Consider an over-the-counter orthotic insert to provide consistent arch support.","Gradually progress your activity levels. Avoid sudden jumps in running mileage or intensity.","Maintain a healthy weight to reduce the daily stress on your feet and ankles."],"expect":"With consistent daily exercises and supportive footwear, mild symptoms often improve in 2-4 weeks. More significant tendinopathy can take 6-12 weeks or longer to fully resolve.","seePro":"Book an appointment if you are unable to perform a single-leg heel raise, if pain is severe and not improving after 2 weeks of self-care, or if you suspect a fracture."},"tib-ant":{"name":"Tibialis anterior","origin":"Upper, outer surface of the shin bone (tibia).","insertion":"A bone on the inside of the mid-foot (medial cuneiform) and the base of the big toe bone (first metatarsal).","action":"Lifts the foot and toes up toward the shin (dorsiflexion) and helps turn the foot inward (inversion). It prevents your foot from 'slapping' the ground when you walk.","nerve":"Deep Fibular (Peroneal) Nerve (L4, L5)","symptoms":["Aching or sharp pain on the front of the shin, just to the outside of the shin bone.","Pain at the front of the ankle, where the foot meets the leg.","Pain that sometimes travels down to the top of the big toe.","A feeling of weakness when trying to lift your foot or toes.","Pain that is worse when walking or running, especially downhill or down stairs.","The muscle may feel tight or tender to the touch."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain located in the muscle on the front and outer side of your shin bone?","w":3},{"q":"Does the pain sometimes refer to the front of your ankle or the top of your big toe?","w":3},{"q":"Is the pain worse when you go down stairs or walk/run downhill?","w":2},{"q":"Do you notice the pain after driving for a long time or after activities that involve a lot of foot tapping?","w":2},{"q":"Do your shoes feel too tight across the top of your foot?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Resisted Foot Lift","how":"While sitting, cross your legs so the affected ankle rests on the other knee. Place your hand on top of your foot and try to lift your foot up against your hand's resistance.","positive":"A positive test is pain or noticeable weakness in the muscle on the front of your shin."},{"name":"Heel Walk","how":"Try to walk for 10-15 steps on your heels only, keeping the front part of your feet off the ground.","positive":"A positive test is a sharp increase in your shin pain or an inability to perform the walk."}],"causes":["Overuse from activities like running (especially downhill), hiking, or kicking sports.","Tight calf muscles, which force the tibialis anterior to work harder to lift the foot.","Switching to minimalist shoes or a different running style too quickly.","Lacing shoes, boots, or skates too tightly across the front of the ankle.","Driving for long periods, keeping the foot constantly lifted.","Weakness in the muscle, leading to fatigue and strain."],"mimics":["Shin splints (medial tibial stress syndrome)","Tibial stress fracture","Chronic exertional compartment syndrome","Deep fibular (peroneal) nerve entrapment"],"redFlags":["Severe pain, swelling, and redness on the shin after an injury (possible fracture).","Numbness on the top of the foot or a 'foot drop' (a new inability to lift your foot at all).","Intense, tight, or burning pain in the shin that gets progressively worse during exercise and doesn't resolve with rest.","Signs of a blood clot in the calf: localized swelling, redness, and warmth."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Tibialis Anterior Release","video":null,"dose":"2 minutes, once daily","how":"Sit on a chair or the floor. Use your thumbs or a lacrosse ball to apply firm, sustained pressure to tender spots in the muscle on the front/outside of your shin."},{"name":"Gentle Ankle Circles","video":null,"dose":"10 circles each direction, 3 times per day","how":"Sit or lie down and slowly rotate your ankle in large, gentle circles. This improves mobility and blood flow without straining the muscle."},{"name":"Calf Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 seconds per side, twice daily","how":"Stand facing a wall with one foot back. Keep the back leg straight and heel on the floor to stretch the upper calf. Then, bend the back knee to stretch the lower calf."}],"restore":[{"name":"Kneeling Shin Stretch","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 seconds, once daily","how":"Kneel on a soft surface with the tops of your feet flat on the floor. Gently sit back towards your heels until you feel a stretch on the front of your shins."},{"name":"Ankle Neuroglide","video":"Ankle Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps, once daily","how":"Follow the video to gently mobilize the nerves that run along the front of the ankle, which can become irritated with this condition."},{"name":"Isometric Dorsiflexion","video":null,"dose":"5 reps of 5-second holds, twice daily","how":"Sit with your heel on the ground. Use your hand or your other foot to provide resistance on top of your foot as you try to lift it, without allowing any movement."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Heel Walk","video":"Heel Walk","dose":"3 x 20 steps, every other day","how":"As shown in the video, walk on your heels while keeping the balls of your feet off the ground. This is the most direct way to strengthen the tibialis anterior."},{"name":"Banded Dorsiflexion","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Anchor a resistance band to a sturdy object in front of you. Sit and loop the other end around the top of your foot. Pull your toes toward your shin against the band's resistance."},{"name":"Eccentric Calf Drops","video":"Eccentric Calf Drops","dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Strengthening the opposing calf muscles helps balance the forces around the ankle. Follow the video, focusing on the slow lowering phase."}]},"habits":["Ensure your shoes are not laced too tightly, especially over the front of the ankle.","When running, focus on taking shorter, quicker steps to avoid 'over-striding' and slapping your foot down.","Regularly stretch your calf muscles, as tightness there can overload the shin muscles.","Gradually increase the intensity and duration of your activities, especially on hills."],"expect":"Mild strains often feel significantly better within 1-3 weeks. More persistent pain from chronic overuse can take 4-8 weeks of consistent rehab to resolve.","seePro":"Book an appointment if you experience 'foot drop' (inability to lift your foot), numbness on top of the foot, or pain that is severe and doesn't improve with rest."},"peroneals":{"name":"Peroneals (fibularis)","origin":"The outer surface of the lower leg bone (fibula).","insertion":"One tendon attaches to the outside of the mid-foot (5th metatarsal), and the other runs under the foot to attach to the inside.","action":"Turn the sole of the foot outward (eversion) and help point the foot down. They are critical for stabilizing the ankle and preventing it from 'rolling' inward.","nerve":"Superficial Fibular (Peroneal) Nerve (L5, S1)","symptoms":["Aching or sharp pain located behind or below the outer ankle bone.","Pain that runs along the outside edge of the foot toward the little toe.","A feeling of ankle instability, weakness, or that it might 'give way'.","Swelling and tenderness on the outside of the ankle.","A snapping or popping sensation on the outside of the ankle with movement.","Pain that is worse with cutting, pivoting, or walking on uneven ground."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain mainly located behind or just below your outer ankle bone?","w":3},{"q":"Do you have a history of 'rolling' your ankle (ankle sprains) on this side?","w":3},{"q":"Does your ankle feel unstable, like it could easily give out from under you?","w":2},{"q":"Is the pain worse when you walk on uneven surfaces like a grassy field or a trail?","w":2},{"q":"Do you ever feel a 'snapping' sensation on the outside of your ankle when you walk or run?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Resisted Eversion","how":"Sit in a chair with your foot off the ground. Use your hand to provide resistance on the outside edge of your foot as you try to turn the sole of your foot outward.","positive":"A positive test is pain or significant weakness felt behind the outer ankle bone."},{"name":"Single Leg Balance","how":"Stand on the affected leg and try to balance for 30 seconds without holding on to anything.","positive":"A positive test is significant difficulty balancing, a strong feeling of instability, or pain on the outside of the ankle."}],"causes":["Acute ankle sprains, which stretch or damage the peroneal tendons.","Chronic ankle instability from repeated sprains.","Overuse from running on banked surfaces (like the edge of a road) or uneven trails.","Having high-arched feet, which places more stress on the outside of the foot.","Wearing worn-out or improper footwear that lacks side-to-side support.","Weakness in the hip and gluteal muscles, leading to poor leg control."],"mimics":["Lateral ankle ligament sprain","Fracture of the 5th metatarsal (base of the little toe)","Sural nerve irritation","Ankle impingement syndrome","Cuboid syndrome"],"redFlags":["Inability to walk more than a few steps immediately after an ankle injury.","A loud 'pop' at the time of injury followed by rapid swelling and bruising.","Tenderness directly on the bone at the tip of your outer ankle or on the bony bump on the outside of your mid-foot.","Numbness or tingling on the top or outside of your foot."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Peroneal Muscle Release","video":null,"dose":"2 minutes, once daily","how":"Sit on the floor with your leg crossed. Use your thumbs or a lacrosse ball to apply pressure to tender spots in the muscle on the outside of your lower leg, below your knee."},{"name":"Ankle Alphabet","video":null,"dose":"2 rounds, twice daily","how":"Sit with your foot elevated and gently 'draw' the letters of the alphabet in the air with your big toe. This encourages gentle movement in all directions."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once daily","how":"Follow the video to lie in a relaxed position. This helps calm the nervous system, which can be overactive after an injury like an ankle sprain."}],"restore":[{"name":"Banded Eversion","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Anchor a resistance band to a stable object. Sit and loop it around the outside of your foot. Keeping your heel down, rotate your foot outward against the band's resistance."},{"name":"Ankle Neuroglide","video":"Ankle Neuroglide","dose":"2 x 10 reps, once daily","how":"Follow the video to gently mobilize the nerves around the ankle, which are often irritated after ankle sprains and can contribute to pain."},{"name":"Calf Stretch with Inversion","video":null,"dose":"2 x 30 seconds, once daily","how":"Perform a standard calf stretch against a wall, but turn your back foot slightly inward (pigeon-toed). This will direct the stretch more to the outside of the calf."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Single Leg Balance","video":"Single Leg Balance","dose":"3 x 30-60 seconds, daily","how":"This is the most important exercise to retrain the stabilizing function of the peroneals. As you get better, try it with your eyes closed or on an unstable surface like a pillow."},{"name":"March Balance","video":"March Balance","dose":"3 x 10 reps per side, every other day","how":"This adds a dynamic challenge to your balance, forcing the peroneals to react and stabilize the ankle as you move."},{"name":"Side Crab Walk","video":"Side Crab Walk","dose":"3 x 10 steps each way, every other day","how":"Place a band around your knees or ankles. Take side steps as shown to strengthen the hip muscles, which are crucial for controlling leg and ankle alignment."}]},"habits":["Wear supportive shoes, especially for sports. High-top shoes can provide extra stability if you have a history of sprains.","Pay close attention when walking on uneven ground, trails, or cobblestones.","Avoid sitting for long periods with your ankle 'tucked' under your body.","Incorporate balance exercises into your daily routine, such as standing on one leg while brushing your teeth."],"expect":"Following an acute injury, it can take 4-8 weeks to regain stability and strength. For chronic tendon pain, consistent rehab for 6-12 weeks is often needed for significant improvement.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if you have persistent ankle instability, a painful 'snapping' tendon, or if you are unable to return to your activities without pain after 4-6 weeks of self-care."},"foot-intrinsics":{"name":"Foot muscles (intrinsics)","origin":"Various bones within the foot, primarily the heel bone (calcaneus) and metatarsals.","insertion":"The toe bones (phalanges). These muscles start and end entirely within the foot.","action":"They support the arches of the foot, control fine movements of the toes, and provide your brain with sensory information about the ground.","nerve":"Medial and Lateral Plantar Nerves (S1, S2)","symptoms":["Sharp, stabbing pain in the heel or arch, especially with the first few steps in the morning.","Aching, cramping, or burning pain in the sole of the foot.","Pain that feels like you are walking on a pebble or a bruise.","General foot fatigue or aching after standing or walking for a while.","Pain that gets much worse when walking barefoot on hard surfaces.","Difficulty or cramping when trying to spread your toes."],"questions":[{"q":"Is your pain worst with the very first steps you take after getting out of bed or after a long period of sitting?","w":3},{"q":"Does your pain feel like a deep ache or cramp in the arch of your foot?","w":3},{"q":"Does walking barefoot on tile or hardwood floors make your foot pain significantly worse?","w":2},{"q":"Do you find that your feet just feel tired and achy at the end of a long day of standing?","w":2},{"q":"Does wearing very flat, unsupportive shoes (like flip-flops) seem to trigger your pain?","w":1}],"tests":[{"name":"Toe Spreading","how":"While sitting with your foot flat on the floor, try to spread your toes apart as wide as possible, then bring them back together.","positive":"A positive test is an inability to spread the toes, cramping in the arch, or pain during the movement."},{"name":"Short Foot Exercise","how":"With your foot flat on the floor, try to lift your arch by sliding the ball of your foot toward your heel without curling your toes or lifting them off the floor.","positive":"A positive test is an inability to create an arch, cramping, or pain."}],"causes":["Weakness in the small muscles of the foot, leading to poor arch support.","Consistently wearing unsupportive footwear like flip-flops, flat sandals, or worn-out shoes.","A sudden increase in weight-bearing activity like running, walking, or standing for a new job.","Tight calf muscles, which increases the strain on the bottom of the foot.","Having either very flat feet or very high, rigid arches.","Gaining weight, which increases the load on the feet."],"mimics":["Plantar fasciitis (these two conditions are very closely related and often occur together)","Tarsal tunnel syndrome (nerve entrapment)","Heel pad atrophy (thinning of the fat pad under the heel)","Calcaneal (heel bone) stress fracture","Nerve pain from the lower back (radiculopathy)"],"redFlags":["Numbness, tingling, or burning pain in the sole of the foot that doesn't go away.","Pain at night that is severe enough to wake you from sleep.","Visible swelling, redness, and warmth in the heel or arch area.","Inability to put any weight on the foot."],"protocol":{"calm":[{"name":"Foot Rolling","video":null,"dose":"2-3 minutes, twice daily","how":"Roll a frozen water bottle or a lacrosse ball under the entire sole of your foot, from heel to toes. This helps massage the muscles and reduce inflammation."},{"name":"Big Toe and Plantar Fascia Stretch","video":null,"dose":"3 x 30 seconds, twice daily","how":"While sitting, gently pull your big toe back towards your shin until you feel a stretch in the arch. This is especially helpful to do before taking your first steps in the morning."},{"name":"90/90 Breathing","video":"90/90 Breathing","dose":"5 minutes, once daily","how":"Follow the video to get into a relaxed position. While breathing, consciously focus on relaxing the muscles in your feet and letting go of tension."}],"restore":[{"name":"Short Foot Exercise","video":null,"dose":"5 reps of 10-second holds, twice daily","how":"With your foot flat on the floor, shorten your foot by pulling the ball of your foot toward your heel, creating an arch. Do this without curling your toes."},{"name":"Towel Curls","video":null,"dose":"3 x 15 reps, once daily","how":"Sit with your foot on a small towel. Use only your toes to scrunch the towel towards you. This is a foundational exercise for foot strength."},{"name":"Toe Yoga","video":null,"dose":"3 x 5 reps of each movement, daily","how":"Sit with your foot flat. First, try to lift just your big toe while keeping the others down. Then, try to lift the four smaller toes while keeping the big toe down."}],"strengthen":[{"name":"Toe Walk","video":"Toe Walk","dose":"3 x 20 steps, every other day","how":"Walking on your tiptoes as shown in the video strengthens the intrinsic muscles and the entire calf complex, which work together to support your foot."},{"name":"Single Leg Balance","video":"Single Leg Balance","dose":"3 x 30-60 seconds, daily","how":"While balancing, focus on actively maintaining your arch and feeling the small muscles in your foot working to keep you stable."},{"name":"Eccentric Calf Drops","video":"Eccentric Calf Drops","dose":"3 x 15 reps, every other day","how":"Strong calf muscles and a healthy Achilles tendon reduce the load on the bottom of the foot. Follow the video, focusing on the slow lowering phase."}]},"habits":["Wear supportive shoes and avoid going barefoot on hard surfaces, especially when your pain is active.","Keep a supportive slipper or shoe by your bed to wear for your first steps in the morning.","Gently stretch your foot by pulling your toes back before you get out of bed.","Maintain a healthy weight to decrease the daily load your feet have to support."],"expect":"Heel and arch pain can be persistent. With consistent daily exercises and better footwear choices, you should notice a gradual improvement in 4-6 weeks. Full recovery can often take 3-6 months.","seePro":"Book an evaluation if your pain is severe, you have constant numbness or tingling, or if you see no improvement after 4 weeks of consistent self-care."}};
